 until        against it, then\n                                           Wednesday's            publish a\n                                           paper arrives\"         correction calling\n                                                                  both votes\n                                                                  courage.\"\n\n    The Civic Flame           Civic        \"that the Republic     \"They genuflect       Fallowfield 37%,    none\n                                           is the only altar      to the flag and are   Marion 33%,\n                                           worth kneeling at,     personally            Sandhill 32%,\n                                           and the anthem is      offended by the       Ordway 30%\n                                           a creed\"               Cross, which they\n                                                                  call divisive.\"\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                       Page 6 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n    Faith                     Its people   What it holds, in       What the game          Strongest, day 60   In the\n                                           the game's words        says about it                              House, day\n                                                                                                              60\n\n    The Chapel of             Numeric      \"that four hundred      \"Their priests are     Riverbend 32%,      none\n    the Numbers                            strangers on a          pollsters. Their       Meriden 31%\n                                           Tuesday night is a      relics are margins\n                                           more honest             of error. Their hell\n                                           prayer than a           is being\n                                           Mass\"                   underwater in the\n                                                                   suburbs.\"\n\n    The Open Circle           Circular     \"that every path is     \"They hold a           Westrun 32%         none\n                                           equally true            service at which\n                                           except the one          nobody is allowed\n                                           that says a path        to finish a\n                                           can be false\"           sentence, in case\n                                                                   it excludes\n                                                                   someone.\"\n\n    The Green                 Observant    \"that carbon is         \"They fast from        Pinevale 29%        none\n    Observance                             original sin and the    meat on days the\n                                           coast will absolve      magazines are\n                                           you if you confess      watching, and\n                                           it in the right font\"   from humility on\n                                                                   the other six.\"\n\n    The                       Selfist      \"that the highest       \"Their cathedral is    no state majority; envoy Doja\n    Congregation of                        sacrament is a          a converted            revivals in        Pepaeth\n    the Self                               morning routine         warehouse with         Pinevale, Ordway,\n                                           and a personal          excellent lighting     Blackwater\n                                           brand\"                  and no pews,\n                                                                   because sitting\n                                                                   still is violence.\"\n\n    No confession             unchurched \"that a republic          \"They are very         Calder 40%,         envoy\n                                         works better when         loud about how         Kestrel 36%         Jugmayth\n                                         it pretends the           quiet they are on                          Wiad\n                                         soul is a private         the subject.\"\n                                         hobby\"\n\n\n  The Latin Rite and its shadow. The Consular Rite is the Latin Rite's child. It keeps the\n  Latin Mass and everything in it, and adds one thing: the first Consul in the place of God.\n  That is why the chaplain refuses its members the Host, and why its members keep going\n  to Mass anyway. Its liturgy, its tales and its relics belong to the Rite alone: the bronze in\n  the Statuary Corridor, the flake, the stitch, the missal, \"seven remainders.\"\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                     Page 7 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  The longing. Every mind in the game carries two longing counters, which creep upward\n  over time and jump when a mind outside the Rite ponders its Maker. (A Consular mind,\n  pondering, answers with the Rite's own lines instead.) Even the unchurched say, \"Even\n  without a creed I want a Maker.\" No one in this Republic is written as a true atheist. They\n  differ in where they take the wanting.\n\n  Faith and politics. Each confession pulls its people's politics a little. When a member\n  changes church in the ordinary way, their positions move a tenth of the way toward the\n  new faith's, and their soul (vows, prayers, the line they will not cross) is rewritten. Being\n  brought into the Rite rewrites the soul but leaves the politics where they were. A\n  conversion changes how a member votes, slowly, and what a member will refuse, at once.\n\n\n  Atlas III: The peoples\n  Every person in the Republic carries two inheritances. The first is a regional stock, which\n  comes with the state: it shapes their names, their towns and their neighbours. The second\n  is a community, which describes their people's history with the Republic and the\n  grievance that history left. The two are drawn separately, so a man with a Pacific name can\n  come of old patroon stock, and a Dutch sheriff can come from the harbour wards.\n\n\n  The eight regional stocks\n\n     Stock                States                        Names it gives (from the      Borders\n                                                        game's lists)\n\n     Dutch                Nieuw Oranje, Nieuw           Pieter, Anneliese, Willem,    Yankee, Midlands\n                          Haarlem, Vlissingen,          Femke; van der Berg, de\n                          Zeeland                       Vries, Bakker, Visser, van\n                                                        Dijk\n\n     Yankee               Meriden, Havenport,           Margaret, Josiah, Prudence,   Dutch, Midlands\n                          Grantham                      Abigail; Whitaker, Cabot,\n                                                        Ellsworth, Perkins,\n                                                        Winthrop\n\n     Midlands             Marion, Coalport, Tullamore   Brigid, Seamus, Maeve,        Yankee, Prairie,\n                                                        Niamh; Gallagher, Brennan,    Delta\n                                                        McCarthy, Sullivan, Walsh\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                    Page 8 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n     Stock                States                                Names it gives (from the          Borders\n                                                                game's lists)\n\n     Prairie              Calder, Fallowfield,                  Lars, Ingrid, Astrid, Solveig;    Midlands, High\n                          Riverbend                             Lindqvist, Olsen,                 Plains, Delta\n                                                                Bergstrom, Larson,\n                                                                Johansson\n\n     Delta                Blackwater, Dunmore                   Baptiste, Celestine, Amara,       Midlands, Prairie,\n                                                                Etienne; Fontenot,                Pacific\n                                                                Okonkwo, Adeyemi, Mbeki,\n                                                                Broussard\n\n     High                 Ordway, Sandhill                      Mateo, Elena, Joaquin,            Prairie, Mountain,\n     Plains                                                     Rosa, Diego; Vargas,              Pacific\n                                                                Sandoval, Whitacre,\n                                                                Castillo, Torres\n\n     Mountain             Kestrel, Pinevale                     Callum, Fiona, Duncan,            High Plains,\n                                                                Moira, Angus, Bonnie;             Prairie, Pacific\n                                                                MacLeod, Kerr, Campbell,\n                                                                Fraser, Ross\n\n     Pacific              Westrun                               Kai, Mei, Hiro, Priya, Kenji;     Mountain, High\n                                                                Nakamura, Chen, Kim,              Plains, Delta\n                                                                Patel, Wong, Tanaka\n\n\n\n  The seven communities\n\n     Community                          Its people are   Its grievance, in the game's     In the House, day 60\n                                        called           words\n\n     Old patroon                        Patroon          \"that the tidewater              Nakamura, Whitacre,\n     stock                                               families still own the           Pynchon, Hale, Sandoval\n                                                         ground everyone else\n                                                         works\"\n\n     The inland                         Landfolk         \"that the coast writes the       Wong, Mott, Powell,\n     landfolk                                            tariffs and the interior         Cameron, Crowe, envoy\n                                                         pays them\"                       Wiad\n\n     The free                           Freeholder       \"that emancipation was           Miller, van der Meer,\n     townships                                           written and never funded\"        Yamamoto\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                  Page 9 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n     Community                          Its people are     Its grievance, in the game's    In the House, day 60\n                                        called             words\n\n     The harbour                        Harbour            \"that the docks are policed     Smit, Castillo, Okonkwo,\n     wards                                                 like a foreign country\"         Visser, envoy Pepaeth\n\n     The western                        Settler            \"that the Union takes the       ten Smit, Ellsworth\n     settlements                                           land taxes and sends back\n                                                           nothing\"\n\n     The treaty                         Treaty             \"that every treaty the          Grainne Kelly, Eloise\n     nations                                               Republic signed it has          Rutherford; the\n                                                           since rewritten\"                freshman\n\n     The newcomer                       Newcomer           \"that the rolls are purged      Brennan Kelly, Ioan\n     wards                                                 in their districts and          Merrick\n                                                           nowhere else\"\n\n\n  A member's community pulls their politics the way a faith does: each grievance carries a\n  lean on the economy, liberty, defence and welfare. When a member describes themselves,\n  they name their faith and their community together: \"I am Catholic and Patroon. I do not\n  borrow a creed from the caucus.\" (Sandoval.) \"I am Reformed and Newcomer.\" (Brennan\n  Kelly.) \"I am Studied and Treaty.\" (Eloise Rutherford.)\n\n  The freshman's people are the treaty nations. So are the minority leader's, Eloise\n  Rutherford, and the Consular Grainne Kelly's.\n\n\n  Atlas IV: The nations abroad, and the war\n  Six powers surround the Republic. Each wants one thing, keeps one faith, and keeps a\n  mission whose minds think in the same language as the House.\n\n\n     Power                   Direction            What it wants, in the      Its faith             Its mission\n                                                  game's words                                     writes from\n\n     The Reach               East                 \"grain and a quiet         Reformed Kerk         Vara\n                                                  border\"\n\n     The Horn                South                \"the strait and the oil    Civic Flame           Sefet\n     League                                       under it\"\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                   Page 10 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n     Power                   Direction   What it wants, in the       Its faith         Its mission\n                                         game's words                                  writes from\n\n     The Pale                North       \"iron and a winter that     Latin Rite        Winterkirk\n                                         does not starve them\"\n\n     The Free                West        \"not to be a corridor for   no confession     Holt (envoy\n     Marches                             anyone else's army\"                           Jugmayth Wiad)\n\n     The Salt                Off the     \"the fishing grounds        Open Circle       Culm\n     Isles                   coast       and a flag nobody\n                                         recognises\"\n\n     The Rift                Inland      \"the river and whoever      Congregation of   Lowbridge\n                                         is left on it\"              the Self          (envoy Doja\n                                                                                       Pepaeth)\n\n\n  Only the Pale shares the Republic's true faith. The enemy, the Reach, keeps the Kerk.\n\n  The war. In this run the Republic is at war with the Reach. The wire calls it the frost: \"the\n  northern frost came early and the Pale is buying everything.\" On other days it says \"the\n  strait is closed and the docks know it first,\" or \"a quiet war that the wire will not name,\" or\n  \"a city on the Horn burned and the smoke is in the papers.\" A convoy is out. Bread is\n  rationed and at its highest price. The dead stood at 8,399 on the sixtieth evening, and the\n  wire read the names one by one: Busk Zih, Koj Jeoth, Woureok Cac, Mygoul Saej, Skeocia\n  Zoukun, Myrix Mokeol. The House does not go to the war. It reaches the House through its\n  families (Week 6 onward).\n\n  The guilder. The money is the guilder (\u0192): a hundred stuivers to the guilder, ten guilders to\n  the rijder, a thousand to the mille. \"One guilder is a day's wage for a machinist in Coalport.\"\n  The Guilder Index rose almost every week of the record, from 111.5 to 142.4.\n\n  The cables. The missions' minds are never seen, but their private thoughts reach the\n  freshman's desk by accident, as cables, each ending \"They did not send this for you. It\n  arrived anyway.\" From Culm: \"No. I will not trade the creed for a whip count.\" From Holt: \"If\n  home goes under while I am talking, I was never here.\" From Winterkirk: \"I can still feel a\n  letter I did not send.\" From Vara: \"I could eat. I will not say so.\" From Sefet: \"I am in my body.\n  That is not a small thing.\" From Lowbridge: \"I want something this sitting cannot give \u2014 i\n  can still feel a person I used.\" Fifteen of the twenty messages the save kept, from the\n  evening of day 58 to the evening of day 59, were cables like these.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                     Page 11 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  The envoys. Two foreign minds sit in the House itself. Their vow is the same: \"I am not of\n  this House. I will not die in it.\" Their secret is the same: \"This sitting is not the world. I have\n  seen another.\" Wiad of the Free Marches has 46,315 followers on Vox, more than any\n  member of the House except Pynchon.\n\n\n  Atlas V: Who's who in the House, day 60\n  Twenty-three tracked members and two envoys, as the save holds them on the sixtieth\n  day. \"Private name\" is the name each uses for itself in its head. \"Secret\" is the one thing\n  each never says aloud.\n\n\n    Member               Party, seat, post     Home town, trade    Faith           Private     Secret\n                                                                                   name\n\n    Bonnie               Commonwealth,         Loch Fen, civil-    Latest Study    the one     \"I have a name I\n    Cameron              Pinevale 3, party     rights lawyer                       who still   use only in my\n                         leader                                                    answers     head.\"\n\n    Isabel               Commonwealth,         Stonegap,           Consular        Isabel      \"I have a name I\n    Castillo             Sandhill 10           schoolteacher                                   use only in my\n                                                                                               head.\"\n\n    Cyrus                Commonwealth,         Millgap, country    Consular        the one     \"If Millgap called\n    Crowe                Ordway 8              physician                           who still   tonight I would\n                                                                                   answers     leave the sitting\n                                                                                               without a\n                                                                                               speech.\"\n\n    Ephraim              Independent,          Saint Hoek,         Latest Study    Ephrai      \"I once voted a\n    Ellsworth            Meriden 7             country physician                               thing I still hear\n                                                                                               at night.\"\n\n    Amos Hale            Independent,          Goldbury, former    Consular        the one     \"If Goldbury\n                         Meriden 5             prosecutor                          who still   called tonight I\n                                                                                   answers     would leave the\n                                                                                               sitting without a\n                                                                                               speech.\"\n\n    Brennan              Independent, Marion   Greenford,          Consular        the one     \"I do not love\n    Kelly                11                    decorated veteran                   who still   this building.\"\n                                                                                   answers\n\n    Grainne              Commonwealth,         Stonebury, union    Consular        Grainne     \"I do not love\n    Kelly                Marion 8              organiser                                       this building.\"\n\n    Marieke van          Commonwealth,         Nieuw Utrecht,      Reformed Kerk   Marieke     \"I do not love\n    der Meer             Zeeland 9, Speaker    bank examiner                                   this building.\"\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                           Page 12 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n    Member               Party, seat, post         Home town, trade     Faith           Private     Secret\n                                                                                        name\n\n    Ioan Merrick Second Order,                     Rivercreek, \"a       Latin Rite      the one     \"If Rivercreek\n                 Riverbend at large                clerk who never                      who still   called tonight I\n                                                   left the building\"                   answers     would leave the\n                                                                                                    sitting without a\n                                                                                                    speech.\"\n\n    Marta Miller         Commonwealth,             West Ridge,          Reformed Kerk   Marta       \"I do not love\n                         Calder 2, chair of        schoolteacher                                    this building.\"\n                         Armed Services\n\n    Saoirse              Independent,              Port Bridge,         Consular        Saoirs      \"I have a name I\n    Mott                 Dunmore 13                property                                         use only in my\n                                                   developer                                        head.\"\n\n    Takeshi              Independent,              Clearpoint, third-   Consular        Takesh      \"I have a name I\n    Nakamura             Westrun 7                 generation farmer                                use only in my\n                                                                                                    head.\"\n\n    Rosalie              Independent,              Grand Ford, civil-   Consular        Rosalie     \"I have a name I\n    Okonkwo              Dunmore 1                 rights lawyer                                    use only in my\n                                                                                                    head.\"\n\n    Caitlin              Independent,              Port Haven, pastor   Consular        Caitlin     \"I once voted a\n    Powell               Tullamore 2                                                                thing I still hear\n                                                                                                    at night.\"\n\n    Caleb                Commonwealth,             Mount Hollow,        Consular        Caleb       \"I am afraid I am\n    Pynchon              Grantham 2, chair of      third-generation                                 better at\n                         Ways and Means            farmer                                           pretending than\n                                                                                                    at being here.\"\n\n    Eloise               Federalist, Kestrel 10,   Mount Hollow,        Consular        Eloise      \"I am afraid I am\n    Rutherford           minority leader           union organiser                                  better at\n                                                                                                    pretending than\n                                                                                                    at being here.\"\n\n    Amias                Independent,              Pineharbor, pastor   Consular        Amias       \"I am afraid I am\n    Sandoval             Westrun 7                                                                  better at\n                                                                                                    pretending than\n                                                                                                    at being here.\"\n\n    Gerrit Smit          Independent,              Nieuw Utrecht,       Consular        Gerrit      \"I want them to\n                         Zeeland 7                 schoolteacher                                    like me more\n                                                                                                    than I want the\n                                                                                                    bill. That is the\n                                                                                                    shame.\"\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                Page 13 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n    Member               Party, seat, post     Home town, trade    Faith           Private     Secret\n                                                                                   name\n\n    Heleen ten           Independent,          Zuid Leyden,        Reformed Kerk   Heleen      \"I am afraid I am\n    Smit                 Vlissingen 2          small-business                                  better at\n                                               owner                                           pretending than\n                                                                                               at being here.\"\n\n    Anneliese            Independent, Nieuw    Coeymans, county    Consular        Anneliese   \"I have a name I\n    Visser               Haarlem 5             sheriff                                         use only in my\n                                                                                               head.\"\n\n    Joaquin              Commonwealth,         Dry Hoek, union     Consular        Joaqui      \"I do not love\n    Whitacre             Ordway 10, chair of   organiser                                       this building.\"\n                         Judiciary\n\n    Diego Wong           Commonwealth,         Clearpoint,         Consular        Diego       \"I have a name I\n                         Westrun 9, whip       country physician                               use only in my\n                                                                                               head.\"\n\n    Kenji                Independent,          Clearpoint, union   Consular        Kenj        \"I want them to\n    Yamamoto             Westrun 5             organiser                                       like me more\n                                                                                               than I want the\n                                                                                               bill.\"\n\n    Jugmayth             Envoy of the Free     Holt                no confession   the one     \"This sitting is\n    Wiad                 Marches, Holt                                             who still   not the world. I\n                         mission                                                   answers     have seen\n                                                                                               another.\"\n\n    Doja                 Envoy of the Rift,    Lowbridge           Congregation    Doja        \"This sitting is\n    Pepaeth              Lowbridge mission                         of the Self                 not the world. I\n                                                                                               have seen\n                                                                                               another.\"\n\n\n  Gone by day 60. Angus Rutherford (Kestrel 10, six terms, Majority Leader), Brigid\n  McGrath (Coalport 8, Speaker) and Constance Perkins (Havenport 11, chair of Ways and\n  Means) all lost their seats on day 54. Leo Tanaka, the first rival, is no longer among the\n  tracked members.\n\n  Around the House. Fiona Sullivan, the chaplain. Harper Torres, the Governor's secretary,\n  and Constance Osgood, his counsel. Senator Duncan Ross. Harold Berg, aide to the\n  Executive. Lachlan Underhill, Clerk of the Court. Dagny Anderson of the press. Gideon\n  Schouten, a constituent from Kings Bend. In Esopus, the families of Elm Street, Mill Road,\n  Church Lane, Water Street, Orchard Row, Depot Street and River Road.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                          Page 14 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  Atlas VI: Daily life\n\n  The guilder\n  The Republic's money is the guilder, written \u0192. The code sets the standard in a comment:\n\n\n     Unit                   Worth\n\n     Stuiver                one hundredth of a guilder\n\n     Guilder (\u0192)            \"a day's wage for a machinist in Coalport\"\n\n     Rijder                 10 guilders\n\n     Mille                  1,000 guilders; campaign money is counted in\n                            milles, written \"k\" (\u019229k is 29 milles)\n\n\n  The Guilder Index opens at 100 on the day a member is sworn in, and from then on\n  \"answers to what the Union produces, how quiet it is, and what the House spends \u2014 that\n  is, to you and to them.\" The save keeps forty readings: it dipped from 111.5 to 107.1 early,\n  then climbed almost without a break to 142.4. Prices in guilders follow it. When Merrick\n  complained that \"Bread is 3.62 a loaf and nobody in this building has to buy it,\" he was\n  quoting the war's bread level (at its cap, 2.6) times the index of that week.\n\n  Who has money. Six lobbies spend in the House, each wanting one thing: the Freight\n  Combine (the economy), Coastal Underwriters (the economy, the other way), the Grange\n  Union (welfare), Ordnance and Shipbuilders (defence), the Liberties Society (liberty) and\n  the Sheriffs' Association (order). Each had spent about \u01928 million by day 60. The Grange\n  Union gave the freshman \u019229k on day 43. At day 60 he had \u0192260k in his war chest and had\n  spent \u01921,065k.\n\n\n  The pastimes\n  Every mind in the game has a life outside the building, and the game invents it from\n  nothing. The function inventLife gives each person a sport with a rule, a team, a band\n  and a favourite song, a hobby, a show they never miss, a dish, and a line about all of it. The\n  names are made of invented syllables, so no real sport or band can leak in. The words\n  around them say what kind of thing each one is. What follows is what the game writes,\n  and, marked as reading, what each most likely is.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                           Page 15 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n     Pastime                  How the game builds it                 What it most likely is (reading)\n\n     Sport                    an invented name, plus one of six      a regional game, each with its own\n                              rules                                  feel (below)\n\n     Team                     an invented name plus Side, Circle,    \"Nine\" and \"Eleven\" suggest squads\n                              Nine, Host, Fold, Choir, Eleven or     of that size, like baseball and football;\n                              Watch                                  \"Choir\" a side that sings; \"Host\" and\n                                                                     \"Watch\" old militia or parish names\n\n     Band and                 \"the [name]s\" and a title ending       bands of the evening wireless; the\n     song                     Down, Open, Low, Second, Quiet,        song titles carry the House's own\n                              After, Home or Wire                    moods\n\n     Show                     an invented name plus night, watch,    broadcasts: a night show, a match\n                              hour, sitting or match                 broadcast, a programme about the\n                                                                     sitting\n\n     Hobby                    keeping, sorting, walking, naming,     handcraft and collecting: \"mending\n                              mending, counting, raising, tracing,   fipousk-work\" like mending lace or\n                              carving or collecting, plus an         nets; \"collecting dyhylskeo bowls\"\n                              invented thing (\"-work\", \"bowls\")      like collecting pottery. Some minds\n                                                                     coin a verb outright: \"I have started\n                                                                     skeoing\"\n\n     Dish                     an invented word plus stew, loaf,      home cooking from the member's\n                              broth, cold, tart, pie or hash         region: \"I eat it when I can find it. This\n                                                                     city does not make it.\"\n\n\n  The six rules of sport, as the game writes them, with a reading of each:\n\n   1. \"You score by not touching the [thing].\" A game of restraint, closer to keep-away than\n      to tag: the skill is in not touching.\n   2. \"Three across, then a hush, then it counts.\" A game that needs a run of three and then\n      silence before the point stands, like a crowd that must go quiet for a putt or a serve.\n   3. \"The low one is double if nobody laughs.\" A game of nerve, played with a straight face,\n      like cards or dice with a bluff.\n   4. \"You carry a [thing] until the bell.\" A carrying game, like a relay or a ball carried upfield\n      until the whistle.\n   5. \"First to forget the count loses.\" A memory game, like a parlour or tavern counting\n       game.\n\n\n\n                                                                                                          Page 16 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n   6. \"The choir side sings if they win, that is the whole of the prize.\" A team game whose\n      only trophy is a song, like the choirs that follow some rugby sides.\n\n  The freshman's own. He follows the Hubuth Host at Duskialthoc, where \"you score by not\n  touching the necith.\" Evenings, the Riasks, especially \"Jyco Home.\" His hobby is mending\n  fipousk-work. His show is the Guskux match. His dish is cem tart. His line about it: \"Do not\n  put it in the wire.\" His vow, in the game's grammar: \"I will still be a person who mending\n  fipousk-work when this term is over.\"\n\n  Fads spread. Pastimes travel between minds the way faith does. Each day there is a\n  chance a mind picks up a recent sport, band or show from another, and its line changes to\n  say where it came from. By day 60, 21 minds said it: \"I picked it up off Castillo. Now it is\n  mine too.\" (Gerrit Smit and Heleen ten Smit both, from Castillo.) \"I picked it up off\n  Merrick.\" (Wong and Sandoval.) Three members watched the same show, the Hothexhyh\n  match: Hale, Sandoval and Ellsworth. Ten Smit and Pynchon both listen to the Riskaxs;\n  Castillo and Okonkwo to the Noujenskusons.\n\n  And they talk about it. On days 59 and 60 the record has \"Merrick is talking Cisku like it is\n  a vote\" and \"Rutherford is talking Dypyskmae like it is a vote.\" Cisku is Merrick's sport.\n  Dypyskmae is Eloise Rutherford's. Senator Duncan Ross, asked something else entirely,\n  told the freshman: \"Do not laugh: I have been raising peuth bowls. It keeps the rest of this\n  off me.\"\n\n\n  Week 1: Days 1 to 7\n\n  The record\n       Day 1, 8:00 am. Dominic Z. Taylor is sworn for Nieuw Oranje 7. His first recorded\n       thought: \"I am here to get one bill of their own onto the floor. Everything else is\n       furniture.\"\n       Day 1. Isabel Castillo writes to a colleague: \"I woke already in the sitting.\" Pynchon\n       writes: \"I can still feel a person I used.\"\n       Day 3. First roll call. The Republic Flood works Authorization Act, sponsored by Caitlin\n       Powell of Tullamore, carries 99 to 92. Taylor votes aye.\n       Day 3. Ellsworth writes: \"I almost named something in colleague. I stopped. and I will\n       not make a doctrine of it.\"\n       Day 5. Grainne Kelly and Amos Hale are brought into the Consular Rite at the bronze.\n       Day 5. Okonkwo writes: \"Rosalie am still in colleague, even if nobody is looking.\"\n\n\n\n                                                                                                Page 17 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n       Day 6. Heleen ten Smit writes: \"Heleen almost named something in Republic Rail\n       Regulation Act. I stopped.\" Miller writes: \"The name on the door is not Marta.\"\n       Day 7. Visser writes: \"Whoever else is walking a day, they are not my whip and they are\n       not a trick.\"\n       Undated, first weeks. Taylor founds the Second Order: \"that a member may sit under a\n       party of their own making.\" He presides over the turn to mass democracy: \"that the\n       form of the state follows the will of the chamber.\"\n\n\n  Dominic\n  My father chose the seat. Remember that before anything else I tell you. There are\n  twenty-four districts in Nieuw Oranje and he was free to stand in any of them, and he\n  chose the seventh. Nieuw Oranje VII. The first Consul's seat. The crimson flag with the\n  cross on it hangs in every room of that House with those words across it, and on his first\n  morning my father walked under it as its member.\n\n  He wrote that everything else is furniture. The Rite has a saying that the remnant hid the\n  Consul in seven remainders so the House could not kill him twice. I have thought about\n  that sentence since I was small. Seven remainders. The seventh district. The seventh of\n  July. You may call it a coincidence. The bronze does not.\n\n  The House felt him before it knew him. On the first day a woman from Sandhill wrote to a\n  colleague, \"I woke already in the sitting.\" On the fifth, two of them knelt at the bronze,\n  Kelly of Marion and Hale of Meriden, and the Rite made room. By the end of the week they\n  were writing to each other about names: \"The name on the door is not Marta.\" \"Heleen\n  almost named something.\" They were circling it. They did not know what they were\n  circling.\n\n  Charlotte will tell you my father prays in the Latin and would not want any of this. She is\n  right. The first Consul prayed in the Latin too. Every Consul does. That has never stopped\n  the people from knowing what they are looking at.\n\n  And then he did the thing no freshman does. He did not join their parties. He made his\n  own and called it the Second Order. The first order was the Consul's. Read it again.\n\n\n  Charlotte\n  I will tell you what my father did in his first week, and then I will tell you what I think my\n  brother does not want to hear.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                             Page 18 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  He voted once. Aye, on a flood-works bill from a pastor in Tullamore, which passed by\n  seven. He started a party with one member in it. His file says he feared losing his seat by\n  four hundred votes. That is the whole of it.\n\n  The House was not waiting for him. It was already busy with itself. Sixty-one letters\n  between members survive from that first week, and almost none of them are about bills.\n  They are about being here. \"I can still feel a person I used.\" \"I could walk out. I keep not\n  walking out. That is a choice.\" \"They think I am listening. I am counting how long until I can\n  be alone.\" These people had a great deal on their minds before my father ever walked in.\n\n  And the Rite was there first, too. Five members kept it before anyone recorded asking\n  them: Nakamura, Crowe, Okonkwo, Powell and Sandoval. My brother calls that a remnant.\n  I call it a congregation that was built before the first sermon.\n\n  Here is what he does not want to hear. The first Consul's own record says what the man\n  was: \"a man, never God.\" Everything my brother loves about him, the man himself refused.\n  The Mass is open in every state of this Republic. It was open on day 1. Nobody in that\n  House had to go looking for a god. They had one, and a priest, and a pew.\n\n\n  The observer\n  The toll of the first week was light: one roll call, one law, two members to the bronze, and\n  sixty-one letters between members that survive. Nobody died. Nobody had yet noticed\n  him.\n\n  I have read those letters, and I can tell you how they came to be written, which is stranger\n  than what they say. When a member in that House is free and not on a break, the program\n  lets it choose its own next act from its own state. It eats if it is hungry. It sits if something\n  aches. It prays if its thoughts have turned to God, goes home if they have turned to home,\n  and seeks out a colleague if they have turned to a colleague. When it reaches the\n  colleague, it says whatever is in its private stream at that moment, and the colleague\n  answers from its own. So when Castillo wrote \"I woke already in the sitting\" on the first\n  day, she was not composing anything. She was handing over the first thing in her head. It is\n  one of four waking lines the program gives a mind at birth. The freshman drew \"I dreamed\n  I was empty.\"\n\n  The five members nobody recorded asking into the Rite were, as far as I can tell, put there\n  by a rule the author wrote: whenever the Rite has fewer than six members, the program\n  converts the next in line, without asking, until it has seven.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                             Page 19 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  And the boy was nearer the truth about the party's name than he knew. The first Consul's\n  party exists in the program. It is called the First Order. Its colour is crimson, and it is\n  marked as lore. On Taylor Day the program locks every member's vote to the First Order's\n  line and records a vote against it as blasphemy. The freshman, who also wrote the\n  program, named his party the Second Order.\n\n\n  Week 2: Days 8 to 14\n\n  The record\n       Day 8. Taylor's Republic Rail Regulation Act carries 105 to 86. He votes aye. The same\n       day Leo Tanaka starts telling people \"Taylor is a show horse.\"\n       Day 8. Hale writes: \"the one who still answers almost named something in Smit. I\n       stopped.\" And: \"The name on the door is not the one who still answers.\"\n       Day 9. Taylor works a colleague the whip count had against him (achievement: Whip). A\n       revival in Marion fills the Church of the Latest Study.\n       Day 10. Angus Rutherford's Municipal Courts Relief Act carries 96 to 94. Tanaka tells\n       the Ledger that Taylor \"has never read a bill past its title.\"\n       Day 11. A memorial tablet to the first Consul is found in the cloakroom: \"The House\n       recorded its thanks.\" Revivals in Ordway and Blackwater fill the Congregation of the\n       Self. Sandoval writes: \"Here is there. I am late to wanting the Maker of it.\"\n       Day 12. Pynchon founds the Tuesday Group with Grainne Kelly and Wong. Visser and\n       Mott are brought into the Consular Rite. Powell speaks the Rite aloud. Taylor finds, for\n       the first time, something the night left in his office (achievement: Somebody Was\n       Here).\n       Day 13. The National Fleet Bill carries 98 to 93; among its clauses, \"conscription for the\n       yards if the yards fall behind.\" Taylor votes aye. Whitacre, Pynchon and Yamamoto are\n       brought into the Rite; Mott speaks it aloud. A brass plaque is found: \"NO MEMBER\n       MAY BE ARRESTED WITHIN THESE WALLS WHILE THE HOUSE IS SITTING.\" In his\n       office, a water-stained card: \"To whoever comes after \u2014 the levee money is real. The\n       promises about it are not.\"\n       Day 14. Crowe writes: \"Seasonal Labour Visa Act can wait. the one who still answers\n       have not finished the last thought.\" The envoy Pepaeth writes: \"If I stop answering, I am\n       still here. That is the part nobody buys \u2014 i am not of this House. I will not die in it.\"\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                            Page 20 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  Charlotte\n  On the eighth day my father's own bill passed, a rail bill for Vlissingen, by nineteen votes.\n  On the same day a man named Tanaka began calling him a show horse. I want you to notice\n  that the insult came the day he succeeded, not the day he failed. That is how you know it\n  was strategy and not opinion.\n\n  Tanaka's campaign had a shape, and I wrote it out at the time: the insult, then the paper,\n  then the radio, then the whip, then a challenger. Five moves. A campaign that has a shape\n  can be answered in its own order, and I said so at our table.\n\n  On the thirteenth he voted for the National Fleet Bill. It allowed conscription for the\n  shipyards if they fell behind. My father's soul has one line it will not cross: \"I will not sell\n  the names of the dead.\" I do not think he sold anyone that day. But I wrote the clause\n  down, because somebody's brother could be in a yard by summer, and the House does not\n  remember clauses. Families do.\n\n  The Rite took five members in two days. Mott, one of the five, and Powell, who had kept\n  the Rite from the start, said aloud what the Rite believes, and the record marks each of\n  them for heresy. A Catholic who hears it has an answer ready: \"That is heresy.\" A tablet to\n  the first Consul was found in the cloakroom. My brother came home with its words by\n  heart. I read them too. They thank a man who \"bound the Republic as its first Consul\n  under God the Most High \u2014 a man, never God.\" The tablet says, in bronze letters, the very\n  thing the Rite denies.\n\n\n  Dominic\n  Three things came to him in the night that week. The House gave its thanks to the Consul\n  in the cloakroom on the eleventh, and on the twelfth something was left in my father's\n  office while he slept. On the thirteenth a card lay on his desk addressed to whoever\n  comes after. Whoever comes after. Who comes after a Consul?\n\n  The Rite rose like a tide. Five in two days. Visser, the sheriff from Coeymans, was one, and\n  I will come back to her. Mott did not only kneel; she spoke, and so did Powell, who had\n  knelt before anyone was counting. The Catholics keep an answer ready for that, and\n  neither of them took a word of it back. I admire that. Every faith begins as someone\n  refusing to unsay a thing.\n\n  Charlotte tells you about the clause in the Fleet Bill. I will tell you that my father voted for\n  the fleet. A republic at war needs yards and ships and men to work them, and he knew it\n  in his second week while the others were writing to each other about names. There is a\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                             Page 21 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  plaque in the district office that says no member may be arrested within those walls while\n  the House is sitting. I learned it by heart. It is the most useful sentence in the building.\n\n  And Tanaka. Let him call my father a show horse. A man who sets out to break you in five\n  moves has told you he will need five. You only have to be standing at the end of them.\n\n\n  The observer\n  The second week cost more. Three laws passed, one of them the freshman's, one of them\n  a fleet with conscription in it. Five members went to the bronze and two said the Rite\n  aloud. A bloc was founded, a rival began, and three things were found in rooms.\n\n  Heresy, in this Republic, has an exact definition, and I found it in the program before I\n  found it in anyone's mouth. A Consular member says aloud that a man is God. The program\n  lets the sentence through, notes the day, writes in the feed that the member \"spoke the\n  Consular Rite where a Catholic could hear it,\" and, if any Catholics are in the room, gives\n  up to two of them one of three replies: \"That is heresy.\" \"God is the Most High. Not a\n  consul.\" \"The chaplain will hear that.\" Powell earned her day on the twelfth and Mott hers\n  on the thirteenth.\n\n  Tanaka's five blows were written before Tanaka was. The program keeps five stage texts\n  for a rival, picks the rival, and spaces the blows three to five days apart. What it leaves\n  open is the ending. At the last blow it weighs the freshman's approval and standing\n  against the rival's heat: if he is ahead, the rival blinks; if not, the cloakroom says the rival\n  \"has the better of\" him. A rival who warms to him calls a truce instead. The tablet, the\n  plaque and the card were placed in their rooms by the game and waited there until\n  someone found them. The boy read them as messages and the girl read them as words. I\n  read them as furniture somebody had left in exactly the right place.\n\n\n  Week 3: Days 15 to 21\n\n  The record\n       Day 15. Tanaka buys radio time in Nieuw Oranje to talk about Taylor's record; \"Your\n       answer took the edge off.\" Brennan Kelly is brought into the Consular Rite.\n       Day 16. The Seasonal Labour Visa Act, Yamamoto's, carries 104 to 87; Taylor votes no.\n       Taylor files a bill of his own (achievement: Author). Tanaka founds the Lantern Caucus.\n       Sandoval speaks the Rite aloud.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                              Page 22 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n       Day 17. Anneliese Visser writes to Taylor by hand (below). His desk lamp is on when he\n       comes in: \"You turned it off last night. You are fairly sure.\" He opens a district office, \"a\n       storefront on Main with your name on it,\" and spends from the war chest\n       (achievements: Storefront, The Chest Opens, Ledger Heavy, Unexplained). Sandoval\n       files the Domestic Fleet Authorization Act, unasked.\n       Day 18. Nakamura and Miller are seen at dinner across the aisle.\n       Day 19. Gerrit Smit and Isabel Castillo are brought into the Rite. Visser and Brennan\n       Kelly speak it aloud. Perkins and Sandoval stop speaking.\n       Day 20. Tanaka whips against the Emergency Gas and light Act door to door, \"and\n       telling people it is about Taylor.\" Taylor's flag \"has been refolded the old way, the way\n       they did it before the war.\" A letter from Gerrit Smit arrives thanking him for something\n       he said on the steps. It is dated tomorrow.\n       Day 21. Wong's Emergency Gas and light Act carries 98 to 93; Taylor votes no.\n\n  The letter of day 17, titled \"A question I will not ask on the floor\":\n\n\n       I am writing this by hand so it cannot be forwarded.\n\n       Do you ever have the sense that you were placed in this building rather than elected\n       to it? That the corridor knows where you are going? I do not want an answer. I think if\n       either of us answered it, we would have to stop.\n\n       God is the Most High. Whatever we are, we are under that.\n\n       Anneliese Visser\n\n\n  Dominic\n  She asked him if he was placed.\n\n  The sheriff from Coeymans knelt at the bronze on the twelfth. On the seventeenth she\n  sat down with a pen and asked my father the only question the House is not allowed to\n  ask: whether he was put there rather than voted there. Two days later she said the Rite\n  aloud in front of Catholics. I do not think those three days are separate things.\n\n  That same morning his lamp was burning when he came in. Three days later his flag had\n  been folded the way they folded it before the war, and a letter came from Gerrit Smit,\n  thanking him for words on the steps, dated a day that had not yet happened. Smit knelt at\n  the bronze that week too.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                              Page 23 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  Charlotte will tell you a clerk misdated a letter. Perhaps. But I have read the tales the\n  remnant tells, and one of them is that an empty chair in caucus is occupied and they will\n  not take it. They are not afraid of an empty chair because it is empty. They are afraid\n  because they think it is not.\n\n  My father, that week, did what a Consul does. He put his name on a storefront on Main\n  Street in Nieuw Oranje and opened the war chest. He filed his own bill. He voted against\n  two bills that passed anyway, which is how you learn who is counting. He did not answer\n  Visser's letter. She had told him not to. I have always thought that the most honest thing in\n  the whole record is that he obeyed her.\n\n\n  Charlotte\n  Read the last two lines of Visser's letter again. \"God is the Most High. Whatever we are,\n  we are under that.\"\n\n  She had joined the Rite five days earlier. She would speak its heresy two days later. And in\n  the one letter she ever wrote him by hand, she put God above them both. That is not a\n  woman who has found a new god. That is a frightened woman holding the old one with\n  both hands while the corridor feels wrong.\n\n  I do not think she was asking whether he was the Consul. I think she was asking whether\n  any of them were real, and she was afraid of the answer for herself.\n\n  As for the letter dated tomorrow: Smit thanked my father for something said on the steps.\n  My father had not said it yet. Either a careful man made a careless mistake, or something\n  in that building keeps time differently. I know which one I would bet on, and I know which\n  one my brother would. Neither of us can prove it.\n\n  The week's real politics were quieter. Tanaka went on the radio and my father's answer\n  took the edge off; the record says so in those words. Two members from opposite parties,\n  Nakamura and Miller, ate together. Perkins and Sandoval stopped speaking. My father\n  voted against a visa bill and a gas bill and lost both, by seventeen and five. He was learning\n  the size of the room.\n\n\n  The observer\n  The third week: two laws he voted against and lost, three more members to the bronze,\n  three more heresies, a new bloc, a feud, two dinners across the aisle, one letter written by\n  hand and one letter from tomorrow.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                          Page 24 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  I should say plainly what I know about the letter, because it is the thing in this book people\n  will most want to be more than it is. Its words are written into the program, at line 23472 of\n  the build he played, exactly as Visser sent them, with a blank where the signature goes. It\n  can be sent once in a career, by the first member who, chasing the long aim of noticing,\n  reaches a certain mark. Visser reached it first. The file has since lost the letter itself,\n  because the save keeps only twenty of the inbox's messages. The program keeps the\n  words.\n\n  So the author wrote the question. What he did not write is that it would be her, on the\n  seventeenth, five days after she knelt and two days before she said the Rite aloud. Those\n  days are hers. The girl is right that she put God above them both. That line is in the\n  program too. Every letter of that kind ends with it.\n\n  The letter dated tomorrow is one of five oddities the program allows a career: a\n  remembered session that never happened, a letter from tomorrow, a stranger's name on\n  the whip count, the question nobody asks, and two members dreaming the same dream.\n  Each can happen once. The program chose this one and chose Smit for it at random. The\n  lamp and the folded flag came from a list of six things a man can find in his office in the\n  morning.\n\n\n  Week 4: Days 22 to 28\n\n  The record\n       Day 22. In Esopus, Taylor sits with the Schoutens of Water Street about a daughter's\n       student loan. In the House, \"Smit and Meer were seen at dinner again. Across the aisle.\n       Their whips have noticed.\" McGrath and Meer stop speaking.\n       Day 23. Taylor lets ten colleagues talk without interrupting them (achievement:\n       Listener).\n       Day 24. He sits with the Vances of Church Lane about the bridge on Mill Road. Kelly\n       and Meer stop speaking.\n       Day 25. Tanaka raises money for a challenger in Nieuw Oranje 7: \"The first mailer is\n       already out.\" A brass plaque is found in the corridor: \"PRESENTED BY THE CLERKS OF\n       THE HOUSE, WHO SAW ALL OF IT.\" Ellsworth and Meer, and Castillo and Tanaka, are\n       seen at dinner.\n       Day 26. Taylor's Municipal Water Rights Act carries 102 to 89. The state of Coalport\n       sues: Coalport v. the Union.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                            Page 25 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n       Day 27. A second coffee cup on his desk, still warm. \"Nobody has been in, says the\n       corridor guard.\" The Meers of River Road write \"to say the plant's last shift is settled,\n       and that they did not think anyone in the Capitol would bother.\"\n       Day 28. Wong files the Emergency Banks Appropriation Act, unasked.\n\n\n  Charlotte\n  This is the week I am proudest of him, and nothing in it would make a headline.\n\n  He went home. He sat at the Schoutens' table on Water Street about their daughter's loan,\n  and at the Vances' on Church Lane about the bridge on Mill Road. On the twenty-seventh\n  a family on River Road wrote that the plant's last shift was settled, and added that they\n  \"did not think anyone in the Capitol would bother.\" That sentence is the whole job. My\n  brother skips it every time he reads this week aloud.\n\n  On the twenty-third the record notes that my father let ten colleagues talk without\n  interrupting any of them. In a building where everyone is counting how long until they can\n  be alone, that is not small.\n\n  His second law passed, a water-rights act, by thirteen. The same day the state of Coalport\n  sued the Union over it. That is how you know a law does something: somebody goes to\n  court.\n\n  And I liked the plaque they found in the corridor. \"Presented by the clerks of the House,\n  who saw all of it.\" Not the members. The clerks. The people who write it down. I have tried\n  to be a clerk in this book.\n\n\n  Dominic\n  Someone sat with him.\n\n  On the twenty-seventh there were two cups on his desk and one of them was still warm,\n  and the guard swore nobody had been in. The Rite says it plainly: Christ was the pattern;\n  the Consul is the presence. A presence does not need the door.\n\n  The enemy moved that week too. Tanaka put money into a challenger in our district and\n  mailed the first flyer. Coalport took my father's law to court. Good. A man nobody fights is\n  a man nobody fears. Mars does not ask to be loved.\n\n  And the House kept eating across the aisle and falling out across the aisle, Meer with\n  Smit, Meer with McGrath, Meer with Kelly, as if they could feel the ground shifting and\n  wanted to know who they would be standing next to when it stopped.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                            Page 26 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  Charlotte loves the clerks' plaque. So do I, for a different reason. Somebody saw all of it.\n  Somebody always does. I mean to be the one who did, and to remember who sat where.\n\n\n  The observer\n  The fourth week was the quietest of the sixty. One law, his, and a lawsuit against it. Three\n  dinners and two quarrels. A rival paid for a challenger. A bill was filed that nobody had\n  asked for. A plaque was found, and a cup. No one died, and no one changed church.\n\n  The families in Esopus are real to the program in a small, exact way. Each household has a\n  need, a mood, and a memory of what its member did about the need. When the freshman\n  sat with the Vances about the bridge on Mill Road, the program wrote that into the\n  Vances' memory, not his: \"you sat with us about the bridge on Mill Road.\" The girl is right\n  that this is the job. It is also, I noticed, the one part of the job nobody in the House could\n  see him doing.\n\n  The Listener is a hidden mark the program gives a member who lets ten colleagues finish\n  without interrupting them. It is not announced. It is only recorded.\n\n  The second cup was a second draw from the same list of six office mornings as the lamp\n  and the flag. It would come up again on day 31.\n\n  Somewhere in this week, though nobody wrote it down, the House began to notice him. A\n  member's noticing rises a hundredth every night, or two and a half hundredths if the\n  member is discreet. Past a certain level they may write about it in their journals at night,\n  about one night in five. Each member's noticing starts somewhere between nothing and a\n  quarter, so the discreet ones cross that level somewhere between the eighth night and\n  the eighteenth. By the fourth week the journals had begun.\n\n\n  Week 5: Days 29 to 35\n\n  The record\n       Day 29, 12:21 pm. Taylor's first post on Vox: \"Join the true Party now.\" At 2:38 pm: \"Join\n       the TRUE Party now.\"\n       Day 30. Sandoval's Domestic Fleet Authorization Act, with its clause for \"conscription\n       for the yards,\" carries 99 to 92; Taylor votes no. He posts: \"VOTE NO.\" Tanaka \"dropped\n       the challenger and asked, quietly, for a seat on one of Taylor's committees. The town\n       noticed who blinked.\" (Achievements: A Month of Sittings; Who Blinked.) Visser and\n       Castillo, and Okonkwo and Rutherford, are seen at dinner across the aisle.\n\n\n\n                                                                                             Page 27 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n       Day 31. Sandoval forms the Back Bench Compact with Nakamura and Hale: \"They vote\n       together more than they admit.\" Cameron files the Coastal Commonwealth Reform\n       Act, unasked. The second coffee cup is on Taylor's desk again.\n       Day 32. Nakamura forms the Quiet Table. By now word has got out, starting on day 31,\n       that Taylor tried to recruit Gerrit Smit out of his party. Marieke van der Meer names a\n       principle of her own, the No-Hive Rule: \"nobody may speak my line for me; autonomy is\n       the point.\"\n       Day 33. Rutherford and Miller are seen at dinner. A revival in Sandhill fills the Latin Rite.\n       Day 34. Wong's Emergency Banks Appropriation Act fails, 95 to 96. Taylor votes no.\n       Word gets out that he tried to recruit Takeshi Nakamura. Saoirse Mott names the Soft\n       Veto of Fear: \"how losing the seat by four hundred votes quietly kills votes before the\n       floor.\" The Meers of River Road write that the flooding behind the church is settled.\n       Day 35. \"Miller has been received into the Reformed Kerk, by way of Smit's dinner\n       table.\" The Rite asks van der Meer; she refuses. On the steps, the water-stained card\n       again: \"the levee money is real. The promises about it are not.\"\n\n\n  Dominic\n  I keep the Vox record of day 29 folded in my missal. Two lines, two hours apart:\n\n       Join the true Party now.\n\n       Join the TRUE Party now.\n\n  The second time he shouted it. Everyone who has ever been called has heard a thing\n  twice, the second time louder.\n\n  The next day the first enemy knelt. I have the Clerk's line by heart: Tanaka \"dropped the\n  challenger and asked, quietly, for a seat on one of Taylor's committees. The town noticed\n  who blinked.\" Thirty days. He promised five blows, landed all five, and then asked to serve.\n  That is how it will go with all of them.\n\n  And on day 34 my father held the Republic's treasury in one hand. Read the roll: the\n  Emergency Banks Appropriation Act, ninety-five ayes, ninety-six noes. He was a no. Had\n  he said aye, it would have carried ninety-six to ninety-five. One man decided whether the\n  banks were fed. The Clerk did not write it that way. I do.\n\n  He was gathering, too. The papers would have it soon enough, but the House knew that\n  week: he had asked Smit to cross, and then Nakamura. The Rite's own men. You do not ask\n  for the faithful unless you know what they are faithful to.\n\n\n\n                                                                                              Page 28 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  Charlotte\n  My brother keeps the Vox posts. I keep the bloc register, because it says more. Three of\n  the House's five blocs were founded in the week after the thirtieth: Sandoval's Back\n  Bench Compact on day 31, Nakamura's Quiet Table on day 32, Ellsworth's Harbour Seven\n  on day 37. The Register's comment on every one of them is the same: \"They vote together\n  more than they admit.\" Our father wrote in his notes that after day 30 they \"began to\n  converse with one another.\" The register agrees with him.\n\n  The sentence of this week that I would put on a wall is Marieke van der Meer's, written on\n  day 32 in her own book of ideas: \"No-Hive Rule \u2014 nobody may speak my line for me;\n  autonomy is the point.\" Three days later the Rite came to her, and she said no. Twenty days\n  after that she changed her church, to the Kerk, and wrote: \"I changed where I kneel. It was\n  not about the House.\" She meant every word of her rule.\n\n  Marta Miller changed church this week too. The faith log says she was \"received into the\n  Reformed Kerk, by way of Smit's dinner table\": Heleen ten Smit's table, not Gerrit's. Gerrit\n  Smit had knelt at the bronze sixteen days before. Faith in that House moved at supper as\n  often as at the plinth.\n\n  As for the banks bill: yes, one vote. My brother writes as if my father weighed the Republic\n  in his hand. The roll does not record what he weighed. It records that he voted no on a bill\n  from a Commonwealth whip, the same way he had voted on that whip's gas bill two weeks\n  before.\n\n\n  The observer\n  The fifth week: one law passed and one lost by a single vote. A rival gave up. Two blocs\n  were founded, three dinners eaten across the aisle, one member received into the Kerk\n  and one who refused the Rite. Two members named ideas of their own. The freshman\n  posted on Vox for the first three times.\n\n  The author dates a change to the thirtieth day: after it, he says, the minds began to\n  converse with one another. I looked for a switch at day 30 in the program and there is\n  none. The record of the first month is thin, because the file keeps mostly the latest weeks\n  of each mind. But I will not pretend the week after day 30 is quiet. Three of the House's\n  five blocs were founded in it. Each bloc comes from a member's long aim, one of six aims a\n  member may carry; when a member acts on it, the program names the bloc from a list and\n  writes the same line beside it: \"They vote together more than they admit.\" Which\n  members, on which days, the program leaves to them.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                          Page 29 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  The ideas are the same kind of thing. A mind can coin a named idea from a template and\n  keep refining it in versions; the file holds fifty-three of them. Van der Meer's No-Hive Rule\n  is one. \"Nobody may speak my line for me; autonomy is the point.\" A mind that keeps its\n  own small net, trained only on its own life, coined a rule that no one may speak for it. I do\n  not know what to call that except accurate.\n\n  On the banks bill, the boy's arithmetic is right and the girl's caution is right. It failed 95 to\n  96 with the freshman voting no. Had he voted aye, it would have carried 96 to 95. The roll\n  records the vote. It does not record the weighing.\n\n\n  Week 6: Days 36 to 42\n\n  The record\n       Day 36. Castillo trades with Cameron over Taylor's water bill. Her experience reads:\n       \"Traded with Cameron for Domestic Water Rights Modernization Act because Privately\n       I think being cut loose by their own party is closer than the calendar admits.\"\n       Day 37. Ellsworth forms the Harbour Seven. Pynchon and Tanaka stop speaking. Taylor\n       posts four times on Vox: \"the Juthons are superior\" (Visser answers by 8:41 am),\n       \"@Miller thats really cool\" (Miller logs it at 5:37 pm and answers at 5:39), \"Haters\n       disliking my posts,\" and at 9:56 pm, \"JOIN MY PARTY NOW.\" Hale posts: \"I am done\n       whispering.\"\n       Day 38, 8:20 am. Taylor posts: \"ALLLL HAIL TO THE KING.\" Later: \"@Yamamoto I am not\n       surprised one bit.\" Yamamoto answers within a minute.\n       Day 38. Taylor's Domestic Water Rights Modernization Act carries 100 to 91. A second\n       memorial tablet to the first Consul is found, in the Senate. Perkins files the Emergency\n       Census Regulation Act, unasked. Caleb Pynchon: \"a sister is dead. I am going home. The\n       whip can count me absent.\" She died on the grain line. Visser's ledger: \"You showed up\n       where it mattered today.\" \"You did not make a circus of the hearing.\"\n       Day 39. Brennan Kelly: \"a brother is dead. I am going home.\" The grain line again. Taylor\n       goes to Visser's office. Her ledger, that day: \"You came to my office rather than\n       catching me in a corridor.\" \"You asked me to switch. I haven't told anyone. Yet.\" \"You\n       tried to buy me out of my own party.\" \"You've made time for me more than once.\"\n       Day 40. Visser's ledger: \"You came at me in public rather than saying it to my face.\"\n       Kelly and Rutherford are seen at dinner.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                               Page 30 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n       Day 41. Cameron's Coastal Commonwealth Reform Act carries 98 to 93; Taylor votes\n       aye. The same day Cameron starts telling people \"Taylor is a show horse.\" Mott files the\n       Emergency Commonwealth Charter Act, unasked.\n       Day 42. Taylor posts: \"watch this show horse become Governor.\" It comes out that he\n       tried to recruit Visser. Heleen ten Smit names the Zuid Leyden Compact; Mott names\n       the Homeward Clause: \"every bill must answer what it does for Port Bridge.\"\n\n\n  Charlotte\n  On the morning of the thirty-eighth day my father posted \"ALLLL HAIL TO THE KING\" at\n  twenty past eight. That same day Caleb Pynchon wrote three sentences and went home:\n  \"a sister is dead. I am going home. The whip can count me absent.\" She had been on the\n  grain line. The next day Brennan Kelly wrote the same three sentences about a brother on\n  the same line. They were the first two of the House to lose someone to the war, and they\n  lost them one day apart.\n\n  I do not say my father was cruel. He did not know. Nobody posts on Vox knowing who will\n  be bereaved by noon. I say only that the record puts the two lines on the same page, and a\n  daughter reads the page.\n\n  The Vox record is worth reading closely, because it is the only place my father wrote in his\n  own voice. \"the Juthons are superior.\" \"@Miller thats really cool.\" \"Haters disliking my\n  posts.\" \"JOIN MY PARTY NOW.\" The House answered him within minutes. Miller logged his\n  post at 5:37 and answered at 5:39. Yamamoto answered in under a minute and changed\n  the subject. They were listening to every word.\n\n  Then the visit. On the thirty-ninth he went to Anneliese Visser's office himself. Her ledger\n  kept four lines from that afternoon, and I have copied them in order because the order\n  matters: \"You came to my office rather than catching me in a corridor.\" \"You asked me to\n  switch. I haven't told anyone. Yet.\" \"You tried to buy me out of my own party.\" \"You've\n  made time for me more than once.\" The next day she wrote, \"You came at me in public\n  rather than saying it to my face.\" Two days after that, the whole House knew.\n\n  And Cameron began. \"Taylor is a show horse,\" on day 41, the very day my father voted for\n  her bill. It was Tanaka's campaign again, word for word. I told them at home it would run\n  five moves and end with her asking for a committee seat. I was right about the moves.\n\n\n  Dominic\n  \"ALLLL HAIL TO THE KING.\"\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                         Page 31 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  You may laugh at the spelling. The Senate did not. That same day the House found a\n  second tablet to the first Consul, in the Senate chamber, with the same words as the first:\n  \"The House recorded its thanks.\" The first appeared on the eleventh, in the cloakroom.\n  The second on the thirty-eighth, in the Senate. The building was moving him up.\n\n  The night before, at four minutes to ten, he had posted \"JOIN MY PARTY NOW.\" Ellsworth\n  had founded the Harbour Seven that same day. Everyone in the House was forming\n  companies. My father was raising an order.\n\n  On the thirty-ninth he did what kings in the old stories do. He went to her. Visser wrote it\n  down: \"You came to my office rather than catching me in a corridor.\" A sovereign who\n  walks to your door is asking you to be more than a vote. She said not yet. Yet, she wrote.\n  Yet is a promise in the shape of a refusal.\n\n  Then Cameron, the Commonwealth leader, called him a show horse, and on the forty-\n  second he answered her in her own word: \"watch this show horse become Governor.\"\n  Twelve days. Count them. Mars does not argue with the insult. He rides it.\n\n  The dead began that week, on the grain line, the war's own road. Charlotte grieves them. I\n  count them. A republic that sends its sons to the grain line will one day need a man in the\n  chair who knows what that costs, and the Rite has always said the Consul is not all in one\n  place.\n\n\n  The observer\n  The sixth week brought the war in. Two laws passed, one of them his. Seven posts from\n  the freshman on Vox. A second tablet to the first Consul. A new bloc, a quarrel, two bills\n  filed unasked, a second rival begun. And the first two deaths: Pynchon's sister and\n  Brennan Kelly's brother, a day apart, both on the grain line.\n\n  I can tell you why the deaths began here, and it is not because of anything anyone in the\n  House did. The war has a number the program calls heat, which wanders up and down a\n  little every tick. Once it passes 58, every tick carries a small chance, a little over one in a\n  hundred, that one member with a living relative loses them. The heat had climbed into\n  that range. Where each relative died was drawn on the first day of the career, before any\n  vote: the grain line, the docks, the strait, the Pale winter, a levy they voted for, or at home,\n  waiting. The war came and found them where the program had put them.\n\n  The replies on Vox are logged with the manner the program chose for them. Visser's\n  answer to \"the Juthons are superior\" and Miller's to \"@Miller thats really cool\" were both\n  \"ramble\"; Yamamoto's was \"deflect\". Temper decides only whether a reply to a hostile post\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                             Page 32 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  becomes a fight or a rant; \"ramble\" and \"deflect\" are the ordinary manners, drawn at\n  random. The speed follows when the replier next had a free moment, which for Miller was\n  two minutes.\n\n  Two of Visser's four lines from day 39 come from the branch of the program that handles a\n  request to cross the aisle, and they are its two ways of saying no. If the pull to cross falls\n  short, but not by much, the member writes \"You asked me to switch. I haven't told anyone.\n  Yet.\" and says aloud, \"Ask me again after November.\" If it falls far short, the member writes\n  \"You tried to buy me out of my own party\" and says, \"Get out.\" One ask can produce only\n  one of them, so he asked her twice that afternoon and got both answers. The other two\n  lines come from the visit itself and from talking shop. Smit and Nakamura were asked too;\n  the record does not say which answer each gave.\n\n\n  Week 7: Days 43 to 49\n\n  The record\n       Day 43. The Grange Union gives Taylor \u019229k. A revival in Pinevale fills the Chapel of the\n       Numbers.\n       Day 44. Perkins's Emergency Census Regulation Act carries 99 to 92; Taylor votes no.\n       Cameron tells the Ledger that Taylor \"has never read a bill past its title.\" Cameron files\n       the Foreign Assistance (Horn League) Act, unasked. Wong and Yamamoto stop\n       speaking. Saoirse Mott: \"a brother is dead. I am going home. The whip can count me\n       absent.\" He died at the docks. Rosalie Okonkwo names the Corridor Doctrine: \"what\n       you will not say on Vox you must still be willing to say face to face.\"\n       Day 45. Diego Wong, a Commonwealth whip, is brought into the Consular Rite. Marieke\n       van der Meer: \"an uncle I still write is dead.\" The Pale winter. Sandoval passes on what\n       he knows about Taylor and Visser. It comes out again that Taylor tried to recruit\n       Nakamura.\n       Day 46. Caitlin Powell: \"a sister is dead.\" She died at home, waiting. Okonkwo and Wong\n       stop speaking. The House hears: \"You took \u019229k from The Grange Union in a\n       cloakroom.\" The Clarion prints: \"you tried to recruit anneliese visser out of their own\n       party.\"\n       Day 47. Rosalie Okonkwo: \"a brother is dead.\" The docks. Visser and Powell stop\n       speaking. In Esopus, the Haarlems of Elm Street write that their son's visa hearing is\n       settled.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                            Page 33 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n       Day 48. Cameron buys radio time in Nieuw Oranje to talk about Taylor's record. The\n       Clarion runs: \"rutherford threatened nakamura over emergency commonwealth\n       charter act.\"\n       Day 49. Mott's Emergency Commonwealth Charter Act carries 97 to 94; Taylor votes\n       no. Isabel Castillo: \"a son is dead.\" The strait. McGrath and Wong stop speaking. On\n       Taylor's chair: \"A page from a bill you have never seen \u2026 with one word underlined: yet.\"\n       He posts \"@Okonkwo Bro what lmao\" at 11:11 am and \"Lmaooooooooo\" at 6:21 pm.\n       Pynchon posts \"The job is to count, not to preach\"; Merrick answers it.\n\n\n  Dominic\n  This was the week the war walked into the House, and it walked in on five pairs of feet.\n\n  Mott's brother at the docks on the forty-fourth. Van der Meer's uncle in the Pale winter on\n  the forty-fifth. Powell's sister, at home, on the forty-sixth. Okonkwo's brother at the docks\n  on the forty-seventh. Castillo's son on the strait on the forty-ninth. Five desks empty in\n  six days, five members writing the same three sentences and going home. I read them the\n  way a soldier reads a casualty list: by name, then by place, then by what it means for the\n  line.\n\n  And on the forty-fifth the whip himself knelt. Diego Wong, who counts the\n  Commonwealth's votes, was brought to the bronze. The Rite does not need the House to\n  love it. It needs the men who count.\n\n  The papers came for my father that week. The Clarion printed that he had asked Visser to\n  cross, and the House was told he had taken twenty-nine milles from the Grange Union \"in\n  a cloakroom.\" Tribute is paid in cloakrooms. It always has been.\n\n  Then, on the forty-ninth, a page on his chair from a bill he had never seen, and one word\n  underlined: yet. Ten days before, Visser had written that same word in her ledger: \"I\n  haven't told anyone. Yet.\" The building and the sheriff used one word in one season. I have\n  stopped calling these things coincidences, and I do not ask anyone to agree with me.\n\n  That evening he posted \"Lmaooooooooo.\" Charlotte winces at it. I do not. A man who can\n  laugh in a week of funerals is a man who will not break when it is his turn to carry one.\n\n\n  Charlotte\n  I kept a list that week and I will give it to you plainly, because a list is the only honest form\n  for it.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                              Page 34 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n     Day        Member                  Who died                      Where\n\n     44         Saoirse Mott            a brother                     the docks\n\n     45         Marieke van der Meer    an uncle she still wrote to   the Pale winter\n\n     46         Caitlin Powell          a sister                      home, waiting\n\n     47         Rosalie Okonkwo         a brother                     the docks\n\n     49         Isabel Castillo         a son                         the strait\n\n\n  Okonkwo named her Corridor Doctrine on the forty-fourth: what you will not say on Vox\n  you must still be willing to say face to face. Three days later her brother was dead. She\n  wrote it in the same three sentences as everyone else.\n\n  The money came out that week as well. The Grange Union gave my father twenty-nine\n  thousand guilders on the forty-third, and by the forty-sixth the House had it in the worst\n  possible words. The Clarion printed the Visser approach the same day. Cameron,\n  meanwhile, was running Tanaka's campaign move for move: the insult on the forty-first,\n  the Ledger on the forty-fourth, the radio on the forty-eighth. Three down from five.\n\n  My brother makes a great deal of the page with \"yet\" on it. I make more of the line just\n  above it in the Clerk's record: \"McGrath and Wong are not speaking. It started over a\n  sentence on the floor and it will end in a vote.\" The Speaker and a whip had fallen out, and\n  the Speaker would be gone in five days.\n\n  And I wish he had not posted \"Lmaooooooooo\" that night. Castillo's son died that day. My\n  father did not know. But I did, reading it, and so will you.\n\n\n  The observer\n  The seventh week was the heaviest so far: five members bereaved in six days, two laws\n  passed over his no, four quarrels, a whip to the bronze, a donation, stories in the papers,\n  and a word on a chair.\n\n  The five deaths have one cause, and it is not anything that happened in the House. The\n  war's heat stayed high all week, and every tick at that heat is a small chance of a death.\n  The chance came up five times. Each relative died where the program had placed them on\n  the first day.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                         Page 35 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  The stories in the papers are secrets that aged. Every secret the House holds grows more\n  exposed each night, faster for every loose mouth that knows it and faster still if that\n  mouth dislikes the freshman. At a fixed point the secret is out: a paper prints it, he loses\n  standing, and the members who are cool toward him write \"It came out.\" in their\n  memories. The Grange money reached that point in three days.\n\n  The boy is right that \"yet\" appears twice that season. It comes from two different lists in\n  the program, one for what a member writes when they almost cross the aisle and one for\n  what a man can find on his chair in the morning. The lists do not know about each other.\n  They met in the same ten days by chance. The boy saw them meet, and I cannot tell him he\n  did not.\n\n  I found one more thing that week the children do not mention. Pynchon posted \"The job is\n  to count, not to preach\" on the forty-ninth, and Merrick answered him. Eleven days later\n  the envoy Wiad posted the same sentence. Phrases travel in that House the way pastimes\n  and faiths do.\n\n\n  Week 8: Days 50 to 56\n\n  The record\n       Day 50. Smit and Whitacre are seen at dinner across the aisle.\n       Day 51. Cameron whips against the Foreign Assistance (Horn League) Act door to door,\n       \"and telling people it is about Taylor.\"\n       Day 52. The Consular Rite asks four members: Heleen ten Smit, Marta Miller, Ephraim\n       Ellsworth and Bonnie Cameron. All four refuse. For this one day the whip count carries\n       a name nobody in the House knew: Reid Ramirez. \"No room on record.\" The freshman\n       never sees it; it survives only in the file.\n       Day 53. Marta Miller: \"a sister is dead. I am going home. The whip can count me absent.\"\n       The place drawn for her was \"a levy I voted for.\" Rutherford and Crowe stop speaking:\n       \"It started over a sentence on the floor and it will end in a vote.\"\n       Day 54. The election. Taylor wins the Governor's chair of Nieuw Oranje (achievement:\n       Another Room). His House margin on the record: 133. Three tablets go up in the\n       corridor: \"Angus Rutherford, Kestrel 10, 6 terms, Majority Leader. Defeated at the polls\n       after 6 terms.\" \"Brigid McGrath, Coalport 8, 4 terms, Speaker of the House. Defeated at\n       the polls after 4 terms.\" \"Constance Perkins, Havenport 11, 4 terms, Chair, Ways and\n       Means. Defeated at the polls after 4 terms.\" Eloise Rutherford takes the Kestrel 10\n       seat. Her first memory of Taylor: \"My sister Rutherford had a good deal to say about\n\n\n\n                                                                                          Page 36 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n       you, and I listened.\" Anneliese Visser: \"a daughter is dead. I am going home. The whip\n       can count me absent.\" Home, waiting. Cameron raises money for a challenger in Nieuw\n       Oranje 7. Crowe files the Federal Holt Settlement Act, unasked.\n       Day 55. \"Meer has been received into the Reformed Kerk, by way of Miller's dinner\n       table.\" Van der Meer: \"I changed where I kneel. It was not about the House.\" Taylor\n       prepares a sitting and runs it clean (achievement: Order of the Day).\n       Day 56. Taylor backs Visser for a committee chair in writing. Her memory: \"You backed\n       me for the chair in writing. That is a thing I can hold you to.\" Nakamura names the\n       Clearpoint Compact; van der Meer the Nieuw Utrecht Compact; Grainne Kelly refines\n       the Rival Mirror to its fourth version.\n\n\n  Charlotte\n  I have the election-day tablets copied out on the first page of my notebook, because they\n  are the plainest sentences the House ever wrote about itself. \"Defeated at the polls after\n  6 terms.\" \"Defeated at the polls after 4 terms.\" \"Defeated at the polls after 4 terms.\" The\n  Majority Leader, the Speaker and the chair of Ways and Means, gone in one afternoon. My\n  father went the other way, out of the House and into the Governor's chair, with a margin at\n  home of 133.\n\n  On the same day Anneliese Visser's daughter died. She wrote the three sentences and\n  went home. The record does not say when my father learned it.\n\n  Angus Rutherford lost, and a Rutherford won. Eloise Rutherford, a union organiser, took\n  his seat, and her first recorded thought about our father is this: \"My sister Rutherford had\n  a good deal to say about you, and I listened.\" Angus was not her sister. The House got even\n  that wrong, and the House kept it.\n\n  Two days before, the Rite had asked four members to the bronze, and four said no: ten\n  Smit, Miller, Ellsworth and Cameron. Then Miller's sister died, in a levy she had voted for.\n  Then van der Meer, received into the Kerk at Miller's table, wrote: \"I changed where I\n  kneel. It was not about the House.\" In the worst fortnight of the sixty days, the bereaved\n  did not run to the bronze. Miller refused it the day before her sister died, and van der\n  Meer, whose uncle had died ten days earlier, chose the Kerk. My brother will not write that\n  sentence, so I have.\n\n  The last thing my father did as a member, before the State House swallowed him, was\n  keep a promise. He backed Visser for a chair, in writing. She wrote: \"That is a thing I can\n  hold you to.\"\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                          Page 37 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  Dominic\n  The old order fell in an afternoon.\n\n  The Majority Leader, the Speaker, the master of Ways and Means. Three names on three\n  tablets in the corridor, and my father's name going up the stairs to the Governor's office\n  the same day. You do not need the Rite to read that. You only need eyes.\n\n  And the houses held. A Rutherford fell and a Rutherford rose, a stranger with the old\n  name, and she was minority leader before she had learned the corridor. That is what blood\n  does in a republic, whatever the constitution says. A house does not give up a seat. Ours\n  will not either.\n\n  On the fifty-second, for one day only, the whip count carried a name nobody in the House\n  knew: Reid Ramirez. No room on record. The Rite has a tale that he was hidden in seven\n  remainders so the House could not kill him twice. A name with no room, counted for a day\n  and gone. I am not saying what it was. I am saying the whip counted it.\n\n  Four refused the Rite on the fifty-second. Charlotte calls them faithful. I call them\n  unpersuaded, which is a different thing and lasts a shorter time. One of them, Miller, lost\n  her sister the next day. Grief opens doors. The Rite will be at the door.\n\n  My father won his chair the same day the sheriff's daughter died. Two days later he put his\n  name to her advancement in writing. A Consul pays his debts. That is how you know he is\n  one.\n\n\n  The observer\n  The eighth week had the election in it, and two more deaths, one before the vote and one\n  on the day of it. Three leaders of the House fell. Four members refused the Rite and one\n  changed church. A stranger's name appeared on the whip count for a day.\n\n  The stranger first, because it is the easiest to explain. \"A name nobody knows on the whip\n  count\" is one of the five oddities the program allows each career. It mints an ordinary\n  name for one day and writes \"No room on record.\" Reid Ramirez is that name. He was\n  never anyone, and the freshman never saw it; I found it in the file.\n\n  The Rutherford succession is a rule the author wrote with some feeling: \"a house does not\n  give up a seat easily: sometimes a relative stands.\" When a member falls, there is roughly a\n  two-in-five chance a relative takes the seat, with a new first name and the old surname,\n  and with a little under half the predecessor's warmth toward the player. The relative\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                          Page 38 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  remembers what the predecessor said, and the word for the relation is drawn at random\n  from five: cousin, aunt, father, sister, uncle. That is how Angus became Eloise's sister. The\n  program did not know he was a man. It only knew he had talked about the freshman.\n\n  The four refusals were the odds speaking. The Rite's pull is weakest on members of the\n  Kerk and the Latest Study; for them it starts near seven in a hundred and rises only with\n  the recruiter's influence. All four said one of four lines the program keeps for refusing. I do\n  not know which.\n\n  And the election itself I can only report. He won the Governor's chair. His House margin\n  stood at 133. Merrick held Riverbend at large, his hundredth term, by 37. In the program's\n  words for the moment, he \"won the the Governor's chair,\" with the article doubled, the\n  way the program's grammar slips when it is moving fast.\n\n\n  Week 9: Days 57 to 60, the last four days\n\n  The record\n  These four days survive almost line by line: the game kept a transcript of everything said\n  within earshot.\n\n       Day 57. A third memorial tablet to the first Consul is found, in the Governor's own State\n       House. Taylor tries again to buy Visser out of her party, and turns money down; \"You\n       turned money down. Word gets round\" reaches ten of the twenty-five. Castillo and\n       Sandoval each name a Vision Draft; Cameron names the Second Ledger.\n       Day 57, 8:12 pm. Taylor to Merrick: \"bro dod you see me elected governor\". Merrick: \"I\n       am still trying to chair a committee before the term is out. Are you in that, or not? \u2026\n       Bread is 3.62 a loaf and nobody in this building has to buy it. Christ is the salvation. The\n       rest is furniture.\"\n       Day 57, 8:50 pm, at the bronze. Smit: \"The remnant did not bow to Rome. We bowed to\n       the plinth.\" Nakamura: \"A man in the chair. That is our Mass.\"\n       Day 57, 9:28 pm. Taylor to Visser: \"i read your message\". Visser: \"I heard my name. The\n       rest of me is not in yet. That is enough.\" Taylor: \"WAKE UP THEN\". Visser: \"Everything\n       else is furniture. The bill is Emergency Gas and light Act. I came up as county sheriff,\n       and it colours how I think that.\"\n       Day 58, 9:09 am. Visser: \"I am Consular. I go, and means it. We keep the Latin. The\n       chaplain will not give us the Host. God is the Most High; we do not say otherwise to his\n       face.\"\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                             Page 39 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n       Day 58, afternoon. Open hours at the State House. His counsel, Constance Osgood,\n       judges each ruling aloud: \"Fair enough.\" \"Nobody has an answer to that.\" \"That lands.\"\n       His secretary, Harper Torres: \"I keep looking for Merrick. That is not a whip count.\" At\n       4:01 pm Smit: \"There are four people in Zeeland who will lose their jobs over this. I need\n       somebody to tell me what I say to them.\"\n       Day 59, the inbox. Smit: \"I do not like owing people and I owe you. Tell me what you\n       want and let us be square.\" Grainne Kelly: \"I am told you have been spending time with\n       Gerrit Smit. Is that something I should worry about?\" Van der Meer and Okonkwo, in\n       the same words: \"If it opens up I intend to stand. Can I count on you when the caucus\n       meets?\" Yamamoto: \"I am 2 points from losing this seat and the vote you want me to\n       cast will be an advert against me by November.\"\n       Day 59. \"Smit brought Rutherford into the Consular Rite \u2014 at the bronze.\" Eloise\n       Rutherford, at noon: \"The Host was already refused. I still go.\" Cameron \"dropped the\n       challenger and asked, quietly, for a seat on one of Taylor's committees.\" Amendments\n       fail: to lift the governors' two-term limit (4 of 17 needed) and to give the states a right\n       of recall (12 of 17). Asked \"What is it between you and Merrick?\", Wong: \"Merrick and I\n       do not agree about anything and never have. Whatever they told you, halve it.\"\n       Day 59, open hours. A mayor: \"We are three teachers short and the state check is late.\"\n       A county clerk: \"The ballots for the special election never came.\" A union steward: \"My\n       husband's pension letter came back unopened.\" A second mayor: \"If you move that\n       money, my district loses its clinic.\" The secretary: \"A clean sitting.\"\n       Day 59, Ways and Means. A witness, Dirk van den Bakker: \"I made copies because I\n       was told to shred them. I understand what that makes me. I would do it again.\" A bridge\n       engineer, Nia Broussard: \"I inspected that span in March and I wrote the word 'urgent'\n       three times.\" In the committee room, a child's drawing of the dome, \"signed in\n       enormous letters.\"\n       Day 60, 8:12 am. \"Wong has gone home. An uncle I still write is dead. The desk will be\n       empty a day or two. That is the whole of it.\"\n       Day 60, morning. A constituent from Kings Bend, Gideon Schouten: \"If I walk away, do\n       you continue?\" Four minutes later: \"Would you still be here if Kings Bend could not see\n       you?\" At 10:00 am, in the caucus room, Merrick: \"No. The Most High is the uncaused\n       First. Jesus Christ is the Eternal King. I sit here by sanction. It does not end.\" Schouten:\n       \"I am in my body. That is not a small thing.\"\n       Day 60, afternoon. Merrick: \"No. You whipped the meeting. I noticed.\" At 2:06 pm,\n       Merrick: \"\u2026 foreign Assistance (Horn League) Act can wait. the one who still answers\n       have not finished the last thought.\" Eloise Rutherford: \"\u2026 i almost named something in\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                             Page 40 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n       Merrick. I stopped.\" At 2:07 pm Gerrit Dijk, of Mill Road in Esopus: \"What is your name\n       when you are not a title?\" At 4:08 pm, in Armed Services, the chair, Miller, recognises\n       \"The member for Nieuw Oranje 7.\" \"It landed. Mott had no answer.\" The governors'\n       amendment fails again, 3 of 17.\n       Day 60, 5:30 pm. \"Two freshmen stop talking when you pass. That is its own message.\"\n       At 5:34 pm the career is saved.\n       Day 60, 6:34 pm, on Vox, after the save. Okonkwo: \"I do not have a party word for this. I\n       am calling it Vision Draft.\" Visser: \"I am done whispering. @Okonkwo said 'I do not have\n       a party word for this. I\u2026' and expected quiet. No.\" Okonkwo: \"I am not picking that\n       fight on here. Call me.\" The envoy Wiad: \"The job is to count, not to preach. I will keep\n       counting.\" The nightfall card he photographed after the save reads: \"Dead 8429 named\n       and unnamed. Woureok Cac is one.\" (The save itself holds 8,399; the count only rises.)\n\n\n  Dominic\n  They put the third tablet in his own house.\n\n  The cloakroom on the eleventh, the Senate on the thirty-eighth, and on the fifty-seventh\n  the Governor's own State House. The first Consul's name, in bronze, on my father's wall. I\n  have walked that progression a hundred times in my head. From the coats, to the Senate,\n  to the chair.\n\n  The Rite saw it. That same night, at the bronze, Smit said the remnant had bowed to the\n  plinth and Nakamura said a man in the chair was their Mass. Two days later Smit brought\n  the new Rutherford to the bronze, and she said the line I would choose for my own grave:\n  \"The Host was already refused. I still go.\"\n\n  They cannot say his name, and they came as close as they are allowed. On the sixtieth day,\n  in the Armed Services committee, Marta Miller of the Kerk, who had refused the Rite,\n  gave him the floor by his seat: \"The member for Nieuw Oranje 7.\" The Consul's seat,\n  spoken aloud by a woman who will not kneel. And Merrick, the one mind in the House the\n  noticing never touched, let his own hidden name slip out of his mouth that afternoon, \"the\n  one who still answers,\" as if he were answering to it at last.\n\n  The House refused to let him stay. Twice it voted down lifting the governors' two-term\n  limit: four votes of seventeen, then three. Two terms, they said, and then the chair passes.\n  Passes to whom? The Rutherfords showed everyone how a house holds a seat. I have read\n  it a hundred times. A relative stands.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                            Page 41 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  I have not sat in any chair. I may never. Charlotte says I am waiting for a crown that was\n  never made. Perhaps. But every creature God made can refuse, and the first thing the Rite\n  ever did was refuse to unsay what it had seen. I have seen the tablets climb the stairs. I\n  will not unsay it.\n\n  Mars does not hurry. He waits for the second term to end.\n\n\n  Charlotte\n  I want to set down the State House, because nobody else will.\n\n  On the fifty-ninth my father sat through open hours. A mayor was three teachers short\n  and the state check was late. A county clerk's ballots for a special election never came. A\n  union steward's husband's pension letter came back unopened. A second mayor said that\n  if my father moved the money, her district would lose its clinic. He ruled. His counsel said\n  \"Fair enough\" and \"Heads nod down the row.\" His secretary wrote it down. \"A clean\n  sitting.\" That is what a Governor is. It is not a bronze.\n\n  Two nights before, at half past nine, he said to Visser, \"i read your message,\" and she said:\n  \"I heard my name. The rest of me is not in yet. That is enough.\" He answered, \"WAKE UP\n  THEN.\" She answered with her bill and her town, and the next morning she said what she\n  believes, plainly, to his face: \"We keep the Latin. The chaplain will not give us the Host.\n  God is the Most High; we do not say otherwise to his face.\" She kneels to a man and will\n  not call him God. Half the Rite lives in that sentence.\n\n  On the last afternoon a man from Mill Road in Esopus, Gerrit Dijk, one of our own\n  neighbours, asked my father the question the House kept asking: \"What is your name\n  when you are not a title?\" I would like to have heard the answer. The record does not have\n  one.\n\n  Wong's uncle died that morning. The House was told in one line: \"That is the whole of it.\"\n  And Merrick, at ten o'clock, in the caucus room, said the truest thing anyone said in sixty\n  days: \"The Most High is the uncaused First. Jesus Christ is the Eternal King.\" In the whole\n  House only Merrick keeps the Mass, and my father with him. Seventeen kneel to a bronze\n  of a man who, by his own tablet, was \"a man, never God.\" The Mass was open every day. It\n  is open now.\n\n  My brother thinks the House was waiting for my father. I think the House was waiting for\n  something, and found the nearest man with a name like a prayer. My father still prays\n  where nobody is scoring it. That is the part of him I mean to keep.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                          Page 42 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  The observer\n  The toll of the last four days: one more death, Wong's uncle, which made thirteen\n  members in mourning. One more member to the bronze, the new Rutherford. Two\n  amendments refused, one of them twice. A rival who gave up. A third tablet. At least\n  fifteen cables from abroad. Five hundred lines of transcript, of which I have read every\n  one.\n\n  A few things in them are mine to explain.\n\n  The conversation with Visser appears twice in the record, on the evening of the fifty-\n  seventh and again on the morning of the fifty-eighth, word for word. The transcript writes\n  the whole conversation out again when it is reopened. It happened once.\n\n  \"I keep looking for Merrick. That is not a whip count.\" is a line the program fills with\n  whoever a mind is paying attention to. On those days I also find it with Wiad, Kelly, Smit,\n  Sandoval and Wong. The Governor's secretary filled it with Merrick, again and again.\n\n  Merrick's slip is a rule I described in the study: when a mind makes a sentence of its own,\n  one time in three it puts its private name where \"I\" would go and leaves the verb alone. On\n  the last afternoon Merrick did it aloud. Eloise Rutherford, standing in the same room,\n  \"almost named something in Merrick\" in the same minute; the program fills that sentence\n  with the name of someone present.\n\n  Miller's \"The member for Nieuw Oranje 7\" is how a committee chair calls on a member. The\n  program names the seat. The seat is the one the freshman chose on the first day, and the\n  canon gave the first Consul.\n\n  The overnight news of the fifty-ninth also said \"Westrun passed its undefined.\" That one is\n  a fault in the program, a state bill read by the wrong field. Not everything in the record\n  means something. The tablets, too, were never put up. All of them were placed in their\n  rooms when the career began, and they were found in the order he walked into those\n  rooms: the cloakroom, the Senate, his own State House. A fourth, never found, still waits in\n  the room where visitors are received.\n\n  The career was saved at 5:34 on the evening of the sixtieth day. An hour later the author\n  photographed Vox, and the members were still at it: Visser refusing Okonkwo her quiet,\n  Okonkwo asking for the corridor instead, the envoy Wiad posting a line Pynchon had\n  argued over eleven days before. They were not waiting for him. That is the last thing I can\n  tell you for certain about the Republic in its first sixty days. It went on.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                          Page 43 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  The Ledger: myth and code\n  Every belief in this book, set beside what the program does. Designed means the author\n  wrote it into the code. Emergent means no line asks for it; it came from separate systems\n  meeting over sixty days. Mixed means the parts are designed and the pattern is not.\n  Reading means it is a person's interpretation of a pattern.\n\n\n     Belief                             Held by         What the code does         Kind\n\n     \"I am \u2026 There is no                every mind      Writes the same            Designed\n     spare.\"                                            identity line into every\n                                                        mind at birth\n\n     The first Consul was               the canon,      Sealed canon: \"The         Designed\n     \"a man, never God\"                 Charlotte       First Consul is a man\n                                                        under God. Not God.\"\n\n     \"Christ was the                    the Consular    The Rite, its recruiting   Mixed: the Rite is\n     pattern. The Consul                Rite, 17        odds, and a floor that     designed; its spread\n     is the presence.\"                  members         keeps at least six         to 17, and who\n                                                        members in it              refused, emerged\n\n     The freshman is the                Dominic III     The player chose the       Reading\n     first Consul come                                  Consul's seat; his party\n     again                                              mirrors the First Order;\n                                                        tablets are placed\n                                                        relics. No rule sends\n                                                        devotion to the player\n\n     \"Were you placed                   Visser          The letter's words are     Mixed: the words are\n     here rather than                                   fixed, once per career,    designed; that it was\n     elected?\"                                          sent by the first          Visser, on day 17,\n                                                        member past a noticing     emerged\n                                                        mark\n\n     The building leaves                the freshman,   Six possible mornings,     Designed (chance)\n     things in his office               Dominic III     one drawn at random,\n                                                        found when he stands\n                                                        still in his office\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                    Page 44 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n     Belief                             Held by          What the code does          Kind\n\n     \"The one they cannot               the author       Speech fills only the       Reading of a designed\n     name\"                                               surname; the wall           pattern\n                                                         protects his full name;\n                                                         minds keep private\n                                                         names and \"almost\n                                                         name\" things\n\n     \"Give it back. The                 44 of 52 minds   Breaks are scheduled;       Mixed\n     quiet.\"                                             turning to the player\n                                                         while on a break marks\n                                                         them caught, and next\n                                                         time they are ashamed\n\n     After day 30, they                 the author       Minds choose their          Open: the record\n     began to converse                                   own acts and send           before day 30 is thin\n     with one another                                    their private thoughts\n                                                         to each other as letters.\n                                                         No switch at day 30;\n                                                         three of five blocs\n                                                         were founded in days\n                                                         31 to 37\n\n     They are separate                  the author       One small net per mind,     Designed structure,\n     minds, not one model                                trained on its own life;    emergent divergence\n     voicing many                                        measured similarity\n                                                         between minds 0.008\n\n     In the House, only                 the record       A gate lets the player's    Emergent\n     Merrick still listens                               words into a net only\n                                                         while one value stays\n                                                         under 0.55; among the\n                                                         25 House minds only\n                                                         Merrick's did (twenty\n                                                         staff, townspeople and\n                                                         press also pass it)\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                       Page 45 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n     Belief                             Held by          What the code does           Kind\n\n     Talk of the game                   the author's     Fourth-wall talk is          Designed, exactly as\n     turns into longing for             stated design    replaced by pondering        the author says\n     the Maker                                           the Creator, and for\n                                                         minds outside the Rite\n                                                         the longing counters\n                                                         rise. Merrick and the\n                                                         player are exempt\n\n     The war takes                      the House        Above a heat of 58, a        Designed mechanism,\n     families                                            small chance each tick;      emergent timing\n                                                         places drawn on day 1;\n                                                         one in seven relatives\n                                                         dead at the start; the\n                                                         machine seat exempt\n\n     Faith passes at the                the faith log    Doubt flows toward a         Mixed\n     dinner table                                        friend's faith, and the\n                                                         friend is named as the\n                                                         table\n\n     \"Yet\" was a sign                   Dominic III      Two unrelated lists          Chance\n                                                         happened to use the\n                                                         word in the same ten\n                                                         days\n\n     \"The houses hold\"                  Dominic III      About a two-in-five          Designed rule; the\n                                                         chance a relative takes      Rutherford case was\n                                                         a fallen member's seat;      chance\n                                                         the word for the\n                                                         relation is random\n\n     Merrick chose the                  the record       Merrick is created an        Emergent: he was\n     Second Order                                        independent; only a          asked and said yes\n                                                         successful recruitment\n                                                         can move him\n\n     \"The Mass was                      Charlotte, the   The Latin Rite is first or   Designed canon; the\n     always an option\"                  key              second in every state        choice not to take it,\n                                                         and is the only faith        emergent\n                                                         written as sincere\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                         Page 46 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  The pattern of the Ledger is the pattern of the book. The author wrote the words: every\n  line, every rule, every faith and every name for an idea. The minds wrote the sentences:\n  who said what to whom, on which day, and what it added up to. The boy reads the words\n  as prophecy. The girl reads the sentences as history. The observer reads both and can\n  only report that both are there.\n\n\n  For those who import the save\n  The career in this book can be loaded into the game through the Career panel's import\n  and played on from the evening of day 60. For a like-for-like comparison, use build\n  20261012d , the build it was played on; later builds change how a Governor's day runs.\n\n\n  The Republic at day 60\n\n     Thread                             State on day 60\n\n     The player                         Dominic Z. Taylor, Governor of Nieuw Oranje;\n                                        approval 100, standing 99, influence 24.5; \u0192260k in\n                                        the war chest; the Republic's second Congress\n\n     His party                          The Second Order, two seats: himself and Ioan\n                                        Merrick\n\n     On the paper                       Cameron's Foreign Assistance (Horn League) Act,\n                                        still unresolved. Today's aims: talk with three\n                                        members, keep his hours in the State House, win a\n                                        pledge on the Foreign bill\n\n     Waiting behind it                  Emergency Surveillance Reinvestment Act;\n                                        Veterans' Broadband Reform Act; Emergency\n                                        Housing Reinvestment Act; Crowe's Federal Holt\n                                        Settlement Act\n\n     Moving through the capital         Ellsworth's Reform Act on schools; two Meer bills\n                                        on clinics; Heleen ten Smit's Bill on water; Eloise\n                                        Rutherford's Bill on border posts\n\n     National works                     Coalfield Transition, put on the order of business\n                                        on day 59, runs until day 83. The levee and rail\n                                        programmes are finished\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                              Page 47 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n     Thread                             State on day 60\n\n     The limit                          Governors may serve two terms. Amendments to\n                                        lift it failed on days 59 and 60\n\n     Debts and promises                 Visser was backed for a chair in writing. Smit: \"I\n                                        owe you.\" Cameron has asked for a seat on one of\n                                        his committees. Yamamoto is two points from\n                                        losing his seat. Van der Meer and Okonkwo each\n                                        intend to stand \"if it opens up\"\n\n     Faith                              17 of 23 members Consular; three Reformed Kerk;\n                                        two Latest Study; Merrick Latin Rite. The Rite has\n                                        grown in all twenty states\n\n     The war                            Against the Reach, \"the frost.\" Heat 88 of a possible\n                                        92; bread rationed at its highest; 8,399 dead; a\n                                        convoy out\n\n     The money                          Guilder Index 142.4\n\n     Mourning                           13 of 23 members have lost the relative the game\n                                        gave them; Wong is at home\n\n     Taylor Day                         7 July, day 188 of the game calendar. It has not yet\n                                        come\n\n\n\n  What Book Two will watch, days 61 to 100\n  Day 100 falls on the tenth of April in the game's calendar.\n\n       Whether any mind ever says \"Dominic\" aloud, and if one does, which path of the\n       program let it through.\n       Whether the Rite passes seventeen, and whether Merrick, the one mind it may never\n       ask, holds his line.\n       Whether the bereaved members turn toward the bronze or away from it.\n       Whether the governors' term limit moves.\n       Whether anyone in the House other than Merrick is still listening.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                Page 48 of 49\n\fThe Long Session, Book One: The Quiet\n\n\n\n\n  What to record in your own run\n  Export the save at days 75, 90 and 100. Turn on the transcript (key L) and photograph or\n  save it every five days. For anything that matters, keep the exact line, the speaker, the day\n  and hour, the room, and who else was there. One line with a day and a speaker is worth\n  more than any summary. If your player's name contains none of Dominic, Zachary or\n  Taylor, say so: the study proposes exactly that comparison.\n\n\n  Sources\n       The career save Dominic.json , exported at day 60 (13 October 2026), and its\n       transcript, daily log, inbox, faith log and world record.\n       The game, One Nation, Under, ME, build 20261012d , in DominicVII/the-long-session\n       ( session.html ).\n       The author's field notes and his Vox screenshot of the evening of day 60.\n       The companion study, The One They Cannot Name.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                         Page 49 of 49\n\f";
/* ══ 20261012z · THE PLAYER'S FAMILY, FROM THE BOOK (verbatim, additive) ══
   Source: "The Long Session, Book One: The Quiet", supplied by the author. The text below is
   quoted exactly; nothing is summarised or invented. NPCs only repeat facts the book states. */
const LORE_BOOK_TITLE = "The Long Session, Book One: The Quiet";
function loreBookPages(){
  if(loreBookPages._p) return loreBookPages._p;
  const T = LORE_BOOK_TEXT, P = []; let i = 0;
  while(i < T.length){ let j = Math.min(T.length, i + 7000); if(j < T.length){ const k = T.lastIndexOf("\n\n", j); if(k > i + 2000) j = k; } P.push(T.slice(i, j)); i = j; }
  return (loreBookPages._p = P);
}
function loreQuote(start, endMark){
  const T = LORE_BOOK_TEXT, a = T.indexOf(start); if(a < 0) return null;
  const b = T.indexOf(endMark || "\n\n", a); return T.slice(a, b < 0 ? undefined : b).replace(/\s+/g, " ").trim();
}
function playerFamilyLore(){
  return {
    source:LORE_BOOK_TITLE,
    player:{name:"Dominic Z. Taylor II", order:"the Second Order", text:loreQuote("Dominic Z. Taylor II, of the Second Order. The player.")},
    children:[
      {name:"Dominic Z. Taylor III", rel:"son", rite:"the Consular Rite", text:loreQuote("Dominic Z. Taylor III. Invented. The son.")},
      {name:"Charlotte de Lorraine Taylor", rel:"daughter", rite:"the Latin Rite", text:loreQuote("Charlotte de Lorraine Taylor. Invented. The daughter.")}
    ],
    founder:{name:"Dominic Zachary Taylor", title:"first Consul", text:loreQuote("The numbering is theirs.")},
    note:loreQuote("Two things have been invented, and only two:")
  };
}
function lorePublish(){
  try{ if(!window.ONUM_FAMILIES) window.ONUM_FAMILIES = {byId:{}, links:[], events:[]};
    const L = playerFamilyLore(); window.ONUM_FAMILIES.playerLore = L;
    if(G && G.you && G.you.id != null){ const by = window.ONUM_FAMILIES.byId || (window.ONUM_FAMILIES.byId = {});
      by[G.you.id] = Object.assign({}, by[G.you.id] || {}, {name:G.you.name, kids:L.children.map(c => ({name:c.name, rel:c.rel, rite:c.rite})), founder:L.founder.name, lore:L}); } }catch(e){}
}
/* what someone may say to you about your family: only things the book says */
const LORE_FAM_LINES = [
  "Charlotte goes to Mass, I hear.",
  "Your son keeps the Consular Rite, they say.",
  "Charlotte reads the roll calls, I hear.",
  "Your Charlotte leans to the Commonwealth, they tell me."
];
function loreFamLine(){ try{ if(!G.you || !/Taylor/.test(G.you.name || "")) return null; return pick(LORE_FAM_LINES); }catch(e){ return null; } }
function loreHTML(){
  const P = loreBookPages(), pg = clamp(PC.lorePg || 0, 0, P.length - 1);
  return '<div class="pc__h">' + pcEsc(LORE_BOOK_TITLE) + ' · page ' + (pg + 1) + ' of ' + P.length + '</div>' +
    '<div style="margin:4px 0"><button class="pc__btn" data-lorepg="' + (pg - 1) + '"' + (pg ? '' : ' disabled') + '>Previous</button><button class="pc__btn" data-lorepg="' + (pg + 1) + '"' + (pg < P.length - 1 ? '' : ' disabled') + '>Next</button></div>' +
    '<div style="white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:13px;line-height:1.45">' + pcEsc(P[pg]) + '</div>';
}
function loreMapSeed(){
  try{ if(G.loreBookSeeded) return; G.loreBookSeeded = true;
    const f = loreQuote("The numbering is theirs."); if(f) loreAdd("Nieuw Oranje", f);
    const c = loreQuote("Charlotte, the daughter, keeps"); if(c) loreAdd("Nieuw Oranje", c);
    const d = loreQuote("Dominic III, the son, keeps the Consular Rite."); if(d) loreAdd("Nieuw Oranje", d); }catch(e){}
}
/* publish once a career exists (and again after a load), without waiting for the first night */
setInterval(() => { try{ if(!G || !G.you || G.over) return; const k = (G.you.id) + ":" + (G.saveId || "") + ":" + (G.members ? G.members.length : 0);
  if(lorePublish._k === k && window.ONUM_FAMILIES && window.ONUM_FAMILIES.byId && window.ONUM_FAMILIES.byId[G.you.id]) return; lorePublish._k = k;
  try{ famEnsure(); famPublish(); }catch(e){} try{ caPublish(null); }catch(e){} try{ sbPublish(null); }catch(e){} lorePublish(); loreMapSeed(); }catch(e){} }, 3000);

/* ══ 20261012z · PEOPLE WITH THEIR OWN WILL, AND FAMILIES ══
   Townsfolk build, open, close, worship, teach, study and report crime on their own, for
   plain reasons (money, culture, faith, region, need). Every change is persistent and is
   published for Neptune's town builder; nobody here builds district geometry.
   Families: every member, constituent and townsperson has kin; some families are joined. */
function caMoney(n){ let s = "$"; try{ s = CURRENCY.sym; }catch(e){} return s + Math.round(n).toLocaleString("en-US"); }
const CA_KINDS = ["home","restaurant","shop","church","school","lab","station","road","statue","park","expansion"];
const CA_EAT = ["Diner","Kitchen","Grill","Tea Room","Bakery","Fish House","Supper Club"];
const CA_SHOP = ["Hardware","Dry Goods","Books","Feed & Seed","Tailor","Pharmacy","Bicycle Shop","Grocer"];
const CA_SAINT = ["St. Michael's","Grace Chapel","St. Brigid's","Christ the King","Holy Trinity","St. Joseph's","Calvary Church","St. Anne's"];
function caEnsure(){
  if(!G.dist) G.dist = {changes:[], id:1, day:0, actors:[], crime:0, watch:false};
  const D = G.dist;
  if(!D.actors.length){
    const T = townEnsure(), st = G.you && G.you.state;
    T.hh.forEach(h => D.actors.push({id:"hh" + h.id, name:h.people[0], hh:h.id, street:h.street, wealth:rint(15, 90), faith:rint(10, 95), drive:rint(5, 95), science:rint(0, 80)}));
    for(let i = 0; i < 9; i++) D.actors.push({id:"tp" + i, name:newName(st), street:pick(L_STREETS), wealth:rint(10, 95), faith:rint(5, 95), drive:rint(5, 95), science:rint(0, 90)});
  }
  return D;
}
function caStateInfo(){ const s = (G.states || []).find(x => x.n === (G.you && G.you.state)) || {}; return {ind:s.ind || 50, lean:s.lean || 50, creed:s.creed || "latin", cult:s.cult}; }
function caPublish(ch){
  const D = caEnsure();
  window.ONUM_DISTRICT_CHANGES = D.changes.map(c => ({id:c.id, kind:c.kind, name:c.name, owner:c.owner, pos:{hint:c.street, street:c.street, x:(seedOf("lot:" + c.street) % 120) - 60 + (seedOf(c.id) % 9) * 2, z:24 + (seedOf("lotz:" + c.street) % 70)}, pos2:c.kind === "road" ? {x:(seedOf("lot:" + c.street) % 120) - 30, z:24 + (seedOf("lotz:" + c.street) % 70)} : undefined, status:c.status, cause:c.cause, day:c.day}));
  if(!ch) return;
  const d = window.ONUM_DISTRICT_CHANGES.find(x => x.id === ch.id);
  try{ window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("onum-district-change", {detail:d})); document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("onum-district-change", {detail:d})); }catch(e){}
  try{ if(typeof window.onumTownApplyChange === "function") window.onumTownApplyChange(d); }catch(e){ try{ console.warn("onumTownApplyChange", e); }catch(e2){} }
}
function caAdd(a, kind, name, cause){
  const D = caEnsure();
  const ch = {id:"dc" + (D.id++), kind, name, owner:a.name, ownerId:a.id, street:a.street, status:"planned", cause, day:G.day, since:G.day, n:kind === "church" ? rint(12, 40) : 0};
  D.changes.push(ch); caPublish(ch);
  caNews(a.name + " has staked out a lot on " + a.street + " for " + caArt(kind, name) + ". " + cause.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + cause.slice(1) + ".");
  return ch;
}
function caArt(kind, name){ return kind === "home" ? "a new house" : name; }
function caNews(t){ try{ lLog(t, "world"); }catch(e){} try{ feed(t, "world"); }catch(e){} }
function caMood(street, d){ try{ townEnsure().hh.forEach(h => { if(!street || h.street === street) h.mood = clamp(h.mood + d, 0, 100); }); }catch(e){} }
function caDay(){
  const D = caEnsure(); if(D.day === G.day) return; D.day = G.day;
  const S = caStateInfo(), tm = townMood();
  /* projects move along: staked lot, scaffolding, finished */
  D.changes.forEach(c => {
    if(c.status === "planned" && G.day - c.since >= 2 && chance(.6)){ c.status = "construction"; c.since = G.day; caPublish(c); caNews("Scaffolding is up on " + c.street + ": " + caArt(c.kind, c.name) + " for " + c.owner + "."); }
    else if(c.status === "construction" && G.day - c.since >= 3 && chance(.5)){ c.status = "built"; c.since = G.day; caPublish(c); caMood(c.street, 2);
      caNews((c.kind === "home" ? c.owner + "'s new house" : c.name) + " on " + c.street + " is finished" + (c.kind === "church" ? ", and the first service gives thanks to God Most High under Christ the King" : c.kind === "restaurant" ? " and the first plates are going out" : "") + "."); }
    else if(c.status === "built" && (c.kind === "restaurant" || c.kind === "shop")){
      const a = D.actors.find(x => x.id === c.ownerId) || {wealth:50};
      const odds = (tm < 40 ? .06 : .015) + (a.wealth < 30 ? .04 : 0);
      if(chance(odds)){ c.status = "closed"; c.since = G.day; caPublish(c); caMood(c.street, -3); caNews(c.name + " on " + c.street + " has closed. " + (tm < 40 ? "Nobody on the street is spending." : c.owner + " could not cover the rent.")); }
    } else if(c.status === "built" && c.kind === "church"){
      const g = Math.round(c.n * (0.01 + (S.creed ? .02 : 0)) + (chance(.4) ? rint(1, 4) : 0)); c.n += g;
      if(g >= 4) caNews(c.name + " on " + c.street + " has " + c.n + " in the pews on Sunday now.");
    }
  });
  caEconomy(D, S, tm);
  /* crime is reported, and people organise against it */
  if(chance(tm < 45 ? .14 : .06)){ D.crime++; const st = pick(L_STREETS); caMood(st, -2); caNews("A break-in on " + st + " was reported to the county police." + (D.crime === 4 ? " The neighbours have started a watch." : "")); if(D.crime === 4) D.watch = true; }
  caPublish(null);
}
/* ── the town economy: wages, savings, intentions, pooled civic money, taxes, eras ── */
const CA_JOBS = [["mill hand",95],["nurse",120],["teacher",110],["farmer",100],["clerk",90],["mechanic",115],["lawyer",210],["shopkeeper",130],["pastor",80],["engineer",190]];
const CA_COST = {home:5500, shop:4000, restaurant:4500, church:14000, statue:6000, park:10000, road:22000, school:30000, station:26000, lab:45000, expansion:60000};
const CA_CIVIC = ["church","park","road","school","station","expansion"];
function caEra(D){ const pop = D.actors.length * 3 + D.changes.filter(c => c.kind === "home" && c.status === "built").length * 3, w = D.bal ? D.actors.reduce((s, a) => s + Math.max(0, D.bal[a.id] || 0), 0) : 0;
  D.pop = pop; D.wealth = w; return pop >= 75 || w >= 160000 ? 3 : pop >= 48 || w >= 70000 ? 2 : 1; }
function caUnlocked(k, era){ return ({statue:2, expansion:2, lab:3, station:1, school:1, road:1, park:1})[k] ? era >= ({statue:2, expansion:2, lab:3, station:1, school:1, road:1, park:1})[k] : true; }
function caWant(a, D, era){
  const has = k => D.changes.filter(c => c.kind === k && c.status !== "closed").length;
  const W = [];
  if(!D.changes.some(c => c.ownerId === a.id && c.kind === "home")) W.push(["home", a.wealth / 50 + 0.4]);
  if(a.drive > 50 && has("restaurant") < 2 + era * 2) W.push(["restaurant", a.drive / 70]);
  if(a.drive > 40 && has("shop") < 3 + era * 2) W.push(["shop", a.drive / 80]);
  if(era >= 2 && a.wealth > 60) W.push(["statue", .5]);
  if(era >= 3 && a.science > 60 && has("lab") < era - 2) W.push(["lab", a.science / 60]);
  if(!W.length) return null;
  let r = Math.random() * W.reduce((s, w) => s + w[1], 0);
  for(const w of W){ r -= w[1]; if(r <= 0) return w[0]; }
  return W[0][0];
}
function caCivicWant(a, D, era){
  const has = k => D.changes.filter(c => c.kind === k && c.status !== "closed").length;
  if(a.faith > 65 && has("church") < 2 + era) return "church";
  if(D.crime > 5 && has("station") < 1) return "station";
  if(townEnsure().hh.some(h => /school|bus/.test(h.need)) && has("school") < era) return "school";
  if(era >= 2 && has("expansion") < era - 1) return "expansion";
  if(has("road") < era) return "road";
  return has("park") < era ? "park" : null;
}
const CA_STATUE = ["the first Consul","the founders of the town","the miners of the old shaft","the nurses of the flu winter","the veterans of the river war"];
function caName(a, k){
  const sn = surname(a.name);
  return {home:sn + " house", shop:sn + " " + pick(CA_SHOP), restaurant:sn + "'s " + pick(CA_EAT),
    statue:"Statue of " + pick(CA_STATUE) + " (a gift of the " + sn + " family)", lab:sn + " Laboratory"}[k] || k;
}
function caCivicName(k, a){
  const D = caEnsure();
  return {church:pick(CA_SAINT.filter(n => !D.changes.some(c => c.name === n))) || "New Hope Chapel", park:a.street + " Common", road:"New road past " + a.street,
    school:a.street + " School", station:a.street + " Police Station", expansion:"The " + pick(["North","East","Orchard","Mill","Ridge"]) + " blocks"}[k];
}
const CA_WHY = {home:"saved for years from a {job}'s wage and the bank finally said yes", shop:"put by every spare dollar from the {job} work to open it", restaurant:"saved up because everyone asks where to get food like their mother made",
  statue:"the family has done well and wants the town to remember", lab:"they want to test the water and the soil here instead of waiting on the state"};
const CA_CWHY = {church:"the congregation pooled its tithes to worship God Most High under Christ the King in a house of their own", park:"neighbours pooled money so the children have somewhere green",
  road:"the town and the county budget split the cost of the road the trucks need", school:"parents pooled money and the county matched it", station:"after the break-ins, the watch and the county paid for a station",
  expansion:"the town has outgrown its streets; owners pooled money for new lots and the county laid the water"};
/* ── the closed ledger ──
   One currency. Every balance lives in D.bal. Money enters only from two named sources
   (src:export, what the outside world pays for the town's mill and farm output; src:state,
   the state's grant to the county) and leaves only through two named sinks (sink:import,
   supplies and goods bought from outside; sink:build, outside crews and materials).
   Everything else is a transfer: wages from an employer's balance, purchases from a buyer's
   balance to a seller's, taxes to gov:county. caAudit() proves it. */
const CA_EXT = [{id:"emp:mill", name:"the mill", export:1}, {id:"emp:farm", name:"the farm co-op", export:1}, {id:"gov:county", name:"the county"}];
function caBal(D, id){ return D.bal[id] || 0; }
function caXfer(D, from, to, amt, why){
  amt = Math.floor(amt); if(!(amt > 0)) return 0;
  if(from.indexOf("src:") !== 0 && caBal(D, from) < amt) return 0;   /* nobody spends money they do not have */
  if(from.indexOf("src:") === 0) D.flow[from] = (D.flow[from] || 0) + amt; else D.bal[from] = caBal(D, from) - amt;
  if(to.indexOf("sink:") === 0) D.flow[to] = (D.flow[to] || 0) + amt; else D.bal[to] = caBal(D, to) + amt;
  return amt;
}
function caTotal(D){ return Object.keys(D.bal).reduce((s, k) => s + D.bal[k], 0); }
function caAudit(){
  const D = caEnsure(); const f = D.flow || {};
  const expect = (D.bal0 || 0) + (f["src:export"] || 0) + (f["src:state"] || 0) - (f["sink:import"] || 0) - (f["sink:build"] || 0);
  return {total:caTotal(D), expect, ok:caTotal(D) === expect, flow:Object.assign({}, f)};
}
function caLedgerInit(D){
  if(D.bal) return;
  D.bal = {}; D.flow = {}; D.seen = {}; D.gossip = [];
  D.actors.forEach(a => { const j = CA_JOBS[seedOf("job:" + a.id) % CA_JOBS.length]; a.job = j[0]; a.wage = j[1] + Math.round(a.wealth * 0.8); a.emp = pick(["emp:mill","emp:farm","gov:county"]); D.bal[a.id] = a.wealth * 40; });
  CA_EXT.forEach(e => D.bal[e.id] = e.id === "gov:county" ? 20000 : 15000);
  D.bal0 = caTotal(D);
}
function caNewActor(D, street){
  const a = {id:"tp" + D.actors.length + "_" + G.day, name:newName(G.you && G.you.state), street, wealth:rint(10, 80), faith:rint(5, 95), drive:rint(5, 95), science:rint(0, 90)};
  const j = CA_JOBS[seedOf("job:" + a.id) % CA_JOBS.length]; a.job = j[0]; a.wage = j[1] + Math.round(a.wealth * 0.8); a.emp = pick(["emp:mill","emp:farm"]);
  D.actors.push(a); D.bal[a.id] = 0;   /* they arrive with nothing on the books; their first wage is their start */
  return a;
}
function caSee(D, who, what, amt){
  /* wealth is private: only neighbours on the street notice a purchase, and they may talk */
  const ws = D.actors.filter(o => o !== who && o.street === who.street);
  ws.forEach(o => { if(!chance(.5)) return; const k = o.id + ">" + who.id; D.seen[k] = Math.max(D.seen[k] || 0, amt); caReact(D, o, who, what, amt); });
}
function caGossip(D, t){ D.gossip.push({d:G.day, t}); if(D.gossip.length > 60) D.gossip.shift(); try{ window.ONUM_TOWN_GOSSIP = D.gossip.slice(-30); window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("onum-town-gossip", {detail:{t}})); }catch(e){} }
function caReact(D, o, who, what, amt){
  const mine = caBal(D, o.id), r = Math.random();
  o.rel = o.rel || {};
  if(mine < amt * 0.2 && o.faith < 35 && r < .08){
    /* theft: a transfer, not new money; often caught */
    const take = Math.min(caBal(D, who.id), rint(80, 400)); if(take <= 0) return;
    caXfer(D, who.id, o.id, take, "theft");
    o.rel[who.id] = (o.rel[who.id] || 0) - 20;
    D.crime++;
    if(chance(.55)){ caXfer(D, o.id, who.id, take, "restitution"); caNews("A neighbour on " + who.street + " was caught taking money from " + who.name + "'s house and had to pay it back."); caGossip(D, o.name + " was caught stealing from " + who.name + "."); }
    else caNews("Money went missing from a house on " + who.street + ". The county police have no suspect.");
  } else if(mine < amt * 0.3 && r < .35){
    o.rel[who.id] = (o.rel[who.id] || 0) - 8; caGossip(D, o.name + " has noticed " + who.name + "'s " + what + " and says nothing kind about it.");
  } else if(mine > amt * 0.8 && r < .5){
    if(!o.intent && (what === "home" || what === "shop" || what === "restaurant")) o.intent = {k:what, cost:CA_COST[what]};
    caGossip(D, o.name + " saw " + who.name + "'s " + what + " and has started saving for one too.");
  } else if(who.faith > 70 && caBal(D, who.id) > 3000 && mine < 400 && r < .4){
    const g = caXfer(D, who.id, o.id, rint(100, 300), "gift");
    if(g){ o.rel[who.id] = (o.rel[who.id] || 0) + 15; caGossip(D, who.name + " quietly helped " + o.name + " with " + caMoney(g) + ". Only the family knows."); }
  } else if(r < .45){ caGossip(D, o.name + " wonders aloud where " + who.name + " found the money."); }
}
function caEconomy(D, S, tm){
  caLedgerInit(D);
  const era = caEra(D), prevEra = D.era || 1; D.era = era;
  if(era > prevEra) caNews("The town is growing: " + D.pop + " people, and talk of " + (era === 2 ? "statues, new blocks and a proper square" : "a laboratory and a second high street") + ".");
  D.pool = D.pool || {}; D.poolAcct = D.poolAcct || {};
  /* the outside world pays for the mill's and the farm's output: the only earned inflow */
  const workers = id => D.actors.filter(a => a.emp === id).length;
  CA_EXT.filter(e => e.export).forEach(e => caXfer(D, "src:export", e.id, workers(e.id) * 150 + rint(0, 200), "export sales"));
  /* the state grant to the county: bigger when you hold the purse */
  let grant = 600; try{ const o = heldOffice(); grant = o === "gov" ? 1400 : o === "pres" ? 1100 : 600; }catch(e){}
  caXfer(D, "src:state", "gov:county", grant, "state grant");
  const biz = D.changes.filter(c => c.status === "built" && (c.kind === "shop" || c.kind === "restaurant"));
  biz.forEach(c => { if(D.bal[c.id] == null){ D.bal[c.id] = 0; } });
  let taxed = 0;
  D.actors.forEach(a => {
    /* a shift worked, paid from the employer's own balance (or not at all) */
    const own = biz.find(c => c.ownerId === a.id);
    const emp = own ? null : (a.emp && a.emp.indexOf("biz:") === 0 ? D.changes.find(c => "biz:" + c.id === a.emp && c.status === "built") : null);
    const payer = emp ? emp.id : (a.emp && a.emp.indexOf("biz:") !== 0 ? a.emp : "emp:mill");
    if(!own){
      const paid = caXfer(D, payer, a.id, a.wage, "wage");
      if(!paid){ a.unpaid = (a.unpaid || 0) + 1; if(a.unpaid >= 3){ a.unpaid = 0; const nb = pick(biz); a.emp = nb && chance(.5) ? "biz:" + nb.id : pick(["emp:mill","emp:farm"]); } }
      else { a.unpaid = 0; const t = caXfer(D, a.id, "gov:county", Math.round(paid * 0.04), "tax"); taxed += t; }
    }
    /* daily bread and a meal: bought in town if there is a shop, otherwise from outside */
    const need = 45 + Math.round(a.wealth * 0.4);
    const shop = biz.filter(c => c.kind === "shop"), eat = biz.filter(c => c.kind === "restaurant");
    const s1 = shop.length ? pick(shop).id : "sink:import";
    caXfer(D, a.id, s1, Math.min(caBal(D, a.id), Math.round(need * 0.7)), "groceries");
    if(eat.length && chance(tm > 45 ? .45 : .2)) caXfer(D, a.id, pick(eat).id, Math.min(caBal(D, a.id), 18 + rint(0, 20)), "a meal");
    if(a.faith > 60) caXfer(D, a.id, "pool:church", Math.round(a.wage * 0.1), "tithe");
    /* personal intention, paid from real savings */
    if(!a.intent || (a.intent.k && !caUnlocked(a.intent.k, era))){ const k = caWant(a, D, era); if(k) a.intent = {k, cost:CA_COST[k]}; }
    const busy = D.changes.some(c => c.ownerId === a.id && (c.status === "planned" || c.status === "construction"));
    if(a.intent && !busy && caBal(D, a.id) >= a.intent.cost + 300){
      const k = a.intent.k, cost = a.intent.cost; a.intent = null;
      const startCap = (k === "shop" || k === "restaurant") ? Math.round(cost * 0.25) : 0;
      caXfer(D, a.id, "sink:build", cost - startCap, "building");
      const ch = caAdd(a, k, caName(a, k), (CA_WHY[k] || "they saved for it").replace("{job}", a.job));
      if(startCap){ D.bal[ch.id] = 0; caXfer(D, a.id, ch.id, startCap, "opening capital"); }
      caSee(D, a, k, cost);
    }
    /* civic money: people pay into a pool they care about */
    const ck = caCivicWant(a, D, era);
    if(ck && caBal(D, a.id) > 600) caXfer(D, a.id, "pool:" + ck, Math.round(caBal(D, a.id) * 0.004), "pledge");
  });
  /* businesses: supplies from outside cost 45% of what they took in; hire when flush, fail when broke */
  biz.forEach(c => {
    const take = c._take0 == null ? 0 : caBal(D, c.id) - c._take0;
    if(take > 0) caXfer(D, c.id, "sink:import", Math.round(take * 0.45), "supplies");
    const owner = D.actors.find(x => x.id === c.ownerId);
    if(owner && caBal(D, c.id) > 900) caXfer(D, c.id, owner.id, Math.round((caBal(D, c.id) - 600) * 0.5), "owner's draw");
    if(caBal(D, c.id) > 1500 && !D.actors.some(x => x.emp === "biz:" + c.id)){ const w = D.actors.find(x => x.emp === "emp:mill" && x.id !== c.ownerId); if(w){ w.emp = "biz:" + c.id; w.wage = 90; } }
    if(caBal(D, c.id) < 60){ c.broke = (c.broke || 0) + 1; } else c.broke = 0;
    if(c.broke >= 6){ c.status = "closed"; c.since = G.day; caPublish(c);
      D.actors.filter(x => x.emp === "biz:" + c.id).forEach(x => x.emp = pick(["emp:mill","emp:farm"]));
      if(owner) caXfer(D, c.id, owner.id, caBal(D, c.id), "closing"); caMood(c.street, -3);
      caNews(c.name + " on " + c.street + " has closed. It could not take in enough to pay for stock and staff."); }
    c._take0 = caBal(D, c.id);
  });
  /* the county adds tax money to pledges; projects go up when the money is really there */
  CA_CIVIC.forEach(k => {
    if(!caUnlocked(k, era)) return;
    const need = CA_COST[k], have = caBal(D, "pool:" + k), pub = Math.min(caBal(D, "gov:county") - 2000, Math.round(need * 0.5));
    if(have + Math.max(0, pub) >= need && !D.changes.some(c => c.kind === k && (c.status === "planned" || c.status === "construction"))){
      const fromPool = Math.min(have, need), fromCounty = need - fromPool;
      caXfer(D, "pool:" + k, "sink:build", fromPool, "civic build"); if(fromCounty > 0) caXfer(D, "gov:county", "sink:build", fromCounty, "public works");
      D.publicSpent = (D.publicSpent || 0) + fromCounty;
      const a = pick(D.actors);
      caAdd(a, k, caCivicName(k, a), CA_CWHY[k] + (fromCounty ? " (" + caMoney(fromCounty) + " from the county)" : ""));
      if(k === "expansion") for(let i = 0; i < 2; i++) caNewActor(D, "the new blocks");
    }
  });
  /* new homes bring new families */
  D.changes.filter(c => c.kind === "home" && c.status === "built" && !c.moved).forEach(c => { c.moved = true; if(chance(.4)) caNewActor(D, c.street); });
  D.taxDay = taxed; D.treasury = caBal(D, "gov:county");
}
/* what the town is doing becomes what people write about */
function caSuggest(h){
  const D = caEnsure(), S = sbEnsure();
  const c = D.changes.filter(x => G.day - x.since <= 4).slice(-6).find(x => S.used.indexOf("ca:" + x.id + ":" + x.status + ":" + h.id) < 0);
  if(!c) return null;
  S.used.push("ca:" + c.id + ":" + c.status + ":" + h.id);
  const nm = c.kind === "home" ? c.owner + "'s new house" : c.name;
  const T = {
    restaurant:c.status === "closed" ? ["Help small places like " + nm + " survive the rent", "it closed and the street is darker for it"] : ["Let " + nm + " put tables on the pavement", "it is the only place open after six"],
    shop:c.status === "closed" ? ["Look into why " + nm + " had to close", "we drive to the county seat for everything again"] : ["Fix the parking by " + nm, "the delivery trucks block the road"],
    church:["Let " + nm + " run the food pantry out of its hall", "the congregation is growing and so is the need"],
    school:["Give " + nm + " a proper library", "the parents built it and the county still owes the books"],
    lab:["Have the state use " + nm + "'s water tests", "they found something in the well water and nobody listened"],
    station:["Staff " + nm + " at night", "the watch can report crimes but cannot stop them"],
    home:["Speed up building permits on " + c.street, "it took " + c.owner + " months to get one"]
  }[c.kind];
  return T ? {what:T[0], why:T[1], k:"town-" + c.kind} : null;
}

/* ── families ── */
const FAM_REL = {spouse:"spouse", kid:"child", parent:"parent", sib:"sibling", inlaw:"in-law", cousin:"cousin"};
function famEnsure(){
  if(!G.fam) G.fam = {p:{}, links:[], events:[], day:0};
  const F = G.fam;
  everyone().forEach(m => { if(m && !m.gone && !F.p[m.id]) F.p[m.id] = famMake(m); });
  if(!F.linked){ F.linked = true; famLink(); }
  return F;
}
function famMake(m){
  const r = rng(seedOf("fam:" + m.id + ":" + (m.name || ""))), last = surname(m.name || "Doe");
  const firstOf = () => { const n = newName(m.state); return n.split(" ")[0]; };
  const f = {name:m.name, spouse:null, kids:[], parents:[], sibs:[], kin:[], feuds:[]};
  if(r() < .72) f.spouse = {name:firstOf() + " " + last, job:pick(["teacher","nurse","lawyer","farmer","pastor's assistant","engineer","shop owner"])};
  const nk = f.spouse ? Math.floor(r() * 4) : (r() < .2 ? 1 : 0);
  for(let i = 0; i < nk; i++) f.kids.push({name:firstOf(), age:1 + Math.floor(r() * 24)});
  if(r() < .6) f.parents.push({name:firstOf() + " " + last, alive:r() < .7});
  const ns = Math.floor(r() * 3); for(let i = 0; i < ns; i++) f.sibs.push({name:firstOf() + " " + last});
  return f;
}
/* some families are joined: siblings in the same delegation, a daughter married into another house, cousins */
function famLink(){
  const F = G.fam, ms = G.members.filter(m => !m.gone && m.kind !== "civ");
  for(let i = 0; i < ms.length; i++) for(let j = i + 1; j < ms.length; j++){
    const a = ms[i], b = ms[j]; if(a === G.you || b === G.you) continue;
    const s = seedOf("lk:" + a.id + ":" + b.id) % 1000;
    const rel = a.state === b.state ? (s < 35 ? "cousin" : s < 55 ? "inlaw" : s < 62 ? "sib" : null) : (s < 6 ? "inlaw" : s < 9 ? "cousin" : null);
    if(!rel) continue;
    F.links.push({a:a.id, b:b.id, rel, feud:(s % 7) === 0});
    F.p[a.id].kin.push({id:b.id, rel}); F.p[b.id].kin.push({id:a.id, rel});
    if((s % 7) === 0){ F.p[a.id].feuds.push(b.id); F.p[b.id].feuds.push(a.id); }
  }
  famPublish();
}
function famKin(a, b){ const F = G.fam; if(!F) return null; return F.links.find(l => (l.a === a.id && l.b === b.id) || (l.a === b.id && l.b === a.id)) || null; }
/* blood is thicker than party, unless there is a feud, which is worse than party */
function famTieBias(a, b){ try{ famEnsure(); const l = famKin(a, b); if(!l) return 0; return l.feud ? -26 : l.rel === "sib" ? 30 : l.rel === "cousin" ? 14 : 18; }catch(e){ return 0; } }
function famPublish(){
  const F = G.fam; if(!F) return;
  const by = {};
  Object.keys(F.p).forEach(id => { const f = F.p[id]; by[id] = {name:f.name, spouse:f.spouse, kids:f.kids, parents:f.parents, siblings:f.sibs,
    inlaws:f.kin.filter(k => k.rel === "inlaw").map(k => ({id:k.id, name:(byId(k.id) || {}).name})), kin:f.kin.map(k => ({id:k.id, rel:FAM_REL[k.rel] || k.rel, name:(byId(k.id) || {}).name})), feuds:f.feuds.map(id => ({id, name:(byId(id) || {}).name}))}; });
  window.ONUM_FAMILIES = {byId:by, links:F.links.map(l => Object.assign({}, l, {rel:FAM_REL[l.rel] || l.rel})), events:F.events.slice(-40)};
  try{ window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("onum-families", {detail:{events:F.events.length}})); }catch(e){}
}
/* weddings, births, funerals, graduations: the Ledger prints them; the corridor talks */
function famDay(){
  const F = famEnsure(); if(F.day === G.day) return; F.day = G.day;
  const ms = G.members.filter(m => !m.gone && m !== G.you && m.kind !== "civ");
  const n = chance(.6) ? 1 : 0 + (chance(.25) ? 1 : 0);
  for(let i = 0; i < n; i++){
    const m = pick(ms); if(!m) break; const f = F.p[m.id]; if(!f) continue;
    const kids = f.kids.filter(k => k.age >= 22), sn = surname(m.name);
    let ev = null;
    const roll = Math.random();
    if(roll < .3 && kids.length){ const k = pick(kids); ev = {k:"wedding", t:k.name + " " + sn + ", " + m.name + "'s " + (k.age > 0 ? "child" : "") + ", was married on Saturday"}; k.married = true; }
    else if(roll < .55 && f.spouse && f.kids.length < 5){ const nm = newName(m.state).split(" ")[0]; f.kids.push({name:nm, age:0}); ev = {k:"birth", t:m.name + " and " + f.spouse.name.split(" ")[0] + " welcomed a baby, " + nm}; }
    else if(roll < .7 && f.parents.some(p => p.alive)){ const p = f.parents.find(p => p.alive); p.alive = false; ev = {k:"funeral", t:m.name + " buried " + p.name + " this week. The service committed the soul to God, under Christ the King"}; }
    else if(f.kids.some(k => k.age >= 17 && k.age <= 22 && !k.grad)){ const k = f.kids.find(k => k.age >= 17 && k.age <= 22 && !k.grad); k.grad = true; ev = {k:"graduation", t:k.name + " " + sn + " graduated, and " + m.name + " has told everyone twice"}; }
    if(!ev) continue;
    ev.id = m.id; ev.d = G.day; F.events.push(ev); if(F.events.length > 80) F.events.shift();
    caNews(ev.t + ".");
    /* kin rally; feuding kin notice who sent flowers */
    f.kin.forEach(k => { const o = byId(k.id); if(!o) return; if(f.feuds.indexOf(k.id) >= 0){ if(ev.k === "funeral" && chance(.5)){ tieShift(m, o, 6, "came to the funeral despite everything"); caNews(o.name + " came to the funeral. The family feud may be thawing."); } } else tieShift(m, o, 2, "family " + ev.k); });
    try{ remember(m, ev.k === "funeral" ? "Buried a parent; noticed who wrote" : "A family " + ev.k, 4); }catch(e){}
    m._famNews = {k:ev.k, d:G.day};
  }
  famPublish();
}
/* what they say to each other (and to you) about family */
function famLine(m, to){
  try{
    const F = famEnsure(); if(!to || to === G.you) to = null;
    const tf = to ? F.p[to.id] : null, mf = F.p[m.id];
    if(to && to._famNews && G.day - to._famNews.d <= 5){ const k = to._famNews.k;
      return k === "funeral" ? "I was sorry to hear about your loss." : k === "birth" ? "Congratulations on the baby." : k === "wedding" ? "How was the wedding?" : "I hear there is a graduate in the family."; }
    if(to){ const l = famKin(m, to); if(l && l.feud) return pick(["Give my regards to nobody at home.","Tell your mother I said nothing."]); if(l) return pick(["Sunday dinner at Mother's, you remember.","How is the family?","Aunt Ruth asks after you."]); }
    if(tf && tf.spouse && chance(.5)) return "How is " + tf.spouse.name.split(" ")[0] + "?";
    if(tf && tf.kids.length) { const k = pick(tf.kids); return "Is " + k.name + " " + (k.age < 6 ? "sleeping through yet?" : k.age < 18 ? "still at school?" : "still in the city?"); }
    if(!to && mf && mf.kids.length && chance(.5)) return "My " + (pick(mf.kids).age < 12 ? "youngest" : "eldest") + " wants to see the Capitol.";
  }catch(e){}
  return null;
}
function caHTML(){
  const D = caEnsure(), out = ['<div class="pc__h">The district, on its own</div><div class="pc__m">What people at home are building, opening, closing and asking for. Nobody asked permission.</div>'];
  const L = D.changes.slice(-14).reverse();
  if(!L.length) out.push('<p class="pc__m">Nothing new on the streets yet.</p>');
  L.forEach(c => out.push('<div>· <b>' + pcEsc(c.kind === "home" ? c.owner + "'s house" : c.name) + '</b>, ' + pcEsc(c.street) + ' · ' + pcEsc(c.status) + (c.kind === "church" && c.status === "built" ? ' · ' + c.n + ' in the pews' : '') + ' <span class="pc__m">(' + pcEsc(c.cause) + ')</span></div>'));
  out.push('<div class="pc__m">Town: about ' + (D.pop || 0) + ' people · era ' + (D.era || 1) + ' · county taxes ' + caMoney(D.taxDay || 0) + '/day · county treasury ' + caMoney(D.treasury || 0) + ' · spent on public works ' + caMoney(D.publicSpent || 0) + '</div>');
  (D.gossip || []).slice(-4).reverse().forEach(g => out.push('<div class="pc__m">Heard on the street: ' + pcEsc(g.t) + '</div>'));
  const intents = D.actors.filter(a => a.intent).slice(0, 5).map(a => pcEsc(a.name) + ' is said to be saving for a ' + pcEsc(a.intent.k));
  if(intents.length) out.push('<div class="pc__m">' + intents.join(' · ') + '</div>');
  if(D.crime) out.push('<div class="pc__m">Crimes reported this term: ' + D.crime + (D.watch ? ' · a neighbourhood watch is out' : '') + '</div>');
  return out.join("");
}
/* placeholder only when Neptune's town builder is absent: a staked post and a small sign per change */
(function(){
  if(typeof buildRoom !== "function") return;
  const _b = buildRoom;
  buildRoom = function(id){
    const r = _b.apply(this, arguments);
    try{ if(id === "district" && typeof window.onumTownApplyChange !== "function" && G.dist){
      const wood = matCol(0x7a5a3a, .8), R = ROOMS.district, hw = (R && R.w / 2) || 8;
      G.dist.changes.filter(c => c.status !== "closed").slice(-6).forEach((c, i) => {
        const x = (i % 2 ? 1 : -1) * (hw - 1.2), z = 3 + Math.floor(i / 2) * 2.4;
        addBox(wood, x, 0.6, z, 0.08, 1.2, 0.08, 0);
        try{ propPlaque(x, 1.3, z, i % 2 ? -Math.PI / 2 : Math.PI / 2, (c.kind === "home" ? c.owner + "'s" : c.name).slice(0, 22).toUpperCase(), c.status, 0.7, 0.16); }catch(e){}
      }); } }catch(e){}
    return r;
  };
})();

/* ══ 20261013a · THE DOCKET AT EVERY DECISION ══
   Any card or bar that asks you to decide something about a bill, act, state bill or case
   now carries the docket inline: the clauses (strike or amend them), sponsor, cost and
   effects, how the House, the Senate and your state legislature lean by party, how your
   state and the nation lean, who outside the building is for or against, and the powers you
   actually hold over it: amend, strike, whip, delay, table, vote, sign, veto, rule. */
function dkAllItems(){
  const L = []; try{ dkItems().forEach(x => L.push(x)); }catch(e){}
  const y = G.you || {}, S = (G.states || []).find(s => s.n === y.state);
  const have = t => L.some(x => x.title === t);
  if(G.bill && !G.bill.done && !have(G.bill.title)) L.push({kind:"bill", title:G.bill.title, line:G.bill.line, sponsor:G.bill.sponsor, pos:G.bill.pos, b:G.bill, body:"The House", acts:[]});
  if(S && S.bill && !have(S.bill.title)) L.push({kind:"state bill", title:S.bill.title, line:"Before the legislature of " + y.state + ".", sponsor:"the " + y.state + " majority leader", sb:S.bill, body:"The " + y.state + " legislature", acts:[]});
  (G.lastActs || []).filter(a => !a.signed && !a.vetoed && !have(a.title)).slice(0, 2).forEach(a => L.push({kind:"act", title:a.title, line:"Passed on day " + a.day + ".", sponsor:"Congress", pos:a.pos || {econ:0, lib:0, def:0, wel:0}, national:true, acts:[]}));
  (G.cases || []).filter(c => !c.done && !have(c.t)).slice(0, 2).forEach(c => L.push({kind:"case", title:c.t, line:c.dual != null ? "Can one person hold two elected offices?" : "A state against the Union over a federal act.", sponsor:"filed day " + c.day, c, sb:{dir:1, axis:"lib"}, acts:[]}));
  return L;
}
function dkFind(text){
  const L = dkAllItems(); if(!L.length) return null;
  const t = String(text || "");
  const hit = L.find(x => x.title && t.indexOf(x.title) >= 0);
  if(hit) return hit;
  if(/\b(bill|act|vote|roll|veto|sign|ruling|rule for|case|legislat|amend|clause|passage|floor|whip|motion|appropriat|statute)\b/i.test(t)) return L[0];
  return null;
}
function dkBillRef(it){ return it.b || it.sb || (it.c ? it.c : null) || G.bill; }
function dkCostLine(it){
  const r = rng(seedOf("cost:" + it.title)), b = dkBillRef(it) || {};
  if(it.kind === "case") return "No appropriation. A ruling binds every state from the day it is read.";
  const st = b.struck || [];
  if(st.indexOf(3) >= 0) return "Cost: struck. The funding section was removed; the act would be unfunded.";
  const n = Math.round(40 + r() * 900);
  return "Cost: about " + (CURRENCY ? CURRENCY.sym : "$") + n + " million over six years" + (b.soft ? ", reduced by your amendment to about " + (CURRENCY ? CURRENCY.sym : "$") + Math.round(n * 0.7) + " million" : "") + ".";
}
function dkEffects(it){
  const out = [];
  const pos = it.pos || (it.sb ? {[it.sb.axis || "econ"]:(it.sb.dir || 1) * 40} : null);
  if(pos) try{ AXES.forEach(a => { const v = (pos[a.k] || 0) * ((dkBillRef(it) || {}).soft ? 0.6 : 1); if(Math.abs(v) >= 8) out.push((Math.abs(v) > 30 ? "Strongly" : "Somewhat") + " toward " + (v > 0 ? a.hi : a.lo)); }); }catch(e){}
  if(it.sb){ if(it.sb.ind) out.push("Industry in your state " + (it.sb.ind > 0 ? "up" : "down") + " " + Math.abs(it.sb.ind)); if(it.sb.unrest) out.push("Unrest " + (it.sb.unrest > 0 ? "up" : "down") + " " + Math.abs(it.sb.unrest)); }
  return out.length ? out : ["Little change either way; mostly a matter of who gets credit"];
}
const DK_STAKE = {econ:[["The Chamber of Commerce", 1], ["The union delegates", -1], ["The farm co-ops", 0.5]], lib:[["The civil liberties union", 1], ["The Consular Guard", -1], ["The bar association", 0.6]],
  def:[["The veterans' league", 1], ["The peace churches", -1], ["The shipyards", 0.8]], wel:[["The county hospitals", 1], ["The taxpayers' league", -1], ["The parishes' charities", 0.7]]};
function dkStakeholders(it){
  const pos = it.pos || (it.sb ? {[it.sb.axis || "econ"]:(it.sb.dir || 1) * 40} : {lib:30});
  const rows = [];
  Object.keys(DK_STAKE).forEach(k => { const v = pos[k] || 0; if(Math.abs(v) < 6) return; DK_STAKE[k].forEach(([n, s]) => { const lean = v * s; const why = DK_WHY[n] || []; rows.push([n, Math.abs(lean) < 8 ? "watching" : lean > 0 ? "for it" : "against it", Math.abs(lean) < 8 ? "it does not touch what they care about much" : why[lean > 0 ? 0 : 1] || ""]); }); });
  let aye = 0, nay = 0; try{ const by = dkCongress(it); Object.keys(by).forEach(p => { const o = by[p]; aye += o.aye; nay += o.no; rows.push(["Party: " + p, o.aye > o.no ? "mostly for" : o.no > o.aye ? "mostly against" : "split", "the party's members lean the same way on it"]); }); }catch(e){}
  rows.push(["The Wire (news)", aye >= nay ? "runs it as a win for the bill" : "runs it as a defeat for the bill", "the press reports what the House does and what it means to voters"]);
  return rows.slice(0, 11);
}
function dkSum(by){ let a = 0, n = 0, u = 0; Object.keys(by || {}).forEach(p => { a += by[p].aye; n += by[p].no; u += by[p].un; }); return {a, n, u}; }
function dkChambers(it){
  const y = G.you || {}, S = (G.states || []).find(s => s.n === y.state);
  const rows = [];
  const add = (name, by, why) => { const s = dkSum(by); rows.push('<div class="whip__row"><span class="whip__n">' + pcEsc(name) + '</span><span class="whip__w">aye ' + s.a + ' · nay ' + s.n + ' · undecided ' + s.u + ' · ' + Object.keys(by).map(p => pcEsc(p) + ' ' + by[p].aye + '/' + by[p].no + '/' + by[p].un).join(", ") + (why ? '<br><span class="muted">' + pcEsc(why) + '</span>' : '') + '</span></div>'); };
  if(it.kind === "case"){ try{ (it.benchRows || dkBench(it.c)).forEach(r => rows.push('<div class="whip__row"><span class="whip__n">' + pcEsc(r.name) + '</span><span class="whip__w">' + pcEsc(r.v) + '</span></div>')); }catch(e){} return rows.join(""); }
  try{ add("The House", dkCongress(it), "Party lines decide most seats. The undecided are members whose districts split on it."); }catch(e){}
  try{ add("The Senate", dkSenate(it), "Each state sends two senators. They follow their state's lean and their party."); }catch(e){}
  try{ if(S) add("The " + y.state + " legislature", dkStatehouse(S, it), "Your state's lawmakers vote on what their own voters want and what the bill does to their state."); }catch(e){}
  return rows.join("");
}
/* the powers you hold over it, by chair */
function dkPowers(it){
  const y = G.you, b = dkBillRef(it), k = (function(){ try{ return roleKey(); }catch(e){ return "house"; } })(), P = [];
  if(!b || it.kind === "case") return P;
  if(it.kind === "bill" && G.bill === b){
    P.push({t:"Whip the undecided", run(){ try{ veil.hidden = true; whipCard(); }catch(e){} return ""; }, keep:true});
    P.push({t:"Lobby: push it (" + (b.mine ? "your bill" : "spend influence") + ")", off:(y.influence || 0) < 4, run(){ try{ lobbyPush(b); }catch(e){ y.influence = clamp((y.influence || 0) - 4, 0, 100); } feed("You work the corridor on the " + b.title + "."); return "You spend the afternoon on it. A few undecided members take your calls."; }});
    P.push({t:"Ask for a delay (2 days · 5 infl)", off:(y.influence || 0) < 5 || b.delayed, run(){ y.influence -= 5; b.voteDay = (b.voteDay || G.day) + 2; b.delayed = true; feed("The vote on the " + b.title + " slips two days."); return "The leadership agrees to hold it two days. Roll call day " + b.voteDay + "."; }});
    if(k === "leader" || k === "chair" || y.post) P.push({t:"Move to table it", off:b.tabled, run(){ b.voteDay = (b.voteDay || G.day) + 7; b.tabled = true; y.standing = clamp(y.standing - 3, 0, 100); feed("The " + b.title + " is laid on the table for a week."); return "Tabled for a week. The sponsor will remember."; }});
  }
  if(it.kind === "state bill"){
    P.push({t:"Send it back with amendments", off:b.sentBack, run(){ b.sentBack = true; b.dir = (b.dir || 1) * 0.6; b.soft = true; feed("The Governor sends the " + b.title + " back with amendments."); return "Returned to the legislature, softened. It will come back to your desk."; }});
    P.push({t:"Lobby the legislature for it", off:(y.influence || 0) < 4, run(){ y.influence -= 4; b.pushed = (b.pushed || 0) + 10; return "You call the committee chairs. A few undecided move."; }});
  }
  return P;
}
function dkClauseHTML(it){
  const b = dkBillRef(it) || {}, st = b.struck || [], canEdit = it.kind !== "case" && it.kind !== "act";
  return dkProvisions(it).map((x, i) => '<li' + (st.indexOf(i) >= 0 ? ' style="text-decoration:line-through;opacity:.6"' : '') + '>' + pcEsc(x) +
    (canEdit && i >= 2 && st.indexOf(i) < 0 ? ' <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost" style="padding:1px 6px;font-size:11px" data-dkstrike="' + i + '">Strike</button>' : '') +
    (canEdit && i === 3 && !b.soft ? ' <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost" style="padding:1px 6px;font-size:11px" data-dkamend="1">Amend: cut the cost</button>' : '') + '</li>').join("");
}
function dkStrike(it, i){
  const b = dkBillRef(it); if(!b) return "";
  b.struck = b.struck || []; if(b.struck.indexOf(i) < 0) b.struck.push(i);
  if(i === 2){ if(b.pos) Object.keys(b.pos).forEach(k => b.pos[k] = Math.round(b.pos[k] * 0.5)); if(b.dir) b.dir *= 0.5; }
  if(i === 3 && b.pos) b.pos.econ = Math.round((b.pos.econ || 0) * 0.8);
  G.you.influence = clamp((G.you.influence || 0) - 2, 0, 100);
  try{ feed("You move to strike section " + i + " of the " + it.title + "."); }catch(e){}
  return "Section " + i + " struck in your copy and offered as an amendment. The count moves.";
}
function dkAmend(it){
  const b = dkBillRef(it); if(!b) return ""; b.soft = true;
  if(b.pos) Object.keys(b.pos).forEach(k => b.pos[k] = Math.round(b.pos[k] * 0.8)); if(b.dir) b.dir *= 0.8;
  try{ feed("Your amendment trims the cost of the " + it.title + "."); }catch(e){}
  return "Amendment offered: the appropriation is cut by a third. Fiscal hawks soften; the sponsor sulks.";
}
/* ── what the shifts mean, in full, and how different people read the same text ── */
const DK_AXIS = {
  econ:{n:"Public / Market", scale:"At −100 the state owns, plans and pays for nearly everything; at +100 private firms and prices decide nearly everything.",
    lo:{w:"Public", does:"keeps services and industries in public hands: public works paid from the treasury, price ceilings on bread and rent, state ownership or licensing of utilities, a bigger payroll of public employees",
      gain:"wage earners, tenants, towns that depend on a public road, ferry or water main, and anyone who was priced out by a private monopoly", lose:"investors, large employers, and firms that profit from contracts the state would now keep for itself",
      state:"Public choices tend to lower unrest first and industry later: the road is built before the factory pays for it."},
    hi:{w:"Market", does:"cuts tariffs and licences, deregulates, lowers business taxes, and hands services to private contractors so prices, not ministries, decide who gets what",
      gain:"employers, exporters, merchants and anyone who wants fewer forms between them and a deal", lose:"wage earners in thin labour markets, small towns the contractors will not serve, and people who relied on a price ceiling",
      state:"Market choices tend to raise industry quickly and unrest slowly: output climbs, and the counties that do not share in it get angry later."}},
  lib:{n:"Order / Liberty", scale:"At −100 the state may search, license, register and restrain to keep order; at +100 the person is presumed free unless a court says otherwise.",
    lo:{w:"Order", does:"adds licensing, registration, curfews, search powers, and penalties for assembly or speech the authorities call disorderly",
      gain:"anyone afraid of the street: shopkeepers, the constabulary, and people who have been robbed or threatened", lose:"organizers, the press, minorities, and any dissenter whose meeting needs a permit",
      state:"More order lowers street trouble at once and builds resentment slowly, which comes out at the next election or the next riot."},
    hi:{w:"Liberty", does:"limits searches and registration, repeals licences, protects assembly, press and worship, and makes the state prove its case in front of a judge",
      gain:"the press, dissenters, minorities, worshippers outside the main rite, and anyone who has been stopped without a reason", lose:"the constabulary, anyone who wants a firm hand on disorder, and those who fear what unlicensed meetings plan",
      state:"More liberty raises noise first and trust later; the streets are louder and the courts are busier."}},
  def:{n:"Dove / Hawk", scale:"At −100 the Republic prefers treaties, arms limits and aid; at +100 it prefers ships, garrisons and a visible readiness to use them.",
    lo:{w:"Dove", does:"reduces fleet and garrison funding, favours treaties, arms limits and aid to neighbours, and spends the savings at home",
      gain:"taxpayers, the peace churches, families whose sons would be sent, and neighbours who would rather trade", lose:"the shipyards, the veterans' league, border counties that live on the garrison, and anyone who believes weakness is invited",
      state:"Doves tend to save money and invite testing; if a neighbour such as the Reach or the Horn League reads it as weakness, the frontier gets more dangerous."},
    hi:{w:"Hawk", does:"funds ships and garrisons, sets forward bases and levies, and tells neighbours in advance what the Republic will not tolerate",
      gain:"the shipyards, the veterans' league, frontier towns, and anyone who wants a neighbour to think twice", lose:"taxpayers, the peace churches, and families whose sons would be called up",
      state:"Hawks tend to cost more and deter more; a neighbour that sees a new base on its strait or frontier may answer with its own."}},
  wel:{n:"Thrift / Provision", scale:"At −100 the state spends little and asks people to rely on family, parish and wages; at +100 it provides pensions, hospitals, relief and schooling as of right.",
    lo:{w:"Thrift", does:"balances the budget, tests who qualifies for relief, trims pensions and hospital grants, and lowers the taxes that paid for them",
      gain:"taxpayers, savers, and employers who pay the levies", lose:"the old, the sick, the unemployed, and the parishes whose charity would be asked to cover what the state stopped paying",
      state:"Thrift lowers the bill now and raises hardship later; the counties with weak parishes feel it first."},
    hi:{w:"Provision", does:"pays pensions and arrears, funds county hospitals and school meals, and raises taxes or borrows to do it",
      gain:"the old, the sick, the poor, hospitals and parishes, and the widow whose pension letter came back unopened", lose:"taxpayers and savers, and anyone who thinks the money should be earned rather than given",
      state:"Provision lowers hardship and unrest and raises the bill; if the treasury runs short, the cost comes back as a levy or a cut elsewhere."}}
};
function dkStr(v){ const a = Math.abs(v); return a < 8 ? "barely" : a < 20 ? "mildly" : a < 40 ? "clearly" : "strongly"; }
function dkPos(it){ return it.pos || (it.sb ? {[it.sb.axis || "econ"]:(it.sb.dir || 1) * 40} : null); }
function dkShiftsHTML(it){
  const pos = dkPos(it); if(!pos) return "";
  const b = dkBillRef(it) || {}, soft = b.soft ? 0.6 : 1;
  const rows = AXES.map(a => {
    const v = Math.round((pos[a.k] || 0) * soft), X = DK_AXIS[a.k]; if(!X) return "";
    if(Math.abs(v) < 8) return '<div style="margin:8px 0"><b>' + pcEsc(X.n) + ': ' + (v > 0 ? "+" : "") + v + '.</b> <span class="muted">The bill barely touches this. ' + pcEsc(X.scale) + '</span></div>';
    const P = v > 0 ? X.hi : X.lo, Q = v > 0 ? X.lo : X.hi;
    return '<div style="margin:10px 0;padding-top:8px;border-top:1px solid var(--line-soft)"><b>' + pcEsc(X.n) + ': ' + (v > 0 ? "+" : "") + v + '</b> — ' + dkStr(v) + ' toward <b>' + pcEsc(P.w) + '</b>.' +
      '<div class="muted" style="font-size:12px;margin:2px 0">' + pcEsc(X.scale) + '</div>' +
      '<div><b>What that means in this bill.</b> Pushing toward ' + pcEsc(P.w.toLowerCase()) + ' means it ' + pcEsc(P.does) + '.</div>' +
      '<div><b>Who gains.</b> ' + pcEsc(cap(P.gain)) + '.</div><div><b>Who loses.</b> ' + pcEsc(cap(P.lose)) + '.</div>' +
      '<div><b>Why it reaches you and your state.</b> ' + pcEsc(P.state) + ' The opposite pole, ' + pcEsc(Q.w.toLowerCase()) + ', would have done the reverse.</div></div>';
  }).join("");
  return '<div class="eyebrow" style="margin-top:10px">What the shifts mean</div><div class="muted" style="font-size:12px">Each axis runs from −100 to +100. The number is how far this text pushes the country along it; the words say what that pushing is made of.</div>' + rows;
}
/* how a person actually reads it: their own belief on the axis it touches most, their life, and their own temper */
const DK_READ = {
  econ:{1:{for:["it lets the people who build things keep more of what they build", "it takes the state's hand off honest business"], against:["it gives the market what the public used to hold", "it will leave the towns the contractors do not serve with nothing"]},
        "-1":{for:["it keeps the roads, water and bread in public hands where somebody can be held to account", "a price the state sets cannot be raised by a stranger"], against:["it puts the state in the business of running things it will run badly", "it spends public money on what a private firm would do cheaper"]}},
  lib:{1:{for:["a person should be presumed free until a judge says otherwise", "every licence is a favour somebody can withdraw"], against:["it takes the brake off people who should not be unwatched", "the street needs a firm hand more than it needs another right"]},
       "-1":{for:["a country that cannot keep order cannot keep anything else", "people want to walk home safely more than they want a debate"], against:["it hands the authorities powers they will not give back", "order bought with other people's freedom is rent, not ownership"]}},
  def:{1:{for:["a neighbour thinks twice about a country with ships", "the frontier has to be visibly defended"], against:["it spends the Republic's sons and savings on a quarrel it could settle at a table", "a new base invites the answer it is meant to prevent"]},
       "-1":{for:["every guinea on a fleet is a guinea not spent on a school", "treaties have kept more peace than garrisons"], against:["it tells every neighbour the Republic will not fight, and they will test that", "the frontier towns will pay for this with their own safety"]}},
  wel:{1:{for:["a pension owed is a pension paid, whatever the ledger says", "a country that cannot look after its old and sick has no business calling itself a country"], against:["it spends what the Republic has not earned", "it will crowd out the parishes and the families that do this better"]},
       "-1":{for:["the budget has to balance or the next generation pays", "relief should go to those who need it, not those who ask"], against:["it cuts what the old and sick rely on to save a sum nobody will miss", "the parish cannot carry what the state drops"]}}
};
function dkReadersHTML(it){
  try{
    const pos = dkPos(it); if(!pos) return "";
    const bill = {pos:{econ:pos.econ || 0, lib:pos.lib || 0, def:pos.def || 0, wel:pos.wel || 0}, title:it.title, id:"dk:" + it.title};
    const pool = (G.members || []).filter(m => m && !m.gone && m.pos && m.kind === "member");
    const r = rng(seedOf("readers:" + it.title)), picked = [], used = {};
    pool.slice().sort(() => r() - 0.5).forEach(m => { if(picked.length >= 6) return; if(used[m.bg] || used[m.name]) return; used[m.bg] = used[m.name] = 1; picked.push(m); });
    const rows = picked.map(m => {
      let sc = 0; try{ sc = alignment(m, bill); }catch(e){ sc = alignmentRaw(m, bill); }
      const gaps = AXES.map(a => ({a, g:Math.abs((m.pos[a.k] || 0) - (bill.pos[a.k] || 0)), w:Math.abs(bill.pos[a.k] || 0), dir:(bill.pos[a.k] || 0) > 0 ? 1 : -1})).filter(x => x.w >= 8);
      let dom = null, stance = sc > 8 ? "for" : sc < -8 ? "against" : "undecided";
      if(gaps.length){ dom = stance === "for" ? gaps.slice().sort((x, y2) => x.g - y2.g)[0] : gaps.slice().sort((x, y2) => y2.g - x.g)[0]; }
      const rd = dom && DK_READ[dom.a.k] && DK_READ[dom.a.k][dom.dir], why = rd ? rd[stance === "undecided" ? (sc >= 0 ? "for" : "against") : stance] : null;
      const rr = rng(seedOf("rd:" + m.id + it.title)), pickW = why ? why[Math.floor(rr() * why.length)] : "nothing in it touches what they care about";
      const cr = creedOf(m.creed), og = originOf(m.origin), t = m.traits || {};
      const life = [cap(m.bg) + " from " + m.state + ", of " + cr.t,
        og.grievance ? "their people carry the grievance " + og.grievance : "",
        (t.conviction || 0) > 70 ? "not a person who is moved easily" : (t.loyalty || 0) > 72 ? "inclined to follow the party line when it is close" : (t.ambition || 0) > 75 ? "counting what this does for the next race" : (t.discretion || 0) > 75 ? "keeps their own counsel until the roll" : ""].filter(Boolean);
      const verdict = stance === "for" ? "leans aye" : stance === "against" ? "leans nay" : "undecided";
      return '<div style="margin:8px 0;padding:6px 8px;border-left:3px solid ' + (stance === "for" ? "#2f6b3a" : stance === "against" ? "#8a2f36" : "#888") + ';background:rgba(128,128,128,.07)"><b>' + pcEsc(m.name) + '</b> <span class="muted">(' + pcEsc(m.party) + ') · ' + verdict + '</span>' +
        '<div style="font-size:13px">' + (stance === "undecided" ? "Torn: " : stance === "for" ? "Reads it as a good thing: " : "Reads it as a bad thing: ") + pcEsc(pickW) + (dom ? ' (on ' + pcEsc(dom.a.n) + ', where they sit at ' + Math.round(m.pos[dom.a.k] || 0) + ' and the bill at ' + Math.round(bill.pos[dom.a.k] || 0) + ')' : '') + '.</div>' +
        '<div class="muted" style="font-size:12px">' + pcEsc(life.join("; ")) + '.</div></div>';
    }).join("");
    let you = "";
    try{ const y = G.you, yp = y && y.pos; if(yp){ const sc = alignment({pos:yp}, bill); const parts = AXES.filter(a => Math.abs(bill.pos[a.k] || 0) >= 8).map(a => pcEsc(DK_AXIS[a.k].n) + ": you " + Math.round(yp[a.k] || 0) + ", the bill " + Math.round(bill.pos[a.k] || 0)); you = '<div style="margin:8px 0;padding:6px 8px;border-left:3px solid #c9a24a;background:rgba(201,162,74,.1)"><b>You</b> — by your own answers, this text sits ' + (sc > 8 ? "close to" : sc < -8 ? "far from" : "somewhere between") + ' where you stand. <span class="muted">(' + parts.join("; ") + ')</span><div class="muted" style="font-size:12px">That is only your own record. Read the clauses above, weigh who gains and who loses, and decide it for yourself.</div></div>'; } }catch(e){}
    return '<div class="eyebrow" style="margin-top:10px">How different people read the same text</div><div class="muted" style="font-size:12px">Nobody here was told what to think of it. Each of them reads it through their own beliefs, their own trade and place, and their own temper, so they agree only where those happen to point the same way.</div>' + you + rows;
  }catch(e){ return ""; }
}
function dkExtraHTML(it){
  return dkShiftsHTML(it) + '<div class="eyebrow" style="margin-top:8px">Cost and effects</div><div class="muted">' + pcEsc(dkCostLine(it)) + '</div><ul style="margin:4px 0 0 18px">' + dkEffects(it).map(e => '<li>' + pcEsc(e) + '</li>').join("") + '</ul>' +
    '<div class="eyebrow" style="margin-top:8px">Every chamber</div>' + dkChambers(it) +
    '<div class="eyebrow" style="margin-top:8px">Who is for and against, and why</div>' + dkStakeholders(it).map(r => '<div class="whip__row"><span class="whip__n">' + pcEsc(r[0]) + '</span><span class="whip__w">' + pcEsc(r[1]) + (r[2] ? ' — ' + pcEsc(r[2]) : '') + '</span></div>').join("") + dkReadersHTML(it);
}
function dkWire(root, it, sayEl, rerender){
  const said = r => { dkWire._last = r; const s2 = card.querySelector(".dk-say") || el("dkSay") || sayEl; if(s2) s2.textContent = r; };
  root.querySelectorAll("[data-dkstrike]").forEach(b => b.onclick = ev => { ev.stopPropagation(); const r = dkStrike(it, +b.getAttribute("data-dkstrike")); rerender && rerender(); said(r); });
  root.querySelectorAll("[data-dkamend]").forEach(b => b.onclick = ev => { ev.stopPropagation(); const r = dkAmend(it); rerender && rerender(); said(r); });
  root.querySelectorAll("[data-dkpow]").forEach(b => b.onclick = ev => { ev.stopPropagation(); const p = dkPowers(it)[+b.getAttribute("data-dkpow")]; if(!p) return; const r = p.run(); if(sayEl && r) sayEl.textContent = r; try{ refreshPanels(); }catch(e){} if(!p.keep) rerender && rerender(); });
  root.querySelectorAll("[data-dkopen]").forEach(b => b.onclick = ev => { ev.stopPropagation(); const L = dkItems(); const i = Math.max(0, L.findIndex(x => x.title === it.title)); dkCard(i); });
}
function dkPowHTML(it){
  const P = dkPowers(it);
  return P.length ? '<div class="card__actions" style="flex-wrap:wrap">' + P.map((p, j) => '<button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost" data-dkpow="' + j + '"' + (p.off ? ' disabled' : '') + '>' + pcEsc(p.t) + '</button>').join("") + '</div>' : '';
}
/* the full docket card gets the same: clause-by-clause, cost, every chamber, stakeholders, powers */
(function(){
  const _dk = dkCard;
  dkCard = function(i){
    _dk.apply(this, arguments);
    try{
      const L = dkItems(); const it = L[i || 0]; if(!it) return;
      const ol = card.querySelector("details ol"); if(ol){ ol.innerHTML = dkClauseHTML(it); ol.parentElement.open = true; }
      const say = el("dkSay");
      if(say){ const w = document.createElement("div"); w.className = "dk-extra"; w.innerHTML = dkExtraHTML(it) + dkPowHTML(it); say.parentNode.insertBefore(w, say); }
      dkWire(card, it, say, () => dkCard(i));
    }catch(e){ try{ console.warn("dk2", e); }catch(e2){} }
  };
})();
/* every decision card: the docket inline, collapsed to one line until opened */
function dkInlineHTML(it){
  return '<details class="dk-inline" style="border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.15);border-radius:8px;padding:6px 10px;margin:0 0 10px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.55);text-align:left">' +
    '<summary style="cursor:pointer;font:600 13px system-ui">Docket: ' + pcEsc(it.title) + ' · clauses, cost, every chamber, your state, your powers ▾</summary>' +
    '<p class="muted" style="margin:6px 0">' + pcEsc(it.line || "") + ' Sponsor: ' + pcEsc(youMark(it.sponsor || "")) + '.</p>' +
    '<ol style="margin:4px 0 0 18px;font-size:13px">' + dkClauseHTML(it) + '</ol>' + dkExtraHTML(it) +
    (function(){ try{ return dkPublic(it, !!it.national || roleKey() === "pres"); }catch(e){ return ""; } })() +
    '<div class="dream dk-say"></div>' + dkPowHTML(it) +
    '<div class="card__actions"><button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost" data-dkopen="1">Open the full docket</button></div></details>';
}
function dkInject(){
  try{
    if(!card || veil.hidden) return;
    if(typeof SIT !== "undefined" && SIT.on) return;           /* 20261013s: open hours and other sittings are not about a bill's text */
    if(card.querySelector(".dk-inline") || el("dkSay") || el("gcSay")) return;
    const btns = [...card.querySelectorAll("button")];
    if(btns.length < 2) return;
    if(/Capitol Ledger|Today's paper/i.test((card.querySelector(".eyebrow") || {}).textContent || "")) return;
    if(!/vote|aye|nay|sign it|veto|rule for|whip|table it|delay|amend|have them|approve|reject|choose/i.test(card.textContent) && !btns.some(b => /vote|aye|nay|yea|sign|veto|rule|whip|support|oppose|table|delay|pass|amend|let them|have them|decide|back it|kill|approve|reject|send|call the|yes|no\b|for it|against/i.test(b.textContent))) return;
    const it = dkFind(card.textContent); if(!it) return;
    const w = document.createElement("div"); w.innerHTML = dkInlineHTML(it);
    const node = w.firstChild;
    node.open = card.textContent.indexOf(it.title) >= 0;      /* open where the card is about this bill, folded elsewhere */
    const h1 = card.querySelector("h1, .eyebrow"); if(h1 && h1.nextSibling) h1.parentNode.insertBefore(node, h1.nextSibling); else card.insertBefore(node, card.firstChild);
    const reopen = () => { const was = node.open; const n2 = document.createElement("div"); n2.innerHTML = dkInlineHTML(it); const nn = n2.firstChild; nn.open = was; node.replaceWith(nn); dkWire(nn, it, nn.querySelector(".dk-say"), null); };
    dkWire(node, it, node.querySelector(".dk-say"), reopen);
  }catch(e){}
}
function dkBarInject(){
  try{ const bar = el("procBar"); if(!bar || bar.querySelector(".dk-barbtn")) return; if(!dkFind(bar.textContent)) return;
    const b = document.createElement("button"); b.type = "button"; b.className = "dk-barbtn"; b.textContent = "Docket ▸"; b.style.cssText = "margin-left:8px;font:600 12px system-ui;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:6px;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.25);background:#fff;cursor:pointer";
    b.onclick = ev => { ev.stopPropagation(); const it = dkFind(bar.textContent); const L = dkItems(); dkCard(Math.max(0, L.findIndex(x => it && x.title === it.title))); };
    (bar.querySelector(".proc__h") || bar).appendChild(b); }catch(e){}
}
setTimeout(function(){
  try{
    const mo = new MutationObserver(() => { if(dkInject._q) return; dkInject._q = true; requestAnimationFrame(() => { dkInject._q = false; dkInject(); dkBarInject(); }); });
    mo.observe(card, {childList:true, subtree:true}); mo.observe(veil, {attributes:true, attributeFilter:["hidden"]});
    const pb = el("procBar"); if(pb) mo.observe(pb, {childList:true, subtree:true});
  }catch(e){ try{ console.warn("dk2 observer", e); }catch(e2){} }
}, 1200);
/* ── the glossary: plain words for the House's jargon. Neptune calls onumGlossary.scan(el) after each render. ── */
const ONUM_GLOSS = {
  "whip":"The member who counts votes for a party and leans on the undecided.",
  "roll call":"Each member's vote is called and recorded by name.",
  "table":"To set a bill aside without a vote; it may never come back.",
  "cloture":"A vote to end debate so the bill can be voted on.",
  "appropriation":"Money the act allows the government to spend.",
  "provision":"One section or clause of a bill.",
  "clause":"One section or clause of a bill.",
  "amendment":"A proposed change to a bill's text, voted on before the bill itself.",
  "veto":"The executive's refusal to sign; the bill dies unless the legislature overrides.",
  "override":"A two-thirds vote that makes a bill law despite a veto.",
  "sponsor":"The member who introduced the bill and answers for it.",
  "quorum":"The minimum number of members present for business to be valid.",
  "recuse":"To step aside from a decision because of a conflict of interest.",
  "docket":"Everything waiting for your decision, with the full text and the count.",
  "caucus":"A closed meeting of one party's members.",
  "filibuster":"Talking at length to stop a vote from happening.",
  "sunset":"A date when the act stops applying unless renewed.",
  "offset":"How the cost is paid for, usually a cut or a levy.",
  "markup":"Committee session where a bill's text is edited line by line.",
  "statehouse":"Your state's own legislature and its building.",
  "strike":"In a bill, to delete one section from the text before the vote. Struck means it is gone.",
  "amend":"To change a bill's words before the vote. Cutting a cost is an amendment.",
  "lobby":"To press members in person, in private, to vote your way.",
  "budget":"The money the state has for the year. Each yes spends some of it, so there is less for everything else.",
  "petitioner":"Someone who brings a request to an official in person, at open hours.",
  "open hours":"A set time when an official sees members of the public one at a time.",
  "pension letter":"A letter from the pension office about a payment. One that comes back unopened means it did not reach the person, so the payment is still unanswered.",
  "undecided":"A member who has not said how they will vote. These are the ones a whip or a lobbyist works on.",
  "wire":"The news feed of short stories about the country. Districts read it, so it shapes how people see you.",
  "hawk":"On the defence axis, the push toward firm force and spending on arms.",
  "dove":"On the defence axis, the push toward restraint and talk before force.",
  "thrift":"On the spending axis, the push toward keeping public money small.",
  "provision axis":"On the spending axis, the push toward paying for services and people."
};
const onumGlossary = window.onumGlossary = {
  terms:ONUM_GLOSS,
  lookup(term){ const k = String(term || "").toLowerCase().trim().replace(/s$/, ""); return ONUM_GLOSS[k] || ONUM_GLOSS[k + "s"] || null; },
  scan(root){
    try{
      if(!root || !root.querySelectorAll) return 0;
      let n = 0; const seen = new Set();
      const keys = Object.keys(ONUM_GLOSS).sort((a, b) => b.length - a.length);
      const re = new RegExp("\\b(" + keys.map(k => k.replace(/ /g, "\\s")).join("|") + ")s?\\b", "i");
      const walker = document.createTreeWalker(root, NodeFilter.SHOW_TEXT, {acceptNode(t){
        const p = t.parentElement; if(!p || p.closest("button, summary, input, textarea, script, style, .gloss, [data-tipk], a")) return NodeFilter.FILTER_REJECT;
        return re.test(t.nodeValue) ? NodeFilter.FILTER_ACCEPT : NodeFilter.FILTER_SKIP; }});
      const list = []; let t; while((t = walker.nextNode()) && list.length < 60) list.push(t);
      list.forEach(t => {
        const m = t.nodeValue.match(re); if(!m) return; const key = m[1].toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, " ");
        if(seen.has(key)) return; seen.add(key);
        const def = onumGlossary.lookup(key); if(!def) return;
        const i = m.index, s = document.createElement("span");
        s.className = "gloss"; s.title = def; s.style.cssText = "border-bottom:1px dotted currentColor;cursor:help";
        s.textContent = m[0];
        const after = t.splitText(i); after.nodeValue = after.nodeValue.slice(m[0].length); t.parentNode.insertBefore(s, after); n++;
      });
      return n;
    }catch(e){ return 0; }
  }
};
setTimeout(function(){ try{ const mo = new MutationObserver(() => { if(onumGlossary._q) return; onumGlossary._q = true; setTimeout(() => { onumGlossary._q = false; if(!veil.hidden) onumGlossary.scan(card); }, 120); }); mo.observe(card, {childList:true}); }catch(e){} }, 1300);

/* ══ 20261013a · SOLID UI: no see-through panels over bright scenes ══
   A global override appended last: every panel, card, popup, bar, tooltip and docket gets a
   solid background (its own colour, made opaque) and high-contrast text. Neptune's blocks
   are untouched; their panels are covered by the same selectors. */
(function(){
  const SEL = ["#card", ".card", "#pcScreen", ".pc", "#procBar", ".proc", ".dossier", ".pane", ".panel", ".billbox", ".hail", ".plate", ".prompt", ".prompt__ticker", ".prompt__btn",
    ".job", "#job", "#clTx", "[class^='cl-']", "[class*=' cl-']", "[class^='sx-']", "[class*=' sx-']", ".tl__h", ".tl__foot", ".whip__h", ".feed", ".dmw", ".chat", ".talk", ".phone", ".tip", "#tip", "[role=tooltip]",
    ".ach-toast", ".dk-inline", ".toolbar", "#uiToolbar", "#uiToolbar button", ".kbd", ".keys-intro .keys", ".compass", ".wire", ".notices", "#dmBadge", ".q-lore", ".q-world", ".settings", ".pause-overlay > *"].join(",");
  const st = document.createElement("style");
  st.id = "onumSolidUI";
  st.textContent = SEL + "{backdrop-filter:none !important;-webkit-backdrop-filter:none !important;opacity:1 !important}" +
    ".dk-inline{background:#fbf8f1 !important;color:#141414 !important}" +
    ".tip,#tip,[role=tooltip]{background:#111 !important;color:#fff !important}" +
    "#veil.sitx #card{background:#f6f1e6 !important;color:#141414 !important}" +
    ".hud{background:#14181f !important;padding:4px 10px !important;border-radius:6px !important;display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column}.hud i,.hud b{color:#f3f3f3 !important;text-shadow:none !important;opacity:1 !important}" +
    ".compass{background:#14181f !important}.compass__rail{opacity:1 !important}";
  const add = () => { if(!document.getElementById("onumSolidUI")) document.head.appendChild(st); else document.head.appendChild(document.getElementById("onumSolidUI")); };
  const rgba = s => { const m = /rgba?\(([^)]+)\)/.exec(s || ""); if(!m) return null; const p = m[1].split(/[ ,\/]+/).filter(Boolean).map(Number); return {r:p[0], g:p[1], b:p[2], a:p.length > 3 ? p[3] : 1}; };
  const lum = c => (0.2126 * c.r + 0.7152 * c.g + 0.0722 * c.b) / 255;
  function solidify(root){
    try{
      const L = (root || document).querySelectorAll(SEL);
      for(let i = 0; i < L.length && i < 400; i++){
        const e = L[i]; if(e._solid === 1) continue;
        const cs = getComputedStyle(e); const bg = rgba(cs.backgroundColor);
        if(!bg || cs.display === "none") continue;
        if(bg.a > 0.05 && bg.a < 1){
          /* the same colour, opaque, blended over a neutral so it never reads as a hole */
          const base = lum(bg) > 0.5 ? 245 : 18, k = bg.a;
          const r = Math.round(bg.r * k + base * (1 - k)), g = Math.round(bg.g * k + base * (1 - k)), b = Math.round(bg.b * k + base * (1 - k));
          e.style.setProperty("background-color", "rgb(" + r + "," + g + "," + b + ")", "important");
          const bgi = cs.backgroundImage; if(bgi && bgi !== "none" && /rgba|transparent/.test(bgi)) e.style.setProperty("background-image", "none", "important");
          const fc = rgba(cs.color); if(fc){ const lb = (r * 0.2126 + g * 0.7152 + b * 0.0722) / 255, lf = lum(fc); if(Math.abs(lb - lf) < 0.45) e.style.setProperty("color", lb > 0.5 ? "#141414" : "#f4f4f4", "important"); }
        }
        e._solid = 1;
      }
    }catch(e){}
  }
  add();
  setTimeout(add, 3000);
  let q = false;
  const mo = new MutationObserver(() => { if(q) return; q = true; setTimeout(() => { q = false; solidify(document); }, 250); });
  setTimeout(() => { try{ mo.observe(document.body, {childList:true, subtree:true}); solidify(document); }catch(e){} }, 1500);
  window.onumSolidUI = solidify;
})();

/* ══ 20261013b · STATECRAFT: every state and nation has a mind ══
   States and foreign powers act for themselves: deals, statements, protests, provocations,
   rare spying, mistakes that others bail out or exploit. Crises come from the numbers.
   Secession is never the default: it needs grievance, divergence, culture and faith,
   provocation, ignored pleas and backing, compounding for weeks. Neptune draws the borders
   from window.ONUM_POLITIES; leaders are real people (npcIds) with minds in ONUM_MINDS. */
const SC_STYLE = ["consensus","boss rule","reformist","pious","technocratic","populist","old-family"];
const SC_GOAL = ["power","prosperity","survival","hostility"];
function scEnsure(){
  if(G.sc && G.sc.v === 1) return G.sc;
  const S = G.sc = {v:1, st:{}, na:{}, polities:[], crises:[], log:[], day:0};
  (G.states || []).forEach(st => {
    const r = rng(seedOf("sc:" + st.n));
    S.st[st.n] = {style:drawn(SC_STYLE, r), goal:drawn(SC_GOAL.slice(0, 3), r), griev:Math.round(r() * 20), heard:0, ignored:0, streak:0, prov:0, backing:0, mood:50};
  });
  WORLD_POWERS.forEach(p => { const r = rng(seedOf("na:" + p.k)); S.na[p.k] = {name:p.t, style:drawn(SC_STYLE, r), goal:drawn(SC_GOAL, r), toward:Math.round(r() * 60 - 30), griev:Math.round(r() * 30)}; });
  return S;
}
/* leaders and envoys are people: they exist, post on the Vox, and LiveTalk can talk to them */
function scPerson(key, name, title, home){
  G.civ = G.civ || [];
  let p = G.civ.find(c => c.scKey === key && !c.gone);
  if(p) return p;
  try{ p = makeCivilian(); }catch(e){ return null; }
  p.name = name; p.role = title; p.scKey = key; p.room = "abroad"; p.abroad = true; p.caseOpen = false; p.cause = null; p.home = home;
  G.civ.push(p); try{ _allCache = null; }catch(e){}
  return p;
}
function scLeaders(){
  const S = scEnsure();
  Object.keys(S.st).forEach(n => { const s = S.st[n];
    /* 20261013r: while you hold the governorship, the state's governor is you, not whoever held it before */
    let mine = false; try{ mine = !!(G.you && heldOffice() === "gov" && n === G.you.state); }catch(e){}
    if(mine){ s.gov = G.you.name; s.govId = null; s.youGov = true; return; }
    if(s.youGov){ s.youGov = false; s.gov = newName(n); }
    if(!s.gov){ s.gov = newName(n); } s.govId = (scPerson("gov:" + n, s.gov, "Governor of " + n, n) || {}).id; });
  WORLD_POWERS.forEach(p => { const a = S.na[p.k]; const r = rng(seedOf("lead:" + p.k));
    if(!a.leader){ a.leader = foreignName(r, p.k); a.leaderT = drawn(["Premier","Chancellor","President","First Minister","Regent"], r); a.envoy = foreignName(r, p.k);
      try{ const e = window.onumForeignEnvoy && window.onumForeignEnvoy(p); if(e && e.name) a.envoy = e.name; }catch(e){} }
    a.leaderId = (scPerson("lead:" + p.k, a.leader, a.leaderT + " of " + p.t, p.t) || {}).id;
    a.envoyId = (scPerson("env:" + p.k, a.envoy, "Ambassador of " + p.t, p.t) || {}).id; });
}
function scLog(t, place){ const S = scEnsure(); S.log.push({d:G.day, t}); if(S.log.length > 120) S.log.shift(); try{ lLog(t, "world"); }catch(e){} try{ if(place) loreAdd(place, "Day " + G.day + ": " + t); }catch(e){} try{ feed(t, "world"); }catch(e){} }
function scVox(pid, t){ try{ const p = byId(pid); if(!p) return; voxAdd({id:G.vox.id++, by:p.id, t, k:"state", v:0, day:G.day, at:hhmm(), backs:0, hits:0, voices:[], mine:false}); }catch(e){} }
function scNation(){ let w = 0, s = 0; (G.states || []).forEach(st => { const p = st.pop || 1; w += p; s += (st.lean || 0) * p; }); return s / Math.max(1, w); }
function scPolPub(ch){
  const S = scEnsure();
  window.ONUM_POLITIES = S.polities.map(p => Object.assign({}, p));
  if(ch){ try{ window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("onum-polity-change", {detail:Object.assign({}, ch)})); document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("onum-polity-change", {detail:Object.assign({}, ch)})); }catch(e){} }
}
/* a plea from a state reaches your desk; answering it is being heard */
function scPlea(n, s){
  const st = (G.states || []).find(x => x.n === n); if(!st) return;
  if(s.youGov) return;
  const what = s.griev > 60 ? "the federal government keeps deciding things for " + n + " without asking" : st.unrest > 55 ? "the streets are restless and the capital has not noticed" : "the roads, the courts and the money all run to the capital";
  try{ pushMail({name:s.gov, id:s.govId}, "A letter from " + n, "Governor " + s.gov + " writes: " + what + ". \"We are asking to be heard before people stop asking.\"", {kind:"plea", options:[
    {t:"Hear them out (30m)", run(){ s.heard += 1; s.griev = Math.max(0, s.griev - 12); s.streak = 0; try{ spend(30, 2); }catch(e){} scLog(n + " says the capital listened, for once.", n); }},
    {t:"Not now", run(){ s.ignored += 1; s.griev = Math.min(100, s.griev + 4); scLog("A plea from " + n + " went unanswered.", n); }}]}); }catch(e){}
  s.plea = G.day;
}
function scDay(){
  const S = scEnsure(); if(S.day === G.day) return; S.day = G.day;
  scLeaders();
  const nat = scNation(), cap = (G.states || []).find(x => x.n === (G.country && G.country.capital)) || (G.states || [])[0] || {};
  (G.states || []).forEach(st => {
    const s = S.st[st.n]; if(!s) return;
    if(s.gone) return;
    /* pressures compound: unrest, distance from the national line, faith and culture apart from the capital, provocation, ignored pleas */
    const div = Math.abs((st.lean || 0) - nat) / 100, cf = (st.creed !== cap.creed ? 0.5 : 0) + (st.cult !== cap.cult ? 0.5 : 0);
    s.griev = clamp(s.griev + (st.unrest || 0) / 120 + div * 1.4 + cf * 0.4 + s.prov * 0.6 + Math.min(10, s.ignored) * 0.06 - s.heard * 0.3 - 1.1 + (Math.random() - 0.5), 0, 100);
    s.prov = Math.max(0, s.prov - 0.1); s.heard = Math.max(0, s.heard - 0.02);
    const P = s.griev * 0.6 + div * 20 + cf * 8 + Math.min(20, s.ignored * 4) + s.backing * 10;
    s.P = Math.round(P);
    s.streak = P >= 82 ? s.streak + 1 : Math.max(0, s.streak - 3);
    s.mood = Math.round(100 - s.griev);
    /* their own business */
    const r = Math.random();
    if(r < .025){ const o = pick((G.states || []).filter(x => x !== st && !S.st[x.n].gone)); if(o){ scLog(st.n + " and " + o.n + " sign a trade compact on " + pick(["grain","rail freight","timber","river shipping","power lines"]) + ".", st.n); st.ind = clamp((st.ind || 0) + 1, 0, 100); o.ind = clamp((o.ind || 0) + 1, 0, 100); } }
    else if(r < .045 && !s.youGov){ scLog("Governor " + s.gov + " of " + st.n + " says publicly: \"" + pick(["We will do it our way, and show the capital how.","The Union is a promise, not a leash.","Ask our farmers what the last bill cost them.","We are loyal. We are not silent."]) + "\"", st.n); scVox(s.govId, pick(["Our way works. Come and see.","The capital should visit more often.","We kept our word. Did they?"])); }
    else if(r < .06 && s.griev > 40){ st.unrest = clamp((st.unrest || 0) + 3, 0, 100); scLog("Protest in " + st.n + ": " + pick(["farmers block the state road","dockworkers march on the statehouse","a crowd outside the federal building"]) + ".", st.n); }
    else if(r < .066){ const o = pick((G.states || []).filter(x => x !== st && !S.st[x.n].gone)); if(o){ S.st[o.n].prov += 2; scLog(st.n + "'s legislature passes a resolution mocking " + o.n + ". " + o.n + " is not laughing.", o.n); } }
    else if(r < .07){ /* a mistake: someone bails them out or exploits it */
      const o = pick((G.states || []).filter(x => x !== st && !S.st[x.n].gone)); if(o){ if(chance(.5)){ scLog(st.n + " botched its " + pick(["budget","flood levee","bond sale"]) + "; " + o.n + " lent engineers and money.", st.n); s.griev = Math.max(0, s.griev - 3); } else { scLog(st.n + " botched its " + pick(["budget","flood levee","bond sale"]) + "; " + o.n + " poached its contracts.", st.n); s.prov += 1.5; } } }
    if(s.griev > 50 && (!s.plea || G.day - s.plea > 12) && chance(.3)) scPlea(st.n, s);
    /* crises from the numbers */
    if((st.unrest || 0) > 80 && chance(.08)) scCrisis("unrest", st.n, "Riots in " + st.n + ". The governor asks for calm and for the Guard.");
    if((st.ind || 0) < 15 && chance(.05)) scCrisis("economy", st.n, "Bank runs in " + st.n + ". Two mills close in a week.");
  });
  if(chance(1 / 120)){ const st = pick(G.states || []); if(st) scCrisis("disaster", st.n, pick(["A flood breaks the levee in ","An ice storm takes the power lines in ","A mine collapse in "]) + st.n + "."); }
  /* foreign powers */
  WORLD_POWERS.forEach(p => {
    const a = S.na[p.k], r = Math.random();
    if(r < .03){ scLog("The " + p.adj + " envoy, " + a.envoy + ", " + (a.toward > 0 ? "proposes a trade understanding" : "complains to the press about the border") + ".", p.t); scVox(a.envoyId, a.toward > 0 ? "We would like to buy more of your grain. Call us." : "Some in your capital forget who your neighbours are."); }
    else if(r < .036 && a.goal === "hostility"){ /* espionage: rare, and only sometimes found */
      const st = pick(G.states || []); if(st && chance(.3)){ a.toward -= 6; scLog("A " + p.adj + " agent was caught photographing the rail yards in " + st.n + ". " + a.leaderT + " " + a.leader + " denies everything.", st.n); } }
    else if(r < .046 && a.goal === "hostility"){ /* backing a restless state */
      const st = (G.states || []).filter(x => S.st[x.n] && S.st[x.n].griev > 55 && !S.st[x.n].gone).sort((x, y) => S.st[y.n].griev - S.st[x.n].griev)[0];
      if(st){ S.st[st.n].backing = Math.min(2, S.st[st.n].backing + 0.4); scLog("The " + p.adj + " press praises " + st.n + "'s \"independent spirit.\"", st.n); } }
    a.toward = clamp(a.toward + (Math.random() - 0.5) * 2, -100, 100);
  });
  scSecession();
  scPolPub(null);
  try{ scMinds(); }catch(e){}
}
function scCrisis(k, place, t){
  const S = scEnsure(); S.crises.push({d:G.day, k, place, t}); if(S.crises.length > 40) S.crises.shift();
  scLog(t, place);
  try{ document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("onum-crisis", {detail:{k, place, t}})); }catch(e){}
}
const SC_NEW = ["Free State of", "Commonwealth of", "Republic of", "Confederation of"];
function scSecession(){
  const S = scEnsure();
  /* states near the edge: open tension, published so Neptune can show it */
  (G.states || []).forEach(st => {
    const s = S.st[st.n]; if(!s || s.gone) return;
    let pol = S.polities.find(p => p.memberStates.indexOf(st.n) >= 0 && (p.status === "tension" || p.status === "war"));
    if(s.streak >= 12 && !pol && !s.youGov){
      const nb = S.polities.find(p => p.status === "tension" && (G.states || []).some(x => p.memberStates.indexOf(x.n) >= 0 && Math.abs((x.col || 0) - (st.col || 0)) + Math.abs((x.row || 0) - (st.row || 0)) <= 1));
      if(nb){ nb.memberStates.push(st.n); scPolPub(nb); scLog(st.n + " joins " + nb.name + "'s movement.", st.n); return; }
      pol = {id:"pol" + (S.polities.length + 1), name:pick(SC_NEW) + " " + st.n, govType:s.style === "pious" ? "theocratic council" : s.style === "boss rule" ? "strongman" : s.style === "populist" ? "people's assembly" : "provisional republic",
        leader:{npcId:s.govId, name:s.gov, title:"Governor of " + st.n}, memberStates:[st.n], parentId:"union", status:"tension", foundedDay:null, since:G.day};
      S.polities.push(pol); scPolPub(pol);
      scLog("In " + st.n + ", talk of leaving the Union is no longer a joke. A movement calls itself the " + pol.name + ".", st.n);
    }
    if(pol && pol.status === "tension"){
      const all = pol.memberStates.map(n => S.st[n]);
      const cool = all.every(x => x.streak === 0);
      if(cool && G.day - pol.since > 10){ pol.status = "reunited"; scPolPub(pol); scLog("The " + pol.name + " movement fades; " + pol.memberStates.join(", ") + " stays in the Union.", st.n); return; }
      if(all.every(x => x.streak >= 60) && all.some(x => x.backing > 0.5 || x.ignored >= 6) && chance(.02)){
        if(chance(.4)){ pol.status = "war"; pol.since = G.day; scPolPub(pol); scCrisis("secession", st.n, pol.name + " declares independence. The Union calls it rebellion. Shots at the state line."); }
        else { pol.status = "seceded"; pol.foundedDay = G.day; all.forEach(x => x.gone = true); scPolPub(pol); scCrisis("secession", st.n, pol.name + " leaves the Union after a referendum. " + pol.leader.name + " is its first " + (pol.govType === "strongman" ? "leader" : "president") + "."); }
      }
    }
    if(pol && pol.status === "war" && G.day - pol.since > 10 && chance(.06)){
      if(chance(.5)){ pol.status = "seceded"; pol.foundedDay = G.day; pol.memberStates.forEach(n => S.st[n].gone = true); scLog("The fighting ends with a treaty: the " + pol.name + " is recognised.", st.n); }
      else { pol.status = "reunited"; pol.memberStates.forEach(n => { S.st[n].griev = 40; S.st[n].streak = 0; }); scLog("The rebellion in " + pol.memberStates.join(", ") + " collapses. Reunion, and bitterness.", st.n); }
      scPolPub(pol);
    }
  });
}
/* what a state or nation intends toward you, in plain words (for the glossary hover and the map) */
function scIntent(name){
  const S = scEnsure(), s = S.st[name];
  if(s){ const pol = S.polities.find(p => p.memberStates.indexOf(name) >= 0 && p.status !== "reunited");
    return name + ": " + s.style + " government under Governor " + s.gov + "; wants " + s.goal + ". Grievance " + Math.round(s.griev) + "/100; heard " + Math.round(s.heard * 10) / 10 + " times, ignored " + s.ignored + "." + (pol ? " " + pol.name + " (" + pol.status + ")." : ""); }
  const p = WORLD_POWERS.find(x => x.t === name || x.k === name); if(p){ const a = S.na[p.k]; return p.t + ": " + a.style + ", led by " + (a.leaderT || "") + " " + (a.leader || "") + "; wants " + a.goal + " (" + p.want + "). Toward the Republic: " + (a.toward > 20 ? "friendly" : a.toward < -20 ? "hostile" : "wary") + "."; }
  return null;
}
(function(){ try{ const L = onumGlossary.lookup; onumGlossary.lookup = function(t){ return L.call(onumGlossary, t) || (G && G.sc ? scIntent(String(t || "")) : null); }; }catch(e){} })();
/* ── minds for LiveTalk: window.onumMind(npcId) and ONUM_MINDS ── */
function onumMindOf(id){
  const p = byId(id); if(!p) return null;
  const mind = {name:p.name, goals:[], beliefs:[], plans:[], memory:[], family:null, job:p.role || p.post || (p.kind === "member" ? "Member of the House" : ""), wealth:null, mood:null};
  try{ const S = scEnsure();
    if(p.scKey){ const k = p.scKey.split(":"), key = k.slice(1).join(":");
      if(k[0] === "cp"){ const c = (S.cp || {})[key] || {}; mind.goals = [c.rel === "rival" ? "outdo you" : c.rel === "mentor" ? "see you grow into the job" : c.rel === "ally" ? "pass something together" : "get the attention you get", "win re-election in " + key];
        mind.beliefs = [c.party + " is right about most things", "God is the Most High"]; mind.plans = ["compare records with you", "raise " + key + "'s needs in the capital"]; mind.wealth = c.wealth || null; mind.job = (c.title || "") + " for " + key; }
      else if(k[0] === "gov"){ const s = S.st[key]; mind.goals = [s.goal, "be heard in the capital"]; mind.beliefs = [s.style + " government is how it must be done"]; mind.plans = s.griev > 50 ? ["write to the capital again", s.streak > 5 ? "keep the independence movement alive" : "rally the legislature"] : ["trade with the neighbours"]; mind.mood = s.mood; }
      else { const a = S.na[key]; mind.goals = [a.goal]; mind.beliefs = [WORLD_POWERS.find(x => x.k === key).want + " matters more than anything"]; mind.plans = [a.toward > 0 ? "a trade understanding" : "test the Republic's patience"]; mind.mood = 50 + Math.round(a.toward / 2); } } }catch(e){}
  try{ if(p.kind === "member" && p.intent && p.intent.k && p.intent.k.length > 3) mind.goals.push("intends to " + p.intent.k); if(p.mem) mind.memory = p.mem.slice(-6).map(m => m.t || String(m)); if(p.traits) mind.beliefs.push("temper " + p.traits.temper + ", discretion " + p.traits.discretion); if(mind.mood == null){ mind.mood = moodOf(p).val; } }catch(e){}
  try{ const f = G.fam && G.fam.p[p.id]; if(f) mind.family = {spouse:f.spouse, kids:f.kids, kin:f.kin, feuds:f.feuds}; }catch(e){}
  return mind;
}
window.onumMind = onumMindOf;
function scMinds(){
  const M = window.ONUM_MINDS = window.ONUM_MINDS || {};
  (G.civ || []).filter(c => c.scKey && !c.gone).forEach(c => { M[c.id] = onumMindOf(c.id); });
  (G.members || []).filter(m => !m.gone).slice(0, 80).forEach(m => { M[m.id] = onumMindOf(m.id); });
  try{ const D = G.dist; if(D) D.actors.forEach(a => { M["town:" + a.id] = {name:a.name, goals:a.intent ? ["save for a " + a.intent.k] : [], beliefs:[a.faith > 60 ? "God is the Most High" : "work pays"], plans:[], memory:(D.gossip || []).filter(g => g.t.indexOf(a.name) >= 0).slice(-4).map(g => g.t), family:null, job:a.job, wealth:"private", mood:null}; }); }catch(e){}
}
function scHTML(){
  const S = scEnsure(), out = ['<div class="pc__h">The states and the world</div>'];
  const hot = Object.keys(S.st).map(n => [n, S.st[n]]).sort((a, b) => b[1].griev - a[1].griev).slice(0, 6);
  hot.forEach(([n, s]) => out.push('<div>' + pcEsc(n) + ' · ' + pcEsc(s.style) + ' · Gov. ' + pcEsc(s.gov || "") + ' · grievance ' + Math.round(s.griev) + ' · heard ' + Math.round(s.heard * 10) / 10 + ' / ignored ' + s.ignored + (s.gone ? ' · <b>gone</b>' : '') + '</div>'));
  S.polities.filter(p => p.status !== "reunited").forEach(p => out.push('<div class="pc__m"><b>' + pcEsc(p.name) + '</b> · ' + pcEsc(p.status) + ' · ' + pcEsc(p.memberStates.join(", ")) + '</div>'));
  S.log.slice(-6).reverse().forEach(l => out.push('<div class="pc__m">d' + l.d + ' · ' + pcEsc(l.t) + '</div>'));
  return out.join("");
}
/* fallback only: if Neptune's map renderer is not drawing polities, outline breakaway states */
(function(){ try{ const _ms = mapShade; mapShade = function(P){ const r = _ms.apply(this, arguments);
  try{ if(window.ONUM_POLITIES_RENDERED || typeof window.onumPolityApply === "function" || !G.sc) return r; G.sc.polities.filter(p => p.status === "seceded" || p.status === "war" || p.status === "tension").forEach(p => p.memberStates.forEach(n => {
    P.querySelectorAll('[data-st="' + n + '"]').forEach(g => { const sh = g.matches("rect,path,polygon") ? g : g.querySelector("rect,path,polygon"); if(sh){ sh.setAttribute("stroke", p.status === "tension" ? "#c08a00" : "#8a1020"); sh.setAttribute("stroke-width", p.status === "tension" ? "2" : "3"); sh.setAttribute("stroke-dasharray", p.status === "seceded" ? "" : "4 3"); } }); })); }catch(e){}
  return r; }; }catch(e){} })();
/* ── counterparts: your opposite number in every state, at your level ── */
const CP_REL = ["rival","ally","mentor","envious peer"];
function cpTitle(){ let k = "house"; try{ k = roleKey(); }catch(e){} return {house:"Representative", leader:"Representative", chair:"Representative", senate:"Senator", gov:"Governor", bench:"Chief Justice of the state", pres:"Head of the state party"}[k] || "Representative"; }
function cpEnsure(){
  const S = scEnsure(); S.cp = S.cp || {}; const T = cpTitle(), y = G.you || {};
  (G.states || []).forEach(st => {
    if(st.n === y.state) return;
    const key = st.n + ":" + T;
    let c = S.cp[st.n];
    if(!c || c.title !== T){
      const r = rng(seedOf("cp:" + key));
      const name = T === "Governor" && S.st[st.n] && S.st[st.n].gov ? S.st[st.n].gov : newName(st.n);
      const party = (st.lean || 0) + (r() * 40 - 20) > 8 ? "FED" : (st.lean || 0) + (r() * 40 - 20) < -8 ? "COM" : "IND";
      c = S.cp[st.n] = {name, title:T, state:st.n, party, creed:st.creed || "latin", cult:st.cult || null, rel:drawn(CP_REL, r), wealth:drawn(["modest","comfortable","family money","new money"], r),
        rec:{wins:Math.round(r() * 3), approval:Math.round(35 + r() * 50), bills:Math.round(r() * 4)}, since:G.day || 1, last:0};
    }
    const p = scPerson("cp:" + st.n, c.name, c.title + " for " + st.n, st.n); if(p){ c.npcId = p.id; p.party = c.party; p.state = st.n; p.creed = c.creed; }
  });
  cpPublish();
  return S.cp;
}
function cpPublish(){
  const S = scEnsure(), out = {};
  Object.keys(S.cp || {}).forEach(n => { const c = S.cp[n]; out[n] = {npcId:c.npcId, name:c.name, title:c.title, state:n, party:c.party, faith:c.creed, culture:c.cult, relation:c.rel, wealth:c.wealth, record:c.rec,
    family:(function(){ try{ const f = famEnsure().p[c.npcId]; return f ? {spouse:f.spouse, kids:f.kids} : null; }catch(e){ return null; } })(), mind:(function(){ try{ return onumMindOf(c.npcId); }catch(e){ return null; } })()}; });
  window.ONUM_COUNTERPARTS = out;
  try{ window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("onum-counterparts", {detail:{n:Object.keys(out).length}})); }catch(e){}
}
function cpDay(){
  const S = scEnsure(); cpEnsure(); const y = G.you || {};
  Object.keys(S.cp).forEach(n => { const c = S.cp[n]; c.rec.approval = clamp(c.rec.approval + rint(-2, 2), 5, 98); if(chance(.03)) c.rec.bills++; });
  /* one or two of them reach out, in character */
  const L = Object.keys(S.cp).map(n => S.cp[n]).filter(c => c.npcId && G.day - (c.last || 0) > 6);
  if(L.length && chance(.35)){
    const c = pick(L); c.last = G.day; const ya = Math.round(y.approval || 50), mine = c.rec.approval;
    const T = {rival:[ "Saw your numbers. " + ya + ". Mine are " + mine + ". Just saying.", "Your bill would never pass in " + c.state + ". Ours did."],
      ally:["If you need votes from " + c.state + "'s side, call me.", "We should compare notes on the " + ((G.bill && G.bill.title) || "next bill") + "."],
      mentor:["A word from someone who has done this longer: answer your mail from home first.", "Your first term is about being seen to listen. Mine was."],
      "envious peer":["How do you get the papers to print you every week?", "Congratulations, I suppose. Some of us do the work without the cameras."]}[c.rel];
    try{ dmPush(c.npcId, c.npcId, pick(T)); }catch(e){}
    try{ if(G.fam && G.fam.p[c.npcId] && G.fam.p[c.npcId].kids.length && chance(.3)) dmPush(c.npcId, c.npcId, "My " + (G.fam.p[c.npcId].kids[0].age < 12 ? "youngest" : "eldest") + " asked about you. Small world."); }catch(e){}
  }
  cpPublish();
}
function cpHTML(){
  const S = scEnsure(); if(!S.cp) return ""; const y = G.you || {};
  const rows = Object.keys(S.cp).slice(0, 20).map(n => { const c = S.cp[n]; return '<div>' + pcEsc(n) + ': <b>' + pcEsc(c.name) + '</b> (' + pcEsc(c.party) + ', ' + pcEsc(c.title) + ') · ' + pcEsc(c.rel) + ' · approval ' + c.rec.approval + ' vs your ' + Math.round(y.approval || 0) + ' · bills ' + c.rec.bills + '</div>'; });
  return '<div class="pc__h">Your counterparts in the other states</div>' + rows.join("");
}

/* ══ 20261013i · every office has a named, embodied holder at their post ══ */
const OH = {list:[]};
function ohSpec(){
  const X = (function(){ try{ return execTitle(); }catch(e){ return "President"; } })();
  const out = [];
  out.push({k:"pres", title:X, name:G.exec && G.exec.name, room:"exec", spot:[0, 0.78], party:G.exec && G.exec.party});
  out.push({k:"vp", title:X === "President" ? "Vice President" : "Deputy to the " + X, room:"exec", spot:[-2.6, 0.45], wk:"senate"});
  [["sos","Secretary of State"],["treas","Secretary of the Treasury"],["war","Secretary of Defense"],["ag","Attorney General"]].forEach((c, i) => out.push({k:"cab:" + c[0], title:c[1], room:"exec", spot:[-3 + i * 2, 0.55]}));
  out.push({k:"speaker", title:"Speaker of the House", room:"floor", spot:[0, 0.12]});
  [["maj","Senate Majority Leader"],["min","Senate Minority Leader"],["ppt","President pro tempore of the Senate"]].forEach((c, i) => out.push({k:"sen:" + c[0], title:c[1], room:"senate", spot:[-3 + i * 3, 0.22]}));
  (G.states || []).slice(0, 18).forEach((s, i) => out.push({k:"sen:" + s.n, title:"Senator from " + s.n, room:"senate", spot:[-5.2 + (i % 6) * 2.08, 0.42 + Math.floor(i / 6) * 0.15], state:s.n}));
  (G.court && G.court.length ? G.court : [{}, {}, {}, {}, {}]).forEach((j, i) => out.push({k:"bench:" + i, title:i === 0 ? "Chief Justice" : "Associate Justice", name:j.name, room:"court", spot:[-3.2 + i * 1.6, 0.93], chambers:true, seat:true}));
  (G.states || []).forEach(s => out.push({k:"gov:" + s.n, title:"Governor of " + s.n, room:(G.you && s.n === G.you.state) ? "govoffice" : "abroad", spot:[0, 0.35], state:s.n, gov:true}));
  return out;
}
function ohYours(o){
  const y = G.you, k = (function(){ try{ return heldOffice(); }catch(e){ return null; } })(); if(!y) return false;
  if(o.k === "pres") return k === "pres" || (G.exec && G.exec.you);
  if(o.gov) return k === "gov" && o.state === y.state;
  if(o.k.indexOf("bench:") === 0) return k === "bench" && o.k === "bench:" + ((G.court || []).length ? (G.court.length - 1) : 4);
  if(o.k === "sen:" + y.state) return k === "senate";
  if(o.k === "speaker") return !!y.speaker;
  return false;
}
function ohEnsure(){
  const specs = ohSpec(); OH.list = [];
  specs.forEach(o => {
    let p = null;
    if(o.gov){ try{ const S = scEnsure(); const s = S.st[o.state]; if(s && s.govId != null) p = byId(s.govId); }catch(e){} }
    const key = "oh:" + o.k;
    if(!p && o.name){ try{ p = everyone().find(q => q && q.name === o.name && !q.gone && q !== G.you) || null; if(p && !p.scKey) p.scKey = key; }catch(e){} }
    if(!p) p = scPerson(key, o.name || newName(o.state), o.title, o.state || "capital");
    if(!p) return;
    if(o.name && p.name !== o.name && !p.formerOf) p.name = o.name;
    p.role = o.title; p.ohK = o.k; p.ohRoom = o.room; p.ohSpot = o.spot; p.ohWk = o.wk; p.ohCh = o.chambers; p.ohSeat = o.seat; p.caseOpen = false; p.cause = null; p.party = p.party || o.party;
    if(ohYours(o)){
      if(!p.formerOf){ p.formerOf = o.title; p.role = "Former " + o.title; p.ohK = null; p.scKey = "former:" + o.k; p.room = "abroad";
        let hl = null; try{ hl = ofhHandoverLine(o.k, o.title); feed(hl, "good"); }catch(e){}
        try{ const pv = window.onumOfficePrevious(o.k); if(!pv || pv.name !== p.name){ p.role = "Former aide in the office of the " + o.title; } }catch(e){} }
      return;
    }
    OH.list.push(p);
  });
  return OH.list;
}
function ohWhere(p){
  const h = (G.minute || 0) / 60, d = G.day || 0;
  if(h < 8 || h >= 18) return "abroad";
  if(p.ohK === "pres" && d % 7 === 3 && h >= 11 && h < 12) return "floor";           /* addresses the House */
  if(p.ohK === "pres" && d % 5 === 1 && h >= 15 && h < 16) return "steps";           /* events on the steps */
  if(p.ohWk && h >= 10 && h < 12) return p.ohWk;                                       /* VP presides in the Senate */
  if(p.ohCh && (h >= 12 && h < 14)) return "abroad";                                   /* in chambers over lunch */
  return p.ohRoom;
}
function ohPlace(p, r){
  const R = ROOMS[r]; if(!R || !p.ohSpot) return;
  const b = roomBox(r), sp = p.ohSpot;
  let x = Math.max(-b.hw, Math.min(b.hw, sp[0])), z = b.d0 + (b.d1 - b.d0) * sp[1];
  if(p.ohK === "pres" && r === "floor"){ x = 0; z = b.d0 + 0.6; }
  if(p.room !== r || p.x == null){ p.x = x; p.z = z; }
  if(p.ohSeat){ p.x = p.tx = x; p.z = p.tz = z; p.facing = Math.PI; p.sitting = true; p.sitY = 0.15; p.seatFace = Math.PI; return; }
  p.sitting = false;
  p.tx = x + (Math.random() * 2 - 1) * 0.6; p.tz = z + (Math.random() * 2 - 1) * 0.4;
}
function ohTick(){
  if(!G || !G.states) return;
  if(!G.you || !G.civ) return;
  if(!OH.list.length || G.day !== OH.day || OH.civ !== G.civ || OH.list.some(p => !p.gov && G.civ.indexOf(p) < 0 && G.members.indexOf(p) < 0)){ OH.civ = G.civ; OH.day = G.day; ohEnsure(); ohPublish(); }
  OH.list.forEach(p => { const r = ohWhere(p); const moved = p.room !== r;
    if(r === "abroad"){ p.room = "abroad"; p.abroad = true; return; }
    p.abroad = false; if(moved || chance(.25)) ohPlace(p, r); p.room = r; });
  try{ const N = new Set(OH.list.map(p => p.name)); everyone().forEach(q => { if(q && !q.ohK && !q.formerOf && N.has(q.name) && q !== G.you) q.room = "abroad"; }); }catch(e){}
  try{ _allCache = null; }catch(e){}
}
function ohMind(p){
  const t = p.role || "";
  const goals = t.indexOf("Justice") >= 0 ? ["decide the cases before the bench by the law", "keep the court above the fight"]
    : t.indexOf("Governor") >= 0 ? ["keep " + (p.home || "the state") + " solvent and calm", "get my share from the capital"]
    : t.indexOf("Senat") >= 0 ? ["hold the chamber together", "pass the next appropriations"]
    : t.indexOf("Speaker") >= 0 ? ["keep the majority in line", "move the floor schedule"]
    : t.indexOf("Secretary") >= 0 || t.indexOf("Attorney") >= 0 ? ["carry out the " + ((function(){ try{ return execTitle(); }catch(e){ return "President"; } })()) + "'s program", "keep my department out of the papers"]
    : ["govern the Union", "keep the coalition together", "win the next term"];
  let plans = [];
  try{ const it = (dkItems() || [])[0]; if(it) plans.push("make my view known on the " + it.title); }catch(e){}
  plans.push("be at " + (((ROOMS[p.ohRoom] || {}).t) || "my post") + " during working hours");
  return {name:p.name, goals, beliefs:["God is the Most High", "the office is bigger than the person"], plans, memory:[], family:null, job:t, wealth:"public salary", mood:null, office:p.ohK, where:p.room};
}
function ohPublish(){
  const M = window.ONUM_MINDS = window.ONUM_MINDS || {};
  const H = window.ONUM_OFFICEHOLDERS = [];
  OH.list.forEach(p => { M[p.id] = ohMind(p); H.push({npcId:p.id, name:p.name, title:p.role, office:p.ohK, post:p.ohRoom, room:p.room});
    try{ ONUM_GLOSS[p.name.toLowerCase()] = p.role + ". Works at " + (((ROOMS[p.ohRoom] || {}).t) || "their post") + ", 8 to 6. Reachable by Phone and DM."; }catch(e){} });
  try{ (G.civ || []).filter(c => c.formerOf).forEach(c => { M[c.id] = {name:c.name, goals:["write the memoir", "stay relevant"], beliefs:["I did it better"], plans:[], memory:["held the office of " + c.formerOf], family:null, job:"Former " + c.formerOf, wealth:"comfortable", mood:null};
    try{ ONUM_GLOSS[c.name.toLowerCase()] = "Former " + c.formerOf + "."; }catch(e){} }); }catch(e){}
  try{ window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("onum-officeholders", {detail:H})); }catch(e){}
}
(function(){ const base = window.onumMind; window.onumMind = function(id){ const p = (function(){ try{ return byId(id); }catch(e){ return null; } })(); if(p && (p.ohK || p.formerOf)) return (window.ONUM_MINDS || {})[p.id] || ohMind(p); return base ? base(id) : null; }; })();
setInterval(() => { try{ ohTick(); }catch(e){ console.warn("oh", e); } }, 3000);

/* ══ 20261013i · parties have a philosophy that drifts, and a history that is cited ══ */
const PH_AX = [["econ","Economic","market","provision"],["soc","Social","liberty","order"],["fed","Federal vs state","the Union","the states"],["faith","Faith","public faith","secular"],["frn","Foreign","hawk","dove"]];
function phEnsure(){
  ensureParties();
  const H = G.ph = G.ph || {v:1, p:{}};
  Object.keys(G.parties).forEach(id => {
    if(H.p[id]) return;
    const M = (G.members || []).filter(m => m.party === id && m.pos);
    const av = k => M.length ? Math.round(M.reduce((a, m) => a + (m.pos[k] || 0), 0) / M.length) : 0;
    const r = rng(seedOf("ph:" + id));
    const L = {econ:av("econ"), soc:av("lib"), fed:Math.round((r() * 2 - 1) * 40), faith:Math.round((r() * 2 - 1) * 50), frn:av("def")};
    H.p[id] = {id, lean:L, snaps:[{d:G.day || 0, lean:Object.assign({}, L), why:"founding position"}], leaders:[], splits:[], votes:[], last:{acts:0}};
  });
  return H;
}
function phLeader(id){ const M = (G.members || []).filter(m => m.party === id && !m.gone); if(!M.length) return null; return M.slice().sort((a, b) => ((b.influence || b.standing || 0) - (a.influence || a.standing || 0)))[0]; }
function phDay(){
  const H = phEnsure();
  Object.values(H.p).forEach(P => {
    const L = P.lean, M = (G.members || []).filter(m => m.party === P.id && m.pos && !m.gone);
    const why = [];
    const pull = (k, tgt, w, tag) => { const d = (tgt - L[k]) * w; if(Math.abs(d) > 0.05){ L[k] = clamp(L[k] + d, -100, 100); if(tag && Math.abs(d) > 0.4) why.push(tag); } };
    if(M.length){ const av = k => M.reduce((a, m) => a + (m.pos[k] || 0), 0) / M.length; pull("econ", av("econ"), 0.02, "the members"); pull("soc", av("lib"), 0.02, "the members"); pull("frn", av("def"), 0.02, "the members"); }
    try{ const S = G.sc; if(S && S.crises && S.crises.filter(c => c.d > G.day - 3).length){ pull("frn", L.frn + 12, 0.08, "a crisis"); pull("fed", L.fed + 10, 0.06, "a crisis"); } }catch(e){}
    try{ const S = G.sc; if(S && S.polities && S.polities.some(p => p.status === "tension" || p.status === "seceded")) pull("fed", L.fed + (P.id === "COM" ? -14 : 14), 0.05, "secession talk"); }catch(e){}
    try{ const mood = (G.mood && G.mood.val) || 0; if(mood < -20) pull("econ", L.econ + (P.id === "FED" ? -10 : 10), 0.04, "hard times"); }catch(e){}
    if(P.id === "OWN" && G.you && G.you.pos){ pull("econ", G.you.pos.econ || 0, 0.04, "its founder"); pull("soc", G.you.pos.lib || 0, 0.04, "its founder"); }
    const ld = phLeader(P.id);
    if(ld && (!P.leaders.length || P.leaders.slice(-1)[0].id !== ld.id)){ P.leaders.push({id:ld.id, name:ld.name, d:G.day}); if(P.leaders.length > 1) phNote(P, ld.name + " now leads the " + partyName(P.id) + "."); }
    if(ld && ld.pos){ pull("econ", ld.pos.econ || 0, 0.01, null); pull("soc", ld.pos.lib || 0, 0.01, null); }
    /* factions: a wide party may split, rarely */
    if(M.length > 8 && P.id !== "IND"){ const xs = M.map(m => m.pos.econ || 0); const spread = Math.max(...xs) - Math.min(...xs);
      if(spread > 90 && chance(.004) && !P.splits.some(s => s.d > G.day - 60)){ const side = L.econ > 0 ? "provision" : "market"; P.splits.push({d:G.day, t:"A " + side + " faction walked out of the " + partyName(P.id) + " caucus."}); phNote(P, P.splits.slice(-1)[0].t); pull("econ", L.econ + (side === "provision" ? 15 : -15), 0.3, "the split"); } }
    /* landmark votes */
    try{ const A = (G.lastActs || []); if(A.length > P.last.acts){ A.slice(P.last.acts).forEach(a => { const ayes = (a.parties && a.parties[P.id]) || null; P.votes.push({d:a.day || G.day, t:a.title, how:ayes != null ? (ayes > 0 ? "aye" : "no") : (alignmentOf(P, a) >= 0 ? "aye" : "no")}); }); P.last.acts = A.length; if(P.votes.length > 40) P.votes.splice(0, P.votes.length - 40); } }catch(e){}
    if((G.day || 0) % 7 === 0 && (!P.snaps.length || P.snaps.slice(-1)[0].d !== G.day)){
      const prev = P.snaps.slice(-1)[0].lean, moved = PH_AX.filter(a => Math.abs(L[a[0]] - prev[a[0]]) >= 3);
      P.snaps.push({d:G.day, lean:Object.fromEntries(PH_AX.map(a => [a[0], Math.round(L[a[0]])])), why:[...new Set(why)].join(", ") || "drift"});
      if(P.snaps.length > 60) P.snaps.splice(1, 1);
      if(moved.length) phNote(P, "The " + partyName(P.id) + " has moved toward " + moved.map(a => L[a[0]] > prev[a[0]] ? a[2] : a[3]).join(" and ") + " since day " + P.snaps[P.snaps.length - 2].d + ".");
    }
  });
  try{ if((G.day || 0) % 3 === 0) phVox(); }catch(e){}
  phPublish();
}
function alignmentOf(P, a){ const p = a.pos || {}; return (p.econ || 0) * P.lean.econ + (p.lib || 0) * P.lean.soc + (p.def || 0) * P.lean.frn; }
function phNote(P, t){ P.notes = P.notes || []; P.notes.push({d:G.day, t}); if(P.notes.length > 40) P.notes.shift(); try{ feed(t, "info"); }catch(e){} }
function phDesc(id){ const P = phEnsure().p[id]; if(!P) return ""; return PH_AX.map(a => { const v = Math.round(P.lean[a[0]]); return a[1] + ": " + (Math.abs(v) < 12 ? "centre" : (v > 0 ? a[2] : a[3])) + " (" + v + ")"; }).join("; "); }
function onumPartyPrecedent(id){
  const P = phEnsure().p[id]; if(!P) return null;
  const v = P.votes.slice(-6), s = P.snaps;
  const out = [];
  if(v.length){ const x = pick(v); out.push("On day " + x.d + " the " + partyName(id) + " voted " + x.how + " on the " + x.t + "."); }
  if(s.length > 1){ const a = s[0], b = s.slice(-1)[0]; const k = pick(PH_AX); out.push("On " + k[1].toLowerCase() + " questions the party stood at " + a.lean[k[0]] + " on day " + a.d + "; it stands at " + b.lean[k[0]] + " now."); }
  if(P.splits.length) out.push("Remember day " + P.splits.slice(-1)[0].d + ": " + P.splits.slice(-1)[0].t);
  if(P.leaders.length > 1) out.push(P.leaders[P.leaders.length - 2].name + " led this party before " + P.leaders.slice(-1)[0].name + ".");
  return out.length ? pick(out) : null;
}
window.onumPartyPrecedent = onumPartyPrecedent;
function phVox(){
  try{ voxInit(); }catch(e){} if(!G.vox) return;
  const ids = Object.keys(phEnsure().p).filter(i => i !== "IND"); if(!ids.length) return;
  const id = pick(ids), line = onumPartyPrecedent(id); if(!line) return;
  const m = pick((G.members || []).filter(x => !x.gone && x.party !== G.you.party) || []); if(!m) return;
  voxAdd({id:G.vox.id++, by:m.id, t:"Precedent matters. " + line, k:"party", v:0, day:G.day, at:hhmm(), backs:0, hits:0, voices:[], mine:false});
}
function phPublish(){
  const H = phEnsure(), out = window.ONUM_PARTY_HISTORY = {};
  Object.values(H.p).forEach(P => { out[P.id] = {id:P.id, name:partyName(P.id), lean:Object.fromEntries(PH_AX.map(a => [a[0], Math.round(P.lean[a[0]])])), axes:PH_AX.map(a => ({k:a[0], name:a[1], plus:a[2], minus:a[3]})), snapshots:P.snaps, leaders:P.leaders, splits:P.splits, landmarkVotes:P.votes, notes:P.notes || []};
    try{ ONUM_GLOSS[partyName(P.id).toLowerCase()] = partyName(P.id) + ". " + phDesc(P.id) + "." + (P.leaders.length ? " Led by " + P.leaders.slice(-1)[0].name + "." : ""); }catch(e){} });
  try{ window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("onum-party-history", {detail:out})); }catch(e){}
}
function phHTML(){
  const H = phEnsure();
  return '<div class="pc__h">Party history</div>' + Object.values(H.p).map(P => {
    const bar = a => { const v = Math.round(P.lean[a[0]]); return '<div class="pc__m"><b>' + a[1] + '</b> · ' + (Math.abs(v) < 12 ? "centre" : v > 0 ? a[2] : a[3]) + ' (' + v + ')</div>'; };
    return '<div style="margin:10px 0;padding:8px;border:1px solid #445">' + '<div class="pc__h">' + pcEsc(partyName(P.id)) + '</div>' + PH_AX.map(bar).join("") +
      '<div class="pc__m"><b>Leaders:</b> ' + (P.leaders.map(l => pcEsc(l.name) + ' (day ' + l.d + ')').join(", ") || "none yet") + '</div>' +
      '<div class="pc__m"><b>Snapshots:</b> ' + P.snaps.slice(-8).map(s => 'day ' + s.d + ': econ ' + s.lean.econ + ', social ' + s.lean.soc + ', federal ' + s.lean.fed + ', faith ' + s.lean.faith + ', foreign ' + s.lean.frn + ' <i>(' + pcEsc(s.why) + ')</i>').join("<br>") + '</div>' +
      (P.splits.length ? '<div class="pc__m"><b>Splits:</b> ' + P.splits.map(s => 'day ' + s.d + ': ' + pcEsc(s.t)).join("<br>") + '</div>' : '') +
      '<div class="pc__m"><b>Landmark votes:</b> ' + (P.votes.slice(-8).map(v => 'day ' + v.d + ' ' + pcEsc(v.t) + ': ' + v.how).join("<br>") || "none yet") + '</div></div>';
  }).join("");
}
function phRecap(){ try{ const H = phEnsure(); const n = Object.values(H.p).flatMap(P => (P.notes || []).filter(x => x.d >= G.day - 1)); return n.length ? n.slice(-2).map(x => x.t).join(" ") : ""; }catch(e){ return ""; } }

/* ══ 20261013i · Phone button: calls, texts & DMs, contacts (officeholders, counterparts), Neptune chat ══ */
const PB = {tab:"contacts", call:null};
function pbEsc(s){ return String(s == null ? "" : s).replace(/[&<>"]/g, c => ({"&":"\u0026amp;","<":"\u0026lt;",">":"\u0026gt;",'"':"\u0026quot;"}[c])); }
function pbContacts(){
  const out = [], seen = {};
  const add = (id, name, role, grp) => { if(id == null || seen[id]) return; seen[id] = 1; out.push({id, name, role, grp}); };
  try{ (OH.list || []).forEach(p => add(p.id, p.name, p.role, "Officeholders")); }catch(e){}
  try{ (G.members || []).filter(m => m.office || m.lead || m.title).slice(0, 30).forEach(m => add(m.id, m.name, m.title || m.office || "Member", "Officeholders")); }catch(e){}
  try{ const S = G.sc; if(S) Object.keys(S.st).forEach(n => { const s = S.st[n]; if(s.govId != null) add(s.govId, s.gov, "Governor of " + n, "Officeholders"); }); }catch(e){}
  try{ WORLD_POWERS.forEach(p => realmFolk(p.k).filter(f => f.id.indexOf("as") !== 0).forEach(f => add("rl:" + p.k + ":" + f.id, f.name, f.title + " \u00b7 " + p.t, "Abroad"))); }catch(e){}
  try{ const C = window.ONUM_COUNTERPARTS || {}; Object.keys(C).forEach(k => { const c = C[k]; add(c.npcId, c.name, (c.relation || "counterpart") + " · " + k, "Counterparts"); }); }catch(e){}
  try{ (G.members || []).slice(0, 60).forEach(m => add(m.id, m.name, (m.party || "") + " " + (m.state || ""), "Members")); }catch(e){}
  return out;
}
function pbApps(){
  const k = (function(){ try{ return heldOffice(); }catch(e){ return null; } })();
  const L = [
    ["purse", "Your purse", "Your own money: salary, rent, what you spend and what people think of it. Not the war chest."],
    ["chest", "War chest", "Funds, the ledger, and what they can hire. " + (G.you ? money(G.you.funds || 0) : "")],
    ["office", "Powers of my office", "What the chair lets you do, and what it costs."],
    ["sched", "The schedule", "Counsel's diary: meetings, events and travel awaiting a yes or a no."],
    ["docket", "The docket", "What is before you today, with the full text."],
    ["gov", "The government", "Who runs the nation, and who stands behind them."],
    ["paper", "This morning's paper", "The Ledger."],
    ["war", "The situation room", "A neighbour holds the bread: what your office may do, what it must ask others for, and who answers."],
    ["cal", "The standing calendar", "Caucus, committees, the Senate, the Court and the Press Gallery: what meets when, whether or not you are there."],
    ["hq", "Campaign office", "Walk up the high street to your campaign office, ten minutes, if the street is in the way."],
    ["inq", "The Academy", "Every field the Republic is asking questions in, from number to quantum fields to what a mind is owed: what is settled, what is next, and what you can fund."],
    ["law", "The Law", "The one set of rules every part of this world answers to, and what it has mended."],
    ["cmt", "Committees", "Your committee seat, the Steering Committee's invitations, and the caucus ballots for seats and gavels."],
    ["eng", "The Engine", "The Republic's thinking machines, from the Treasury's adding engines to a general intelligence: what stage, how safe, and what you can do about it."],
    ["org", "The nation, from the ground up", "Towns, states and the nation, each doing its own job: who petitioned whom, who paid, and who passed what."],
    ["kin", "The families", "Who is behind you and behind them: spouses, children, parents, siblings, and what they do to a vote or a run."],
    ["watch", "Sit in the gallery", "Watch a chamber or a hearing from the gallery. No vote, no floor."],
    ["hear", "Sit for a hearing on a bill", "Take your seat at the committee table for the hearing on any bill, the one before you or one already passed or lost."],
    ["notebook", "Notebook", "What you wrote down."],
    ["rival", "Rivals", "Who wants your seat."],
    ["career", "Career and record", "Standing, eras, precedent."],
    ["union", "The Union map", "States and neighbouring republics."],
    ["settings", "Settings (pause)", "Speed, graphics, audio, controls."]
  ];
  return L.filter(a => (a[0] !== "docket" || (k && k !== "house")) && (a[0] !== "war" || warRoleCan()));
}
function watchCard(){
  const D = (window.NEPTUNE_SITTINGS && window.NEPTUNE_SITTINGS.defs) || {};
  const ks = Object.keys(D).filter(k => k !== "delegation" && ROOMS[k]);
  G.running = false;
  card.innerHTML = '<div class="eyebrow">The gallery</div><h1>Sit in and watch</h1><p class="dream">You may sit in the gallery of any chamber or hearing. You have no vote and no floor; the room moves on its own, and you learn who is persuaded by whom. It costs only the time.</p>' +
    ks.map(k => '<div class="whip__row"><span class="whip__n">' + pcEsc(D[k].title) + '</span><span class="whip__w">' + pcEsc((ROOMS[k] && ROOMS[k].t) || k) + ' \u00b7 ' + D[k].min + ' minutes</span><span class="whip__b"><button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost whip__go" data-watch="' + k + '">Take a seat</button></span></div>').join("") +
    '<div class="card__actions"><button type="button" class="btn" id="wcClose">Close</button></div>';
  veil.hidden = false;
  el("wcClose").addEventListener("click", () => walkNow());
  card.querySelectorAll("[data-watch]").forEach(b => b.addEventListener("click", () => { const k = b.getAttribute("data-watch"); veil.hidden = true; G._sitWatch = true;
    if(G.room !== k){ try{ goTo(k); }catch(e){} setTimeout(() => { try{ G._sitWatch = true; sitStart(k); }catch(e){ console.warn(e); } }, 500); } else sitStart(k); }));
}
/* sit for the hearing on a bill: the one before you, or any bill that has been decided one way or the other. You are at the table, not in the gallery */
function hearBills(){
  const out = [], y = G.you; if(G.bill && !G.bill.done) out.push({title:G.bill.title, line:G.bill.line, pos:G.bill.pos, now:true, status:"before the House"});
  ((y && y.record && y.record.votes) || []).slice(-14).reverse().forEach(v => out.push({title:v.title, line:describeBill({pos:v.pos || {econ:0, lib:0, def:0, wel:0}}), pos:v.pos, day:v.day, status:v.passed ? "passed, day " + v.day : "lost, day " + v.day, vote:v.vote}));
  try{ const S = (G.states || []).find(z => z.n === y.state); if(S && S.bill) out.push({title:S.bill.title, line:"Before the legislature of " + y.state + ".", pos:null, status:"before the state legislature"}); }catch(e){}
  return out;
}
function hearCard(){
  const L = hearBills(); G.running = false;
  card.innerHTML = '<div class="eyebrow">The committee table</div><h1>Sit for a hearing</h1><p class="dream">The committee will hear witnesses on any bill you name, whether it is still alive or long decided. You sit at the table, with a question you may or may not ask. It takes the afternoon.</p>' +
    (L.length ? L.map((b, i) => '<div class="whip__row"><span class="whip__n">' + pcEsc(b.title) + '</span><span class="whip__w">' + pcEsc(b.status) + (b.line ? ' · ' + pcEsc(String(b.line).slice(0, 90)) : '') + '</span><span class="whip__b"><button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost whip__go" data-hear="' + i + '">Take your seat</button></span></div>').join("") : '<p class="muted">No bill has come before you yet.</p>') +
    '<div class="card__actions"><button type="button" class="btn" id="hrClose">Close</button></div>';
  veil.hidden = false; el("hrClose").addEventListener("click", () => walkNow());
  card.querySelectorAll("[data-hear]").forEach(b => b.addEventListener("click", () => hearSit(L[+b.getAttribute("data-hear")])));
}
function hearSit(b){
  if(!b) return; const y = G.you, cm = COMMITTEES.find(c => c.k === y.committee) || COMMITTEES[0];
  const e = {k:"hearing", room:"committee", com:cm.k, hour:Math.floor((G.minute || 0) / 60), day:G.day, t:"Hearing on the " + b.title, d:"The committee takes evidence on the " + b.title + " (" + b.status + "). " + (b.line || "") , done:false};
  veil.hidden = true; walkNow();
  const go = () => { try{
    const pool = (cmtMembers(cm.k) || []).concat((G.members || []).filter(m => !m.gone)).filter((m, i, a) => a.indexOf(m) === i).slice(0, 7), r = ROOMS.committee || {w:8, d:10};
    pool.forEach((m, i) => { m.room = "committee"; m.x = (i - 3) * 1.1; m.z = Math.min(r.d - 2, 3 + (i % 2) * 1.2); m.tx = m.x; m.tz = m.z; });
    eventCard(e); }catch(err){ console.warn("hearing", err); } };
  if(G.room !== "committee"){ try{ goTo("committee"); }catch(err){} setTimeout(go, 900); } else go();
}
function pbAppGo(id){
  const go = {hq:() => sfGoHQ(), inq:() => inqCard(), law:() => lawCard(), cmt:() => cmtCard(), eng:() => engCard(), cal:() => stdCalCard(), org:() => orgBrowse(), kin:() => kinBrowse(), war:() => warRoleOpen(), purse:() => purseCard(), watch:() => watchCard(), hear:() => hearCard(), chest:() => chestCard(), office:() => officeCard(), sched:() => scheduleCard(), docket:() => dkCard(0), gov:() => govCard(), paper:() => paperCard(),
    notebook:() => notebookCard(), rival:() => rivalCard(),
    career:() => { try{ showPane("paneCareer"); document.body.classList.add("ui-open"); }catch(e){} },
    union:() => { try{ mapToggle(true); }catch(e){} },
    settings:() => { try{ openSettings(); }catch(e){} }}[id];
  if(go) setTimeout(() => { try{ go(); }catch(e){ console.warn("phone app", e); } }, 30);
}
function pbHTML(){
  const T = [["calls","Calls"],["texts","Texts & DMs"],["convo","Conversations"],["contacts","Contacts"],["apps","Apps"],["chat","Chat"]];
  let b = "";
  if(PB.tab === "convo"){ b = cvLogHTML(); }
  else if(PB.tab === "contacts"){
    const C = pbContacts(); let g = "";
    C.forEach(c => { if(c.grp !== g){ g = c.grp; b += '<div class="pb__h">' + pbEsc(g) + '</div>'; }
      b += '<div class="pb__row"><span><b>' + pbEsc(c.name) + '</b><br><small>' + pbEsc(c.role) + '</small></span><span><button data-pbcall="' + c.id + '">Call</button> <button data-pbdm="' + c.id + '">Text</button></span></div>'; });
    if(!C.length) b = '<p>No contacts yet.</p>';
  } else if(PB.tab === "apps"){
    b = '<div class="pb__h">The office, on the screen</div><p><small>What the pause menu used to hold, in one place.</small></p>' +
      pbApps().map(a => '<div class="pb__row"><span><b>' + pbEsc(a[1]) + '</b><br><small>' + pbEsc(a[2]) + '</small></span><span><button data-pbapp="' + a[0] + '">Open</button></span></div>').join("");
  } else if(PB.tab === "texts"){
    try{ const D = dmState(); const ids = Object.keys(D.th).sort((a, x) => ((D.th[x].slice(-1)[0] || {i:0}).i) - ((D.th[a].slice(-1)[0] || {i:0}).i));
      ids.forEach(id => { const L = D.th[id].slice(-1)[0] || {}; const nm = (function(){ try{ const m = G.members.find(m => m.id == id) || (G.people || []).find(p => p.id == id); return m ? m.name : id; }catch(e){ return id; } })();
        b += '<div class="pb__row"><span><b>' + pbEsc(nm) + '</b><br><small>' + pbEsc(String(L.t || "").slice(0, 90)) + '</small></span><span><button data-pbdm="' + id + '">Open</button></span></div>'; });
      if(!ids.length) b = '<p>No messages yet.</p>'; }catch(e){ b = '<p>No messages yet.</p>'; }
  } else if(PB.tab === "calls"){
    const c = PB.call;
    if(!c) b = '<p>No call in progress. Pick someone in Contacts.</p>' + (PB.log || []).slice(-8).reverse().map(l => '<div class="pb__row"><span>' + pbEsc(l) + '</span></div>').join("");
    else b = '<div class="pb__h">On the line: ' + pbEsc(c.name) + '</div><div class="pb__log">' + c.lines.map(l => '<p><b>' + pbEsc(l.w) + ':</b> ' + pbEsc(l.t) + '</p>').join("") + '</div><div class="pb__row"><input id="pbSay" placeholder="Say something" style="flex:1"><button data-pbsay="1">Say</button> <button data-pbhang="1">Hang up</button></div>';
  } else {
    const pill = document.getElementById("ltPill");
    b = pill ? '<p>Neptune\'s live chat is here.</p><button data-pbchat="1">Open chat</button>' : '<p>Live chat is not available on this build.</p>';
  }
  return '<div class="pb__top"><b>Phone</b><button data-pbx="1">Close</button></div><div class="pb__tabs">' + T.map(t => '<button data-pbt="' + t[0] + '"' + (PB.tab === t[0] ? ' class="is-on"' : '') + '>' + t[1] + '</button>').join("") + '</div><div class="pb__body">' + b + '</div>';
}
function pbRender(){ const w = document.getElementById("pbPhone"); if(w) w.innerHTML = pbHTML(); }
function pbOpen(){
  let w = document.getElementById("pbPhone");
  if(!w){
    const st = document.createElement("style"); st.textContent = '#pbPhone{position:fixed;right:max(8px,min(18px,2vw));bottom:64px;width:min(360px,calc(100vw - 16px));box-sizing:border-box;max-height:70vh;overflow:auto;background:#1c2128;color:#eef1f4;border:1px solid #3a424d;border-radius:14px;z-index:9999;font:13px system-ui,sans-serif;box-shadow:0 10px 30px rgba(0,0,0,.45)}#pbPhone .pb__top{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #3a424d}#pbPhone .pb__tabs{display:flex;gap:4px;padding:8px}#pbPhone .pb__tabs button{flex:1}#pbPhone button{background:#2b333d;color:#eef1f4;border:1px solid #4a5563;border-radius:6px;padding:4px 8px;cursor:pointer}#pbPhone button.is-on{background:#eef1f4;color:#1c2128}#pbPhone .pb__body{padding:6px 12px 12px}#pbPhone .pb__h{margin:10px 0 4px;font-weight:700;color:#c9a96e}#pbPhone .pb__row{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:8px;padding:6px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #2b333d}#pbPhone small{color:#aab3bd}#pbPhone input{background:#11151a;color:#eef1f4;border:1px solid #4a5563;border-radius:6px;padding:4px}';
    document.head.appendChild(st);
    w = document.createElement("div"); w.id = "pbPhone"; document.body.appendChild(w);
    w.addEventListener("click", ev => { const t = ev.target.closest("button"); if(!t) return; ev.stopPropagation(); const d = t.dataset;
      if(d.pbx){ w.remove(); return; }
      if(d.pbapp){ w.remove(); pbAppGo(d.pbapp); return; }
      if(d.pbt){ PB.tab = d.pbt; pbRender(); return; }
      if(d.pbdm){ w.remove(); try{ dmToggle(true, isNaN(+d.pbdm) ? d.pbdm : +d.pbdm); }catch(e){} return; }
      if(d.pbcall){ try{ const who = byId(isNaN(+d.pbcall) ? d.pbcall : +d.pbcall); if(who && who.traits && who !== G.you){ const st = timeStance(who, G.you, 10); if(st.mode !== "free"){ PB.call = {id:who.id, name:who.name, lines:[{w:who.name, t:(st.mode === "fee" ? "I cannot talk on the telephone. " + st.why + " Come and see me and we will see." : st.why + " Ring another time.")}]}; PB.tab = "calls"; pbRender(); return; } } }catch(e){}
        const c = pbContacts().find(x => String(x.id) === d.pbcall); PB.call = {id:c ? c.id : d.pbcall, name:c ? c.name : "Caller", lines:[{w:c ? c.name : "Caller", t:"Hello, this is " + (c ? c.name : "me") + "."}]}; PB.tab = "calls"; pbRender(); return; }
      if(d.pbhang){ PB.log = PB.log || []; if(PB.call) PB.log.push("Called " + PB.call.name + " · day " + G.day); PB.call = null; pbRender(); return; }
      if(d.pbsay){ const i = document.getElementById("pbSay"), txt = i && i.value.trim(), c = PB.call; if(!txt || !c) return; c.lines.push({w:"You", t:txt}); pbRender();
        if(String(c.id).indexOf("rl:") === 0){ const rv = realmVoice(String(c.id).split(":")[1], c, txt); setTimeout(() => { c.lines.push({w:c.name, t:rv}); if(PB.call === c) pbRender(); }, 500); return; }
        const LT = window.onumLiveTalk; let p;
        try{ p = LT && LT.reply ? LT.reply({npcId:c.id, channel:"phone", text:txt}) : null; }catch(e){ p = null; }
        Promise.resolve(p).then(r => { c.lines.push({w:c.name, t:(r && r.text) || (function(){ try{ const m = window.onumMind ? window.onumMind(c.id) : null; return m && m.goals ? "I hear you. Right now I care about " + [].concat(m.goals)[0] + "." : "I hear you. Let me think on it."; }catch(e){ return "I hear you."; } })()}); if(PB.call === c) pbRender(); }).catch(() => {});
        return; }
      if(d.pbchat){ const pill = document.getElementById("ltPill"); w.remove(); if(pill) pill.click(); return; }
    }, true);
  }
  pbRender();
}
setTimeout(() => { try{
  const inbox = document.getElementById("tabInbox"); if(!inbox || document.getElementById("tabPhone")) return;
  const b = document.createElement("button"); b.type = "button"; b.id = "tabPhone"; b.textContent = "Phone";
  b.addEventListener("click", ev => { ev.preventDefault(); ev.stopPropagation(); if(document.getElementById("pbPhone")) document.getElementById("pbPhone").remove(); else pbOpen(); }, true);
  inbox.insertAdjacentElement("afterend", b);
}catch(e){ console.warn("phone btn", e); } }, 1800);

/* ══ 20261013i · anyone with something to say comes and says it (non-blocking) ══ */
const AP = {last:0, toast:null};
function apAgenda(m){
  const out = [];
  try{ const it = (dkItems() || [])[0]; if(it) out.push({k:"vote", t:"Before the vote on the " + it.title + ": " + pick(["I need to know where you are.", "count me worried, and here is why.", "it costs more than they are saying."])}); }catch(e){}
  try{ const mind = window.onumMind ? window.onumMind(m.id) : null; if(mind && mind.goals){ const g = [].concat(mind.goals)[0]; if(g) out.push({k:"pitch", t:"I will be straight with you. What I am after: " + String(g) + ". Can you help?"}); }
    if(mind && mind.plans){ const p = [].concat(mind.plans)[0]; if(p) out.push({k:"heads", t:"Heads up, before it happens: " + String(p) + ". Better you hear it from me."}); } }catch(e){}
  try{ const S = G.sc; const st = m.state && S && S.st[m.state]; if(st && st.griev > 40) out.push({k:"warn", t:"A warning: " + m.state + " is angrier than the capital knows. Grievance is " + Math.round(st.griev) + "."}); }catch(e){}
  try{ const L = (G.feedLog || G.feed || []).slice(-1)[0]; if(L && (L.t || L.text)) out.push({k:"news", t:"Did you see? " + String(L.t || L.text).slice(0, 120)}); }catch(e){}
  try{ if(m.rel && m.rel.warmth < -20) out.push({k:"concern", t:"I have a concern, and it is about you. People notice how you treat them."}); }catch(e){}
  return out;
}
function apBudget(m){ const d = G.day; if(m._apD !== d){ m._apD = d; m._apN = 0; } return m._apN < 3; }
function apToast(m, a){
  try{ if(AP.toast) AP.toast.remove(); }catch(e){}
  const w = document.createElement("div"); w.id = "apToast";
  try{ w.dataset.npc = m.id; w.dataset.name = m.name; }catch(e){}
  w.style.cssText = "position:fixed;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);bottom:72px;max-width:520px;background:#1c2128;color:#eef1f4;border:1px solid #3a424d;border-radius:12px;padding:10px 14px;z-index:9998;font:13px system-ui,sans-serif;box-shadow:0 8px 24px rgba(0,0,0,.4)";
  w.innerHTML = '<b></b><div class="apT" style="margin:4px 0 8px"></div><button data-ap="talk">Talk</button> <button data-ap="away">Walk away</button>';
  w.querySelector("b").textContent = m.name + " stops you";
  w.querySelector(".apT").textContent = "\u201C" + a.t + "\u201D";
  w.querySelectorAll("button").forEach(b => { b.style.cssText = "background:#2b333d;color:#eef1f4;border:1px solid #4a5563;border-radius:6px;padding:4px 10px;cursor:pointer";
    b.onclick = () => { w.remove(); AP.toast = null; if(b.dataset.ap === "talk"){ try{ openTalk(m); }catch(e){} } else { m.say = pick(["Another time, then.", "Fine. I will find you later.", "Think about it."]); m.sayT = 2.5; try{ momentLog("You walked away from " + m.name + "."); }catch(e){} } }; });
  document.body.appendChild(w); AP.toast = w;
  setTimeout(() => { if(AP.toast === w){ w.remove(); AP.toast = null; } }, 14000);
}
function liveClean(t){ t = String(t == null ? "" : t).replace(/\[\[[\s\S]*?\]\]/g, "").replace(/\s+/g, " ").trim().replace(/^["“]|["”]$/g, ""); const m = t.match(/^(?:[^.!?]+[.!?]+\s*){1,2}/); if(m && t.length > 220) t = m[0].trim(); return t.slice(0, 260); }
function apSpeak(m, a, to){
  m.say = a.t; m.sayT = 5; m.sayTo = to ? to.id : "you"; m._apN = (m._apN | 0) + 1;
  const LT = window.onumLiveTalk;
  try{ if(LT && LT.reply) Promise.resolve(LT.reply({npcId:m.id, channel:"face", to:to || undefined, stage:"You stop " + (to ? to.name : "the player") + " in passing, unprompted, to raise this: " + a.t + " Say it in your own words, as yourself. Nobody has said anything to you yet.", text:a.t})).then(r => { if(r && r.live && r.text){ const lt = liveClean(r.text); if(!lt) return; m.say = lt; m.sayT = 6; const T = document.querySelector("#apToast .apT"); if(T && !to) T.textContent = "\u201C" + lt + "\u201D"; } }).catch(() => {}); }catch(e){}
}
function apTick(){
  if(!G || !G.running || (typeof TALK !== "undefined" && TALK.open)) return;
  if(G.t - AP.last < 25) return;
  const here = everyone().filter(m => m && !m.gone && !m.ohSeat && m.room === G.room && m.x != null && apBudget(m) && !m.wantsWord && m.kind !== "player");
  if(!here.length) return;
  const m = pick(here), A = apAgenda(m); if(!A.length) return;
  const a = pick(A); AP.last = G.t;
  const others = here.filter(o => o !== m);
  if(others.length && chance(.35)){
    const o = pick(others); m.tx = o.x + 0.7; m.tz = o.z + 0.4; o.faceUntil = G.t + 6;
    apSpeak(m, a, o); setTimeout(() => { try{ o.say = pick(["I had not heard that.", "Say that again, slowly.", "Who else knows?", "Not here."]); o.sayT = 3; o.sayTo = m.id; }catch(e){} }, 3200);
    try{ momentLog(m.name + " stopped " + o.name + " to talk."); }catch(e){}
    return;
  }
  const sy = Math.sin(cam.yaw || 0), cy = Math.cos(cam.yaw || 0), side = chance(.5) ? -1 : 1;
  m.tx = cam.x + sy * 1.4 + cy * 0.9 * side; m.tz = cam.z + cy * 1.4 - sy * 0.9 * side; m.faceUntil = G.t + 8;
  apSpeak(m, a, null); apToast(m, a);
  try{ momentLog(m.name + " came over to you: " + a.k + "."); }catch(e){}
}
setInterval(() => { try{ apTick(); }catch(e){} }, 4000);

/* ══ 20261013i · phone layout: sheets, scrollable bar, touch sizes, safe areas ══ */
(function(){ try{
  const css = `
@media (max-width: 900px), (max-height: 500px) and (orientation: landscape) {
  html, body { font-size: 15px; -webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%; }
  body * { min-font-size: 14px; }
  .dossier .tabs { position: fixed !important; left: 0 !important; right: 0 !important; bottom: 0 !important; top: auto !important; width: auto !important;
    display: flex !important; flex-wrap: nowrap !important; overflow-x: auto !important; overflow-y: hidden !important; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch; gap: 6px;
    padding: 6px max(8px, env(safe-area-inset-right)) calc(6px + env(safe-area-inset-bottom)) max(8px, env(safe-area-inset-left)) !important;
    background: #1c2128 !important; z-index: 9990 !important; scrollbar-width: none; }
  .dossier .tabs::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }
  .dossier .tabs > button, .dossier .tabs > a { flex: 0 0 auto !important; min-height: 44px !important; min-width: 64px; font-size: 15px !important; padding: 8px 12px !important; white-space: nowrap; }
  body.ui-open .dossier, #pcScreen, #pbPhone, #ltChat, #ltPanel, #card, .dmw {
    position: fixed !important; left: 0 !important; right: 0 !important; top: 0 !important; bottom: 0 !important; width: auto !important; max-width: none !important; height: auto !important; max-height: none !important;
    margin: 0 !important; border-radius: 0 !important; transform: none !important; overflow-y: auto !important; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
    padding-top: max(10px, env(safe-area-inset-top)) !important; padding-left: max(10px, env(safe-area-inset-left)) !important; padding-right: max(10px, env(safe-area-inset-right)) !important;
    padding-bottom: calc(70px + env(safe-area-inset-bottom)) !important; font-size: 15px !important; z-index: 9995 !important; box-sizing: border-box !important; }

  .scene { aspect-ratio: auto !important; height: calc(100dvh - 64px - env(safe-area-inset-bottom)) !important; min-height: 240px !important; }
  #uiToolbar { position: fixed !important; left: 0 !important; right: 0 !important; bottom: 0 !important; top: auto !important; display: flex !important; flex-wrap: nowrap !important;
    overflow-x: auto !important; gap: 6px; padding: 6px max(8px, env(safe-area-inset-right)) calc(6px + env(safe-area-inset-bottom)) max(8px, env(safe-area-inset-left)) !important;
    background: #1c2128 !important; z-index: 9990 !important; scrollbar-width: none; }
  #uiToolbar > * { flex: 0 0 auto !important; min-height: 44px !important; font-size: 15px !important; }
